\begin{abstract}
We study electrical and thermal transport in a one-dimensional Luttinger liquid coupled to a three-dimensional acoustic phonon in both the ``clean" (umklapp scattering) and ``dirty" (disorder scattering) limits using the memory matrix formalism.
By explicitly incorporating the Debye cutoff on phonon frequencies, we consider the low and high temperature transport regimes.
We find the crossover temperature separating these two regimes to be interaction-dependent, with repulsive interactions increasing the crossover temperature by at most an order of magnitude.
This provides an illustration for how interactions can enhance the low-temperature scattering regime in an interacting Fermi system.
\end{abstract}

