\section{Discussion}

The most interesting aspect of this investigation is the appearance of a strong peak in the d-wave condensate at moderate couplings, which subsides, as expected, at low density and half-filling.  Apart from the BCS-like form of the order parameter, this symmetry was not built in from the beginning.   We note, however, that the condensate disappears at large $U$.  This is certainly contrary to a picture in which the superconducting state is achieved by doping the Mott insulator at strong coupling.  On the other hand, some numerical investigations find no condensate at all in the absence of next-nearest neighbor interactions, at comparable values of $U/t$ \cite{qin2020}. 

We also emphasize that there are many local minima around a given Hartree-Fock state (i.e.\ an eigenstate of particle number), and there are many Hartree-Fock states.  But the energy of a local minimum in the neighborhood of a given Hartree-Fock state is always slightly greater than that of the Hartree-Fock state itself, although the difference is generally less than one percent.  Of course, a Hartree-Fock state is an eigenstate of particle number, while in the local minimum only the expectation value of particle number is fixed.   

It may be of interest to couple electrons, in our calculational scheme, to a dynamical electromagnetic field.  We leave this possibility for future investigation.   \bigskip                                                                                                                                            


