\section{Using the BayesianLasso package}

To illustrate the functionality of the \pkg{BayesianLasso} package, we provide examples of its key functions, including density evaluation, cumulative distribution, random sampling, and quantile calculations.

\subsection{Probability density function (PDF)}

The function \code{dlasso()} computes the probability density function of the Lasso distribution for given parameters. Example \ref{EXP:1} plots the density of a $\text{Lasso(2,1,3)}$ distribution, shown as a solid black line in Figure \ref{fig:density_comparison},

\begin{example}\label{EXP:1}
The density of the Lasso$(2,1,3)$ distribution.
\begin{lstlisting}[style=Rstyle]
library(BayesianLasso)
# Define parameters
a <- 2
b <- 1
c <- 3
x <- seq(-3, 3, length = 1000)
# Plot the density of Lasso(2,1,3)
plot(x, dlasso(x, a, b, c, logarithm = FALSE), type = 'l', 
     xlab = "x", ylab = "Density", 
     ylim = c(0, 2.25), yaxt = "n", bty = "n")
\end{lstlisting}
\end{example}

\subsection{Cumulative distribution function (CDF)}
The function \code{plasso()} calculates the cumulative distribution function of the Lasso distribution. In Example \ref{EXP:2}, we compute the CDF at $x = -1$ for a $\text{Lasso}(2,1,3)$ distribution:

\begin{example}\label{EXP:2}
The CDF at $x = -1$ for a $\text{Lasso}(2,1,3)$ distribution.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=Rstyle]
CDF_value <- plasso(-1, a, b, c)
print(CDF_value)
[1] 0.00176594

\end{lstlisting}
\end{example}




