	%	We establish conductance bounds for systematic-scan and random-scan Gibbs samplers when the target distribution satisfies a Poincaré or log-Sobolev inequality and has sufficiently regular conditional distributions. These bounds lead to mixing time guarantees that extend beyond the log-concave setting, offering new insights into the convergence behavior of Gibbs sampling in broader regimes. Our approach leverages novel three-set isoperimetric inequalities and a sequential coupling argument for the Gibbs kernel, providing a systematic framework for analyzing conductance. Additionally, we demonstrate that these bounds remain valid for log-Lipschitz and log-smooth target distributions, further extending their applicability to non-log-concave settings.

	We establish bounds on the conductance for the systematic-scan and random-scan Gibbs samplers when the target distribution satisfies a Poincar\'e or log-Sobolev inequality and possesses sufficiently regular conditional distributions.
	These bounds lead to mixing time guarantees that extend beyond the log-concave setting, offering new insights into the convergence behavior of Gibbs sampling in broader regimes.
	Moreover, we demonstrate that our results remain valid for log-Lipschitz and log-smooth target distributions. Our approach relies on novel three-set isoperimetric inequalities and a sequential coupling argument for the Gibbs sampler.


