	\subsection{Our contributions} 

	% In our main results (Theorem XXX below)
	In this paper, we establish conductance bounds for the systematic-scan and random-scan Gibbs samplers over a broad class of target distributions supported on $\R^d$.
	%particularly those satisfying a functional inequality such as a Poincaré or log-Sobolev inequality and possessing sufficiently regular conditional distributions.
	In particular, consider a target distribution $\pi$ satisfying a $L^q$-Poincar\'e inequality with constant $\cpo{q}$ and whose one-dimensional conditional distributions are H\"older continuous in total variation distance with constant $M$ and exponent $\beta$ (see Subsection \ref{subsec:assumptions} for formal definitions). 
	Denote by $P_\mathrm{SS}$ and $P_\mathrm{RS}$ the Markov kernels corresponding to the systematic-scan and random-scan Gibbs samplers, respectively.
	%Denote $P_\mathrm{SS}$ (and $P_\mathrm{RS}$) the Markov kernel for the systematic-scan Gibbs sampler (and the random-scan respectively).
	Then, for sufficiently large $d$, our main results (Theorem \ref{thm:condss} and Corollary \ref{cor:condrs}) provide the following bounds on the conductance of the systematic-scan Gibbs kernel, $\Phi(P_\mathrm{SS})$, and the random-scan Gibbs kernel, $\Phi(P_\mathrm{RS})$:
	\begin{gather*}
		\Phi (P_\mathrm{SS}) \gtrsim \frac{\cpo{q}}{\left(Md\right)^{q/\beta}}, \\
		\Phi (P_{\mathrm{RS}} ) \gtrsim \frac{\cpo{q}}{M^{q/\beta} (d\cdot \log d)^{q/\beta + 1}},
	\end{gather*}
	where $\gtrsim$ denotes inequalities that hold up to a universal constant. Bounds on the mixing time in total variation distance then follow  directly from \citet[Corollary 1.5]{lovasz1993random}.


