\subsection{Theoretical : link with the Hawkes cumulants}
With the previous methodology, we are able to calculate the \textit{nth}-order moment of any $(\bZeta,\bM,\bP)-$MSPD. In addition, we know that the Hawkes process $\boldsymbol{H}$ can be represented as a $(\bZeta,\bM,\bP)-$MSPD. Hence, one can be interested in trying to derive a general formula for the \textit{nth}-order moment of the process. However, when we started calculating the 3th moment we remarked that this methodology was more linked with the cumulants of the process. 
To briefly illustrate this idea, we recall that the first three cumulants of random variable $X$ are given by,
\begin{align*}
	\kappa_1(X)&:=\E[X] \\
	\kappa_2(X)&:=\E[X^2]-(\E[X])^2 \\
	&= \kappa_1[X^2] - \kappa_1(X)^2\\
	\kappa_3(X)&:=\E[(X-\E[X])^3] \\
	& =  \kappa_1[X^3] - 3 \kappa_1(X) \kappa_2(X) - \kappa_1(X)^3,\\
\end{align*}
and consider a unidimentional Hawkes process $H$ defined by the following SDE, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Hawkes_imbed}
	\left\lbrace
	\begin{array}{l}
		H_T = \int_{(0,t]\times\R_+} \ind{\theta \leq \lambda_t} N(dt,d\theta) \\\hspace{10cm} t\geq 0 \\
		\lambda_T:=\mu + \int_{(0,t)\times\R_+}\Phi(T-t) \ind{\theta \leq \lambda_t}  N(dt,d\theta).\\
	\end{array}
	\right.    
\end{equation}
where $\mu >0$ and $\Phi : \R_+ \to \R_+$ such that $||\Phi||_1<1$. 
Then using the previous methodology we have that, 

\begin{align*}
	\E[H_T^2] &= \sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\frac{1}{n!}\E[\boldsymbol{\mathcal{I}}_n(\T_n H_T)H_T]\\
	&\b{\text{By Mecke equation}}\\
	&=\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\int_{\Delta_T^n}\int_{\R_+}\T^n_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)}H_T\E\left[ H_T\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)}^{+,n}\right]\prod_{i=1}^n d\theta_i dt_i \\
	&\b{\text{By proposition \ref{prop:expectation}}}\\
	&=\kappa_1(H_T)^2\\
	&+\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\int_{(\Delta^n_T \times \R_+)^n} \left[\mu \prod_{m=2}^{n} \Phi(t_m-t_{m-1})\right] \sum_{j=1}^n\left(1 + \int_0^T\Psi(v-t_j)dv\right)dt_1\dots dt_n. \\
\end{align*}
Thus we have that, 
\begin{align*}
	\kappa_2(H_T) &=\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\int_{(\Delta^n_T \times \R_+)^n} \left[\mu \prod_{m=2}^{n} \Phi(t_m-t_{m-1})\right] \sum_{j=1}^n\left(1 + \int_0^T\Psi(v-t_j)dv\right)dt_1\dots dt_n.
\end{align*}
We can show that the quantity above can be easily represented by the first cumulant of the intensity of the process. Furthermore, 
when calculating $\E[H_T^3]$ with this methodology we can show that, 
\begin{align*}
	\E[H_T^3] &= \sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\frac{1}{n!}\E[\boldsymbol{\mathcal{I}}_n(\T_n H_T)H_T^2]\\
	&\b{\text{By Mecke equation}}\\
	&=\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\int_{\Delta_T^n}\int_{\R_+}\T^n_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)}H_T\E\left[ H_T^2\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)}^{+,n}\right]\prod_{i=1}^n d\theta_i dt_i \\
	&=\kappa_1(H_T)^3  + 3\kappa_1(H_T)\kappa_2(H_T) + C_T
\end{align*}
where $C_T$ represents additional terms that can also be expressed using the first cumulant of the intensity of the process. For the sake of clarity and length, we won't explain the simplification of these terms in this paper, since they require some intermediate calculations.

These methodology would certainly make it possible to deduce a closed formula for the cumulants of a Hawkes process, but beyond the third cumulant, the structure of the calculations becomes very combinatorial.




\subsection{Practical : Stop Loss contracts, tail value at risk, decorrelation speed, simulations}
In this section, we'll present some of the practical openings that will feed into the second part of this article. 

As presented in \cite{bessy2021multivariate}, it turns out that some cyber events follow a dynamic in which they occur in clusters, prompting the authors to model the frequency of these events using a multivariate Hawkes. This idea was extended in \cite{boumezoued2023cyber} where the authors add to the self-excitation feature an exogenous process related to vulnerability disclosures. \gr{Vous avez des ref pour la finance ? } With these motivations, we could consider a loss process inspired of the one in definition (\ref{definition:MHP}), which would be of the following form, 

\begin{definition}\label{def:Ldependencemultivar}
Let $\bM\in(\R_+^*)^d$ and $\bP$ a self-excitation \textit{d-}kernel. Moreover, consider $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{L}}$ a \textit{d-}kernel such that for all $(i,k)\in [\![1,d]\!]^2$, $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{L}}^{i,j}(.,y) = m^{i,k}(y)\ind{i=k}$. Then process $\boldsymbol{L}$ solving the following SDE,
	\begin{equation}
		\left\lbrace
		\begin{array}{l}
			\boldsymbol{L}^{i}_T:= \int_{\bX_T} \hspace{0.15cm}m^{i,k}(y) \ind{\theta \leq\bLamb_t^k} \ind{i=k}\bN(dk,dt,d\theta,dy)\hspace{2cm} t\geq 0 \\
			\bLamb_T^i:= \bM^i + \int_{\bX_T} \bP^{i,k}(T-t,y_i)\ind{\theta \leq\bLamb_t^k}  \bN(dk,dt,d\theta,dy),
		\end{array}
		\right. 
	\end{equation}
	is a Multivariate Compound Hawkes Process with Dependencies. Thus, this process is a $(\boldsymbol{\mathcal{L}},\bM,\bP)-$MSPD.

\end{definition}

\gr{peut être a changer la forumulation car très copier coller de votre papier}\\
Moreover, as presented in \cite{Hillairet_Reveillac_Rosenbaum}, in a Stop Loss policy, the reinsurance company pays the loss amount above a threshold $\underline{K}$, up to a given amount ($\overline{K}-\underline{K}$). The payoff of such policy is given by, 
$$
		\left\lbrace
\begin{array}{l}
	0 \hspace{2.15cm}\text{if}\; L_T \leq \underline{K},\\
	K_T - \underline{K} \hspace{1cm}\text{if}\; \underline{K}\leq L_T \leq \overline{K}, \\
	\overline{K} - \underline{K} \hspace{1.2cm}\text{if}\; L_T \geq \overline{K},
\end{array}
\right. 
$$
where, $(L_t)$ is the loss process that activates the policy and the process $(K_t)$ is the effective loss that will be reimbursed.
Thus, the premium of that policy is given by the expectation which is, 
$$
\E[K_T \ind{L_T>\underline{K}}] - \underline{K}\P[L_T \in[\underline{K},\overline{K}]] + (\overline{K}-\underline{K})\P(L_T \geq \overline{K}).
$$
Hence in order to price a Stop Loss policy, one has to be able to compute $\E[K_T \ind{L_T>\underline{K}}]$. In this way, future work will seek to calculate this quantity with the $L$ following definition (\ref{def:Ldependencemultivar}), by exploiting the results provided in this paper. Moreover, by particularizing the self-excitation kernel, we'll be able to simulate certain quantities such as this expectation. In addition, we will investigate calculations related to risk measures such as the tail value at risk.  \gr{On rajoute un mote sur la vitesse de décorrélation et des probas de ruine ?}



\begin{remark}[What about the \textit{n-th} moment ?]
	This methodology of mixing the pseudo-chaotic expansion with Mecke equation has proved effective in calculating the first two moments of any $(\bZeta,\bM,\bP)-$MSPD. But in fact, it allows one to go further since, 
	\begin{align*}
		\E[\myZ{T}\boldsymbol{Z}^{i^2}_{T}] &=\lim_{M\to \infty}\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\frac{1}{n!}\E[\boldsymbol{\mathcal{I}}_n(\T_n \myZ{T}\ind{([0,T]\times[0,M])^n)}\boldsymbol{Z}^{i^2}_{T}]\\
		&=\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\sum_{k_1=1}^d \dots \sum_{k_n=1}^d \int_{\Delta_T^n}\int_{\R_+^2}\T^n_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)} \myZ{T}\E\left[ \boldsymbol{Z}^{i^2}_{T}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)}^{+,n}\right]\prod_{i=1}^n\bnu^{k_i}(dy_i)d\theta_i dt_i 
	\end{align*}
	and, 
	\begin{align*}
		\E\left[ \boldsymbol{Z}^{i^2}_{T}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_p)}^{+,p}\right] &= \sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\frac{1}{n!}\E[\boldsymbol{\mathcal{I}}_n(\T_n \boldsymbol{Z}^{i}_{T}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_p)}^{+,p})\boldsymbol{Z}^{i^2}_{T}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_p)}^{+,p}]\\
		&\hspace{-3cm}=\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}\sum_{k_1=1}^d \dots \sum_{k_n=1}^d \int_{\Delta_T^n}\int_{\R_+^2}\T^n_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)} \boldsymbol{Z}^{i}_{T}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_p)}^{+,p}\E\left[ \boldsymbol{Z}^{i}_{T}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_p)}^{+,p}\circ\varepsilon_{(\bx_1,\dots,\bx_n)}^{+,n}\right]\prod_{i=1}^n\bnu^{k_i}(dy_i)d\theta_i dt_i.
	\end{align*}
	In the previous equations, each quantity can be calculated with the tools we provide in this paper.
\end{remark}

