\begin{abstract}
The shadow of an abstract simplicial complex $\K$ with vertices in $\R^N$ is a subset of $\R^N$ defined as the union of the convex hulls of simplices of $\K$.
The Vietoris--Rips complex of a metric space $(\S,d)$ at scale $\beta$ is an abstract simplicial complex whose each $k$-simplex corresponds to $(k+1)$ points of $\S$ within diameter $\beta$.
In case $\S\subset\R^2$ and $d(a,b)=\|a-b\|$ standard Euclidean, the natural shadow projection of the Vietoris--Rips is already proved by Chambers et al. to be $1$-connected.
We extend the result beyond the standard Euclidean distance on $\S\subset\R^N$ to a family of path-based metrics $d^\eps_\S$.
From the pairwise Euclidean distances of points of $\S$, we introduce a family (parametrized by $\varepsilon$) of path-based Vietoris--Rips complexes $\Ri^\varepsilon_\beta(\S)$ for a scale $\beta>0$. 
If $\S\subset\R^2$ is Hausdorff-close to a planar Euclidean graph $\G$, we provide quantitative bounds on scales $\beta,\eps$ for the shadow projection map of the Vietoris--Rips of $(\S,d^\eps_\S)$ at scale $\beta$ to be $1$-connected.
As a novel application, this paper first studies the homotopy-type recovery of $\G\subset\R^N$ using the abstract Vietoris--Rips complex of a Hausdorff-close sample $\S$ under the $d^\eps_\S$ metric. 
Then, our result on the $1$-connectivity of the shadow projection lends itself to providing also a geometrically close embedding for the reconstruction.
Based on the length of a shortest loop and large-scale distortion of the embedding of $\G$, we quantify the choice of a suitable sample density $\varepsilon$ and a scale $\beta$ at which the shadow of $\mathcal{R}^\varepsilon_\beta(\S)$ is homotopy-equivalent and Hausdorff-close to $\G$. 
\end{abstract}


