\subsection{$\pi_1$-Surjection in $\R^2$}
We now consider the question of $1$-connectivity of the shadow projection $p$.
We provide a sufficient condition for the abstract simplicial complex $\K$ so that $p$ induces an epimorphism on the fundamental group.

Let $\K$ be a simplicial complex with vertices in $\R^2$.
Any path $\gamma$ in the geometric realization of its $1$-skeleton $|\K^{(1)}|$ can be described by a sequence of oriented edges in $\K$, and its projection $p(\gamma)$ must also form a path in the $1$-skeleton of the shadow $\sh(\K)$.
However, the converse is not generally true, i.e., every shadow path is not necessarily the projection of a Vietoris--Rips path. 
Nonetheless, every shadow path can be \emph{homotopically lifted} to $\K$ under the following lifting condition.
\begin{definition}[Lifting Condition]\label{def:lifting-cond}
We say that $\K$ has the \emph{lifting condition} if whenever the images $AB$ and $CD$ of two simplices $[AB]$ and $[CD]$ of $\K$ intersect, then there exist vertices $E,F\in\K^{(0)}$ such that either
\begin{enumerate}[(A)]
\item $[ABE]$ and $[CDE]$ are simplices of $\K$, or 
\item $[AEF]$ and $[CDEF]$ are simplices of $\K$ with $EF$ intersecting $AB$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
The following theorem guarantees that $p$ induces a surjection on the fundamental group.
\begin{theorem}[Surjectivity of Shadow Projection]\label{thm:shadow-surjection}
Let $\K$ satisfy the lifting condition. 
Then, the projection map induces a surjection on $\pi_1(\K)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every oriented shadow path $\gamma$ in $\sh(\K)$ can be associated with a sequence of oriented edges in $\K$. Note that these edges from $\K$ may not necessarily form a path, but projections of consecutive edges must intersect at a shadow vertex.
If none of the intersections are transverse, there is nothing to prove.

