\section{The Experiment and Challenge}

\subsection{Experiment 1: TRIBE  Traditional ABM (benchmark)}
We begin with an experiment to benchmark our traditional ABM version, TRIBE, across 200 simulations of an artificial trading society. Our unique contribution with this test is the enhancement of client agency through areas of active modeling.  We rely on the parameter sets in Table \ref{tab:simulation_params} for all our tests. Much of this data is derived from various stylized facts in literature (\cite{Pinter2023} and \cite{vidler2024decodingotcgovernmentbond} in particular) and also data published by the Australian and UK governments (\cite{RBA}, \cite{apra2024superannuation} and \cite{BOE} respectively).  This benchmark essentially looks at a simple coin toss of yes/no to trading desire, having been sampled from a Bernoulli distribution. The direction of trading, should an agent desire to trade, is then also determined by sampling from a Bernoulli distribution.  In this week, we decompose direction of trade from intention to trade.  Later on, we utilize an LLM to enhance the intention component of this model. 

