(iii) Toward finding scaling factor for the charge-changing cross sections of C, N, O, and Ca elements on a $^{12}$\rm C target at $\sim$200-300 MeV/nucleon \cite{17,25,26}: In this part, we consider the analysis of the experimental data for CCCSs of (a)  $^{12-16}$\rm C, $^{14,15,17}$\rm N, and $^{16}$\rm O isotopes at $\sim$200 MeV/nucleon \cite{26}, (b) $^{11-15}$\rm C, $^{13-17}$\rm N, and $^{15,17,18}$\rm O at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon \cite{25}, and (c) $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon \cite{17}. Following the procedure, outlined above, we consider $^{12}$\rm C and $^{15}$\rm N at $\sim$200 and 300 MeV/nucleon, $^{16}$\rm O at $\sim$200 MeV/nucleon, $^{18}$\rm O at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon, and $^{42}$\rm Ca as the 'test' nuclei. Here, it may be noted that we have chosen $^{18}$\rm O as a 'test' nucleus at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon, because it is the only isotope of oxygen that has experimental value for CCCS at this energy; in the case of nitrogen, the smaller error in the experimental CCCS for $^{15}$\rm N favors to choose it as a 'test' isotope instead of the $^{14}$\rm N one.  Next, taking $\epsilon_{avg}(E)$ from Fig. \ref{fig1}, Eq. (\ref{eq23}) is employed to find the values of $\Delta\epsilon$ that reproduces the CCCSs for the 'test' nuclei on a $^{12}$\rm C target at the respective energies. These values of $\Delta\epsilon$ are then used to predict the CCCSs for the remaining isotopes. The scaling factor for the considered isotopes at $\sim$200-300 MeV/nucleon is given in Table \ref{tab4}, and the corresponding (predicted) CCCSs are also presented in Table \ref{tab4}. Here, we notice that, like the $^{28}$\rm Si +  $^{12}$\rm C case (\ref{fig1}), the energy dependence of the scaling factor at relatively lower energies is also reflected in the analysis of the CCCSs for C, N, and O isotopes at $\sim$200 and 300 MeV/nucleon; for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes, the experimental CCCSs are available only at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon, noting could be said about the energy dependence of the scaling factor for these isotopes unless we have more experiments on Ca isotopes involving different incident energies. Regarding the predicted values of the CCCSs (\ref{tab4}), it is found that, except for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes, we get satisfactory account of the data for other isotopes. The CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes show large deviations between theory and experiment, indicating the failure of the scaling factor approach, thus suggesting to explore some other method to account for the anomaly in $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes. Thus, the problem of $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes needs some special attention and will be discussed again in the next part of the calculation. However, before proceeding, we conclude that the study of the scaling factor for $^{28}$\rm Si + $^{12}$\rm C charge-changing cross sections at 90-1296 MeV/nucleon could be taken as a starting point to develop a method that can be used to find the systematic energy- dependent scaling factor for other elements and one is able to provide satisfactory account of the CCCS data for Be, B, C, N, O, and F isotopes on a $^{12}$\rm C target at 200-930 MeV/nucleon. Here, it ia important to add that the experimental charge radii of light (stable) nuclei are known, the of consideration of a stable isotope as a 'test' isotope seems to provide a better approach in finding the scaling factor that can be used to predict relaible estimates for the charge radii of unstable (exotic) isotopes of diffrent elements by studying their CCCSs.

(iv) Charge-changing cross sections for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes on a $^{12}$\rm C target at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon \cite{17}: As discussed above, the analysis of CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca + $^{12}$\rm C at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon has shown that, unlike other elements, the use of the scaling factor approach fails in accounting for the experimental data. In other words, it might happen that the scaling factor approach is not suitable to properly take into account the effect of the presence of projectile neutrons to the CCCSs for Ca isotopes. The similar situation was also observed by Tanaka \textit{et al.} \cite{17} and it was shown \cite{17} that, instead of the scaling factor approach, the introduction of the charged-particle evaporation effect induced by the neutron-removal reaction is able to provide a better way to account for the contribution due to projectile neutrons to CCCS and one could have satisfactory explanation of the experimental data. In this connection, it is important to note that the evaluation of CCCS in the said approach \cite{17} involves both the proton as well as neutron distribution of the projectile; we feel that such an approach goes against the very spirit of CCCS measurements, for it was believed that the study of CCCS may be a tool to provide explicitly the information about the proton distribution (radius). Thus, there is still a need to study CCCS involving only the projectile proton distribution and evolve some other mechanism that can account for the effects due the presence of projectile neutrons. To expedite this task, we refer an earlier work on $^{3}$\rm He-nucleus total reaction cross-section ($\sigma_{R}$) \cite{34} in which it was demonstrated that the phase of the NN amplitude plays an important role in accounting for the $\sigma_{R}$ data, and its value depends upon both the incident energy as well as projectile-target system. Keeping this in mind, we expect that, in the present context, the phase of the NN amplitude may simulate the effect of the presence of projectile neutrons in CCCS calculation. So, our aim is to find the value of the phase of the NN amplitude for a given projectile-target system and assess how far it accounts for the CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes on a $^{12}$\rm C target at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon, involving the proton radii obtained using the deformed relativistic Hartree-Bogoliubov theory in continuum (DRHBc) \cite{31}.

Following Franco and Yin \cite{35}, the phase of the NN amplitude can be taken into account by multiplying Eq. (\ref{eq18}) by the phase factor $e^{-i\gamma_{NN}q^{2}/2}$ and treating the phase $\gamma_{NN}$ as a free parameter; we shall assume $\gamma_{pp}$ = $\gamma_{pn}$, thus involving only one parameter for a given projectile-target system. As mentioned above, our interest is to predict CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca + $^{12}$\rm C at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon using an already available information on the proton radii of 
$^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes, involving the effect of the presence of projectile neutrons through phase of the NN amplitude. To make the above calculations parameter free, we proceed as follows. First of all, we obtain SDHO proton distributions for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes that reproduce their experimental charge radii \cite{36,37,38}. Using these proton distributions, we now allow free variation of the phase of the NN amplitude $\gamma_{NN}$ to fit the CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca on a $^{12}$\rm C target at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon. The values of $\gamma_{NN}$, so obtained (shown in Fig. \ref{fig7}), are then used to predict CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca + $^{12}$\rm C at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon, employing the SDHO proton density that now reproduces the charge radii of $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes as calculated using the DRHBc \cite{31}. The results of such calculations, presented in Fig. \ref{fig7}, are found to provide quite a satisfactory explanation of the experimental data in all the cases. Thus, our calculations suggest that inclusion of the phase of the NN amplitude may provide an alternative to the scaling factor approach in taking into account the effect of the presence of projectile neutrons in the study of CCCSs for Ca isotopes. Moreover, it may be added that the phase of the NN amplitude could also be helpful to establish the credibility of proton radii of Ca isotopes used in the analysis of CCCS. Finally, we consider it worthwhile to comment on the trend of the phase of the NN amplitude obtained in the analysis of CCCSs for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca + $^{12}$\rm C at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon. As noticed in Fig. \ref{fig7}, the value of the phase is maximum for $^{42}$\rm Ca and it goes on decreasing, with increase in the neutron number, till we reach at $^{48}$\rm Ca, beyond which it further increases. This behavior of $\gamma_{NN}$ is similar to that observed in experimental charge radii of $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes \cite{36,37,38}. Like the charge radius, the minimum value of the phase $\gamma_{NN}$ for $^{48}$\rm Ca may be due to its doubly magic nature. This suggests that the magnitude of the effect of projectile neutrons in CCCS calculation for $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes on a $^{12}$\rm C target may be connected with the absolute value of the phase of the NN amplitude; in the present situation, since the values of $\gamma_{NN}$ (Fig. \ref{fig7}) and the experimental charge radii of $^{42-51}$\rm Ca isotopes follow similar trend, one may also consider the relation between the effect of the projectile neutrons in CCCS calculation, phase of the NN amplitude, and charge radius on equal footings. Keeping this in mind, the larger value of $\gamma_{NN}$, beyond $^{48}$\rm Ca, may support the swelling of the $^{48}$\rm Ca core in Ca isotopes beyond N = 28 \cite{17}.      

\section{Summary and Conclusions}
\label{sec4}

In this work, we have presented a theoretical study of the charge-changing cross sections (CCCSs) for Be, B, C, N, O, F, Si, and Ca isotopes on a $^{12}$\rm C target in the Glauber model, involving a phenomenological scaling factor to accommodate the effect of the presence of projectile neutrons in the calculation of CCCS. The calculations have been performed in three steps: (i) In the first step, we made detailed study of the $^{28}$\rm Si + $^{12}$\rm C charge-changing cross sections at 90-1296 MeV/nucleon and obtained the energy dependence of the scaling factor in the said energy range. (ii) Based on these results, we, in the second step, proposed a method that can be used to find the scaling factor for the charge-changing cross sections of other elements. The calculations included the study of CCCSs for the isotopes of Be, B, C, N, O, F, and Ca at 200-930 MeV/nucleon. It is found that our method to find the scaling facor for the isotopes of Be, B, C, N, O, F, and Ca works well to account for the CCCSs in all the cases, except for Ca isotopes where the calculated values differ significantly from the measured values. (iii) Finally, we have reexamined the anomaly observed in the case of Ca isotopes and tried to find an alternative to the scaling factor approach so that one can also accommodate the CCCSs for Ca isotopes on a $^{12}$\rm C target at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon. In this connection, it is found that inclusion of the phase variation of the NN amplitude, which is the basic input of the Glauber model, is able to provide an alternative way to serve the purpose of (phenomenological) scaling, and one gets quite a satisfactory account of the Ca data.

In conclusion, we found that the study of  $^{28}$\rm Si + $^{12}$\rm C charge-changing cross sections at 90-1296 MeV/nucleon provides a basis 
to find the energy-dependent scaling factor for studying the CCCSs of other elements. The extracted scaling factor is found to provide quite
a satisfactory account of the CCCS data for Be, B, C, N, O, and F isotopes at 200-930 MeV/nucleon. Unfortunately, we noticed that the similar procedure for obtaining the scaling factor does not work for the CCCSs of Ca isotopes at $\sim$300 MeV/nucleon, and, at present, we do not have any plausible explanation for this anomaly, except to find some other way that can accommodate the CCCS data for Ca isotopes. In this connection, we found that the consideration of the phase of the NN amplitude seems to be an alternative to the scaling factor approach in providing a satisfactory explanation of the CCCS data for Ca isotopes. Overall, the present work seems promising in providing the energy-dependent scaling factor, needed in the study of nucleus-nucleus CCCS, involving only the projectile protons. Still we need more data on CCCS for other elements and also at a wider range of incident energies, in order to assess the usefulness of the scaling factor approach as adopted in this work.  
