\noindent
The function $N_6(t)$ corresponds to the exponential generating function of tables containing only unknown columns. By inclusion/exclusion, $N_6(t)$ equals $F_6(t)$ assuming $m_1=m_3=m_5=0$ and $m_2=1,m_4=3,m_6=15$. Equivalently, $N_6(t)$ is the sixth moment of the determinant of a matrix whose entries are i.i.d. with the standard normal distribution. By the known result for the even moments of the determinant of such a matrix (Pr\'{e}kopa \cite{prekopa1967random}), we have that %However, those moments of entries exactly match that of the standard normal distribution. By the known result for even determinant moments with normal entries (Pr\'{e}kopa \cite{prekopa1967random}), we have
\begin{equation*}
    F_6(t)|_{X_{ij} \,\sim\, \mathsf{N}(0,1)} = N_6(t) = \frac{1}{48}\sum_{n=0}^\infty (n+1)(n+2)(n+4)! \,t^n.
\end{equation*}

