Upon seeing the example above, we discover the following structure -- any graph $g$ must be composed out of
\begin{itemize}
    \item disjoint cycles of length at least two
    \item a (single) tree whose root is $\nu$
\end{itemize}
Disjoint cycles of $g$ directly correspond to disjoint sub-tables found in $\tau$. This correspondence is not a bijection. Luckily, for a given cycle, there are exactly $\binom{6}{2} = 15$ ways how a sub-table corresponding to a given cycle can look like based on the location of the remaining pair in the known $4$-columns. These ways can be depicted using the following diagram in Figure \ref{fig:4knownpositions}.
