We may write the following structural equation
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\mathcal{F}}_6^* =
    \textsc{Set}\left(15\,\textsc{Cyc}_{\geq 2}\left(\,
    \fourcolab
        \,\right)\right)
    \star \mathcal{R}_6,
\end{equation}
where we denoted $\mathcal{R}_6$ as the set of all $\tilde{\mathcal{F}}^*_6$ tables whose graph contains no cycles of known $4$-columns. In terms of generating functions, we must have
\begin{equation}\label{Eq:R6struct}
    \tilde{F}^*_6(t) = \exp(-15(m_4-3)t-15\ln(1-(m_4-3)t)) R_6(t).
\end{equation}
Although the known $4$-columns in some $\tau \in \mathcal{R}_6$ do not form sub-tables since they are not disjoint from the remaining unknown columns, they are still very tightly associated with them. We can consider the following operation of \textbf{collapse}
\begin{itemize}
    \item each chain of $4$-columns attached to $\nu$ in $g$ corresponds to a subset of $4$-columns in $\tau$.
    \item These columns contain an unmatched pair of $a$'s not equal to any number tagging the chain.
    \item By deleting those columns and inserting the unmatched pairs into the known columns, we get a collapsed table $\tau'$ on unknowns columns only.
\end{itemize}
An example how a table is collapsed is shown in Figure \ref{fig:graphF6N6}.
