\noindent
In order to create (any) $\tau$ from $\tau'$, notice that we can mount a chain of $4$-columns to a pair in the collapsed $\tau'$. The chains stem out of $\tau'$ almost like branches out of a trunk. A chain (with length including zero) of known $4$-columns can be described as a structure (we always have positive signs)
\begin{equation}
    \textsc{Seq}\left(\,
    \fourcolab
        \,\right)
\end{equation}
with the corresponding EGF equal to $\frac{1}{1-(m_4-3)t}$. Each unknown column has three pairs to which we can attach a chain of known $4$-columns. Since it is also equipped with the factor of $t$ in the generating function, we can simply replace $t$ in $N_6(t)$ with $\frac{t}{(1-(m_4-3)t)^3}$. Structurally,
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{R}_6 =
    \mathcal{N}_6 \left(
    \begin{tikzpicture}[baseline = 6.2ex,scale = 0.41]
        \node[vertex] (A1) at (1*1.3,1) {};
        \node[vertex] (A2) at (1*1.3,1.7) {};
        \node[vertex] (A3) at (1*1.3,2.7) {};
        \node[vertex] (A4) at (1*1.3,3.3) {};
        \node[vertex] (A5) at (1*1.3,4.3) {};
        \node[vertex] (A6) at (1*1.3,5) {};
        \draw[pair] (A5.center) to (A6.center);
        \draw[pair] (A3.center) to (A4.center);
        \draw[pair] (A1.center) to (A2.center);
        \draw (1.3-0.5, 0.5) rectangle (1*1.3+0.5, 5.5);
    \end{tikzpicture}
        \star \,
    \textsc{Seq}\left(\,\fourcolab\,\right)
    \star
    \textsc{Seq}\left(\,\fourcolabII\,\right)
    \star
    \textsc{Seq}\left(\,\fourcolabIII\,\right)
    \right).
\end{equation}
In terms of generating functions, $R_6(t) = N_6\left(\frac{t}{(1-(m_4-3)t)^3}\right)$. All together,% by Equations \eqref{Eq:f6matching}, \eqref{Eq:Fstar6}, \eqref{Eq:R6struct} and \eqref{Eq:R6exp}
\begin{equation}
F_6^\mathrm{sym}(t) = F_6^*(t) = \frac{e^{(m_6-15m_4+30)t}}{(1\!-\!(m_4\!-\!3)t)^{15}} N_6\left(\frac{t}{(1\!-\!(m_4\!-\!3)t)^3}\right).
\end{equation}

\noindent
Finally, we generalise our approach to deduce $F_6^\mathrm{cen}(t)$. Relaxing the condition $m_3 = 0$ by making $m_3$ arbitrary, we get one extra column type which appears in the structure of all tables $\mathcal{F}^\mathrm{cen}_6$ with nontrivial weight and that is the $3$-column. These $3$-columns, however, form a disjoint set of sub-tables. This is because they are the only columns in which a number can appear in a triplet. Denote $\mathcal{Q}_6$ as the set of all tables constructed out of $3$-columns only (with column order irrelevant), then
\begin{equation}\label{Eq:F6F6Q}
    \mathcal{F}^\mathrm{cen}_6 = \mathcal{F}_6^\mathrm{sym} \star \mathcal{Q}_6
\end{equation}

Our goal is to find the construction relation for $\mathcal{Q}_6$. Let $b$ be a number in the first row of a given column of table $\tau \in \mathcal{Q}_6$. Since it is a 3-column, we denote the other number in the column as $b'$. We construct a permutation $\pi$ to a given table $\tau$ as composed from all those pairs $b \rightarrow b'$. Note that since $b$ and $b'$ are always different, the set off all $\pi$ corresponds to the set $D_j$ of all \textbf{derangements}. This association alone, however, is not a bijection.
