\begin{abstract}
Automatic software system optimization can improve software speed, reduce operating costs, and save energy.
Traditional approaches to optimization rely on manual tuning and compiler heuristics, limiting their ability to generalize across diverse codebases and system contexts.
Recent methods using Large Language Models (LLMs) offer automation to address these limitations, but often fail to scale to the complexity of real-world software systems and applications.

We present SysLLMatic, a system that integrates LLMs with profiling-guided feedback and system performance insights to automatically optimize software code.
We evaluate it on three benchmark suites: HumanEval\_CPP (competitive programming in \Cpp),
  SciMark2 (scientific kernels in Java),
  and
  DaCapoBench (large-scale software systems in Java).
Results show that SysLLMatic can improve system performance, including latency, throughput, energy efficiency, memory usage, and CPU utilization.
It consistently outperforms state-of-the-art LLM baselines on microbenchmarks.
On large-scale application codes, it surpasses traditional compiler optimizations, achieving average relative improvements of 1.85× in latency and 2.24× in throughput.
Our findings demonstrate that LLMs, guided by principled systems thinking and appropriate performance diagnostics, can serve as viable software system optimizers.
We further identify limitations of our approach and the challenges involved in handling complex applications. 
%This work provides a foundation for building next-generation tools to support developers and research software engineers in creating efficient software.
This work provides a foundation for generating optimized code across various languages, benchmarks, and program sizes in a principled manner.
%\NJE{@GKT Changed last sentence.} \GKT{Check!}


\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Software Engineering, Automatic Programming, Performance, Code Optimization, Large Language Models
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Software performance directly affects reliability~\cite{Jain1991}, user experience~\cite{Wilke2013}, energy use~\cite{Dayarathna2015, krasner2021cost, garraghan2014}, and sustainability~\cite{Ferme2017}; performance failures pose downtime and security risks~\cite{Liu2022Acquirer,mitre2024cwe1132,Deng2025AISecurity}.
% \PA{I will suggest highlighting 2-3 recent and real cases where inefficient software caused losses (financial, reputation, etc) to companies.}
% \PA{Do you have examples of these cases? If not, I can help you look around.}
Performance optimization becomes increasingly essential as data centers consume 3–4\% of electricity in major regions~\cite{powering_intelligence2024, eu2024sustainability, Chen2025energy}.
Despite its importance, software performance optimization often loses out to organizational goals like feature development, time-to-market, and business objectives~\cite{paulsen2018criticality}.
This is partly because efficiency, while valuable, is costly to achieve—requiring deep expertise, tooling, and time—and is typically prioritized only when performance bottlenecks become urgent or business-critical~\cite{currie2024green}.
%In addition, the high-performance computing community has increasingly recognized the importance of balancing performance with other nonfunctional requirements, such as portability---which enhances reproducibility---and programmer productivity~\cite{pennycook_metric_2016,chamont_performance_2019}.


The research community has extensively studied software optimization, including via
  compiler techniques~\cite{Muchnick1998},
  algorithmic improvements~\cite{Bader2002},
  and
  hardware-aware tuning~\cite{Buchty2012}.
%The expertise needed to apply these methods,
% Large Language Models (LLMs), including ChatGPT and models within the Llama and DeepSeek families, offer a promising alternative by automating software engineering tasks like debugging and code generation~\cite{Ozkaya2023, sarkar2022likeprogramartificialintelligence}, overcoming many of the limitations inherent in traditional methods.
% % \KL{Consider mentioning other LLMs, such as Llama}
% More recently, research has explored the application of LLMs for code optimization, spanning high-level~\cite{TODO}, low-level~\cite{TODO}, and domain-specific languages~\cite{TODO}. 
% Studies have evaluated their effectiveness across key metrics such as runtime, memory, and code quality, showing promising results in advancing automated code optimization~\cite{gong2025}.
Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a reasoning support tool~\cite{Ozkaya2023}.
Researchers have begun to apply them to code optimization, with encouraging preliminary results~\cite{gong2025,islam_eval_llm_codegen_2025}.
However, most prior work has focused on small code snippets from competitive programming benchmarks and has struggled to scale to real-world software systems~\cite{gong2025}.
Existing approaches also failed to systematically incorporate performance optimization knowledge and address code optimization in a principled way (\cref{sec:GapAnalysis}).

% \JD{Read TOMATS --- Hemingway.}

To address these limitations, we present SysLLMatic, an LLM-based code optimization pipeline that integrates system performance knowledge and runtime feedback.
SysLLMatic refactors software to improve metrics including latency, resource utilization, and energy efficiency.
It consists of three components:
  a catalog of performance optimization patterns,
  a profiling module,
  and
  a refinement module that applies the Diagnosis Cycle to iteratively optimize code.
SysLLMatic uses pre-trained LLMs and does not include any type of fine-tuning.
Information from the profiling module and performance optimization catalog is injected into prompts, which are then fed to the LLM to yield optimized code.
Thus, SysLLMatic readily generalizes across languages and benchmark suites.

We evaluate SysLLMatic on both synthetic benchmarks (HumanEval~\cite{chen2021evaluatinglargelanguagemodels} and SciMark2~\cite{scimark2}) and real-world applications (DacapoBench)~\cite{Blackburn2025}.
We compare it to prior LLM-based optimizations and traditional compiler-based optimizations. 
Overall, SysLLMatic outperforms baselines across three benchmarks on multiple performance metrics.
It achieves the highest relative improvements on SciMark2.
On HumanEval, it improves correctness by 12.41\% and Percentage Optimized (\%Opt) up to +8.88\% across all metrics over the LLM optimization baseline, with similar relative gains.
Notably, the compiler baseline achieves strong \%Opt on HumanEval, highlighting the continued strength of traditional compiler optimizations.
On four DaCapo applications, SysLLMatic achieves substantial performance gains, including a 3.44× latency and 4.63× throughput improvement in BioJava through parallelization.
Our results show that principled guidance improves the effectiveness of LLM-based software optimization.

\ul{In summary, we contribute}:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{A Catalog of Performance Optimization Patterns (\cref{sec:perf_optim_pattern}):}
      We present a systematic catalog of 43 performance optimization patterns targeting various performance metrics.
    \item \textbf{A Novel System Performance Optimization Framework (\cref{sec:methodology}):}
    Our language-agnostic LLM framework combines domain-specific knowledge from the catalog with program analysis to apply performance optimization patterns.
    \item \textbf{Multi-Metric Evaluation on Real-World Programs (\cref{sec:experiment}):}
    We apply the proposed framework to synthetic microbenchmarks and real-world applications from DacapoBench.
    Our results show an average of 1.85× and 2.24× improvements in latency and throughput, respectively.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.05cm}
\noindent
\underline{\textbf{Significance:}}
% Code optimization has been studied for over five decades, traditionally driven by compiler-based techniques and, more recently, by LLMs.
In this work, we explore LLMs as a tool for optimizing large-scale applications --- we aim to do this by leveraging principles of software optimization, contained in the performance optimization catalog.
Importantly, this moves closer to the goal of "push-button optimization", in which code can be significantly improved with little manual effort.
This is of great importance to researchers and software engineers, who can then focus on integrating \textit{software components} rather than spending significant time debugging and optimizing code.

