\section{Background and Related Works}
\label{sec:background}

This section provides background on systems performance (\cref{sec:rel-work-systems-performance}), then summarizes the state-of-the-art approaches to traditional (\cref{sec:rel-work-traditional-optimization}) and LLM-based code optimization (\cref{sec:rel-work-llm-optimization}).

\subsection{Systems Performance}
\label{sec:rel-work-systems-performance}
\subsubsection{Motivation}
Performance is a critical concern in modern computing systems.
For server workloads, % like distributed and cloud-based environments,
performance bottlenecks undermine responsiveness, throughput, and cost-efficiency~\cite{Gregg_2021}.
On the client side, performance affects energy consumption and perceived quality, especially on resource-constrained devices~\cite{Selakovic2016}.
In high-performance computing (HPC), optimized code enables large-scale simulations and data analysis that are otherwise computationally infeasible~\cite{Gupta2016}.
Across these and other domains, effective performance engineering enables high-quality software. %supports speed and broader goals such as reproducibility, sustainability, and maintainability.
%This motivates a systematic approach to identifying, measuring, and resolving bottlenecks as an integral part of the software systems development lifecycle.

% \subsubsection{Principles}
% System performance refers to the efficiency with which a computer system, including hardware and software components, executes tasks.
% Common performance bottlenecks stem from instruction-level inefficiencies~\cite{Koppelman2014}, memory hierarchy issues~\cite{Lam1991}, I/O contention~\cite{Zhan2012}, or inefficient algorithms~\cite{mitre2024cwe1132}.
% Optimizing system performance is inherently challenging; it is highly context-dependent, influenced by workload characteristics, system configuration, and architectural constraints~\cite{Daud2008}.
% Thus, system performance analysis often involves extensive profiling, trace analysis, and recording of relevant performance metrics such as latency, throughput, and memory.

\subsubsection{Methodology}
Foundational methods in system performance analysis, such as scalability modeling~\cite{Amdahl1967}, design heuristics~\cite{Jain1991}, and capacity planning~\cite{Gunther1997}, underpin modern approaches.
Building on these, Gregg summarizes best practices in \textit{Systems Performance} (2\textsuperscript{nd} ed.)~\cite{Gregg_2021}.
%This book presents 43 methodologies encompassing observational, experimental, and hypothetical analysis, analysis life-cycle models, statistical methods, and tuning techniques.
We draw on this body of work to inform our system design; the specific methodologies employed in this study are discussed below.

\textbf{Diagnosis Cycle}:
As summarized by Gregg, the Diagnosis Cycle methodology follows an iterative process (\cref{fig:cycle}).
Hypotheses are tested and refined through data collection, balancing principled reasoning against evidence. %empirical data.
%The iterative nature of the cycle enables the continuous refinement of hypotheses as new data are collected and analyzed.

\vspace{-0.15cm}
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, trim={2.4cm 6cm 2.4cm 20.5cm}, clip]{figures/background.pdf}
    \caption{
    Diagnosis Cycle: Performance hypotheses are tested and refined~\cite{Gregg_2021}.
    }
    \label{fig:cycle}
\end{figure}
\vspace{-0.15cm}

\textbf{System Profiling}:
Profiling is essential for analyzing and optimizing application performance, offering visibility into resource usage across CPU, GPU, and system components~\cite{mckenney1999differential, Graham1983, Graham1982}.
Tools like \texttt{perf} use timed sampling to capture user and kernel stack traces~\cite{shende1999profiling}.
These profiles are often analyzed with Flame Graphs~\cite{gregg2016flame}, where frame width indicates frequency, helping identify performance hotspots.
Beyond CPU profiling, tools like Intel RAPL provide data on energy use and power consumption~\cite{Marcus2012,thorat2017energy,khan2018rapl}. %, and correlated statistics such as CPU frequency.
In the context of HPC and systemwide benchmarking, tools such as NSight~\cite{iyer2016gpu}, THAPI~\cite{bekele2025thapi}, and GeoPM~\cite{geopm2017} offer useful traces or visualizations.
Performance engineers employ such tools in the diagnosis cycle to identify performance bottlenecks and guide targeted optimizations~\cite{future_soft_perf_eng_2017,perf_eng_case_smith_1982}.

\subsection{Traditional Approaches for Code Optimization}
\label{sec:rel-work-traditional-optimization}

