\textit{Code optimization} refactors programs to improve performance or efficiency without changing functionality, often prioritizing runtime metrics over readability or maintainability.
Traditional techniques largely focused on compiler-level transformations~\cite{bacon1994compiler}, such as global common-subexpression elimination~\cite{John1970}, loop-invariant code motion~\cite{Aho1986}, and register allocation~\cite{CHAITIN1981}.
% Sparse conditional constant propagation enabled constant folding across complex control flow with near-linear time~\cite{Wegman1991}.
%Cytron et al.~\cite{Cytron1991} introduced static single-assignment (SSA) form for unified data-flow analyses.
%Profile-guided optimization (PGO) followed, with path profiling~\cite{Ball1996} enabling code layout improvements based on runtime traces.
These and other advances %such as SSA~\cite{Cytron1991} and PGO~\cite{Ball1996}
are integrated into compilers such as LLVM~\cite{Lattner2004} and the JVM. %, a SSA-based infrastructure supporting compile-, link-, and run-time optimization.
%Beyond traditional compilers, tools like OpenTuner~\cite{Ansel2014} automate the search for optimal compiler configurations.

Complementing compiler-level transformations, source-code-level optimizations directly modify source code to improve performance. 
Common techniques include loop transformations (\eg unrolling~\cite{bacon1994compiler}) and tiling~\cite{Lam1991} to enhance instruction-level parallelism and cache locality.
Other key techniques are data structure selection~\cite{Couto2017} and memoization~\cite{bacon1994compiler,davis2021using,ford2002packrat}.
%memoization~\cite{bacon1994compiler}, dead code elimination~\cite{Cytron1991}, precision tuning~\cite{Khalifa2022}, and data structure selection~\cite{Couto2017} to improve memory access and algorithmic complexity.
%These optimizations can be applied manually or through source-to-source transformation tools. %, targeting performance gains such as increased throughput and reduced cache misses.
However, a key challenge for such optimizations is the vast search space~\cite{white_evo_improvement_code_2011}, requiring significant manual effort and expertise~\cite{Ansel2014, Selakovic2017}.
%Evolutionary programming approaches have been can handle certain code optimizations, but they are not universal~\cite{white_evo_improvement_code_2011}.
This motivates the need for automated techniques that can explore this space more efficiently. %---this is an area where LLMs offer a promising new perspective, as discussed in the next section.

\subsection{LLMs for Code Optimization}
\label{sec:rel-work-llm-optimization}
Large Language Models (LLMs) --- including ChatGPT and models from the LLaMA and DeepSeek families --- have been used to
  automate many engineering tasks
  such as code generation and completion~\cite{Li2023, nijkamp2023codegen, austin2021programsynthesislargelanguage},
  test case creation~\cite{wang2024, yuan2024},
  and defect detection and repair~\cite{wu2023, kang2023, chen2023, huang2023}.
Recently, they have been applied to code optimization~\cite{gong2025}, for
    high-level~\cite{shypula2024learning, liu2024evaluatinglanguagemodelsefficient},
    low-level~\cite{cummins2023largelanguagemodelscompiler, cummins2024metalargelanguagemodel, taneja2024},
    and
    domain-specific languages~\cite{wei2025improvingparallelprogramperformance}.
  These models can
    enhance runtime performance~\cite{Chen2024, Gao2025, shypula2024learning},
    reduce memory usage~\cite{Chen2024, Garg2022},
    and improve energy efficiency~\cite{dearing2025leveragingllmsautomateenergyaware}.
    %and increased overall code quality~\cite{li2025falconfeedbackdrivenadaptivelongshortterm}.
LLMs can accommodate complex code structures and support higher-level transformations such as loop restructuring and algorithm substitution.

Recent work has explored various approaches to LLM-based code optimization, with feedback-based iterative optimization~\cite{madaan2023selfrefine} being the most prominent~\cite{gong2025}.
%This approach leverages performance feedback to guide successive refinements.
For example, EFFI-LEARNER \cite{huang2024} employs a self-optimization pipeline that leverages the overhead profile to improve code efficiency. PerfCodeGen~\cite{peng2024perfcodegenimprovingperformancellm} leverages unit test runtime to guide performance improvements in generated code.
More recently, MARCO~\cite{rahman2025marcomultiagentoptimizinghpc} introduces a multi-agent framework for optimizing HPC code.
These works show promise on small benchmarks (\eg single-function LeetCode-style tasks) but their scalability to multi-language, application-level codebases remains unclear.

A recent survey by Gong \etal~\cite{gong2025} echoes this concern, cataloging key limitations of current LLM-based optimizers and noting that only 5 out of 55 (9\%) studies evaluate on full real-world projects, underscoring the gap between theory and practice.
Among these few, RAPGen~\cite{garg2025rapgenapproachfixingcode} uses retrieval-augmented prompting to guide LLMs in repairing performance bugs in C\# code. However, it is confined to \.NET-specific APIs and evaluates fixes only via static metrics like Verbatim Match, without execution. Choi \etal~\cite{Choi2024} explore iterative LLM-based refactoring to reduce cyclomatic complexity in Java projects from the Defects4J benchmark. Though the method is nominally language-agnostic, the evaluation relies solely on static code metrics. DeepDev-PERF~\cite{Garg2022} fine-tunes BART on real-world C\# projects and assesses performance via pre-defined benchmark tests, but does not report the impact on end-to-end application behavior.
In contrast to these approaches, we develop a language- and framework-agnostic framework that enables end-to-end optimization on real-world codebases, with performance evaluated at the application level.

\section{Problem Statement}

\subsection{Gap Analysis}
\label{sec:GapAnalysis}
Our work addresses three key gaps in LLM-based software optimization, as shown in~\cref{tab:related-works-llm-opt}.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Scalability challenges in LLM-based optimization:} Existing LLM-based methods automate code transformation but typically operate on small, isolated code segments, failing to scale to complex, multi-module software systems.
    \item \textit{Limited generalizability:} Current approaches are often fine-tuned for narrow tasks or rely on language-specific heuristics, with no principled guidance grounded in systems thinking. This limits their applicability across different languages and workload types.
    \item \textit{Limited metric scope:} Most prior works focus on optimizing a single metric--typically runtime--without considering multi-objective trade-offs (\eg energy, throughput) essential for real-world performance~\cite{gong2025}. Additionally, application optimizations often rely on static code metrics, not runtime performance feedback.
\end{enumerate}


