\subsection{Design Overview}
\label{sec:Design-overview}
Our system applies the Diagnosis Cycle~\cref{sec:background} to LLM-guided software optimization~\cref{fig:design}. To ensure scalability to real-world software systems, we begin by identifying performance-critical hotspots and extracting the corresponding source code for transformation~\cref{sec:perf_hotspot}. The optimization workflow proceeds through the four stages of the Diagnosis Cycle. In the hypothesis formulation stage~\cref{sec:formulation}, the Advisor LLM proposes optimizations based on high-level transformation patterns in \cref{fig:patterncatalog}. During instrumentation~\cref{sec:Instrumentation}, the system collects structural and dynamic context by extracting AST and runtime traces using performance profiling. These artifacts inform the Generator's prompt construction. In the data collection and refinement stages~\cref{sec:data-collection-refinement}, the new code variants generated by the Generator are then verified for correctness and re-profiled. The Evaluator analyzes the updated profiling data to assess improvements and guide further iterations.



\subsection{Performance Hotspot Identification}
\label{sec:perf_hotspot}
We identify optimization targets by analyzing Flame Graph traces (\cref{sec:background}), which rank functions by performance overhead. We start by getting the collapsed text format of the Flame Graph, where each line represents a stack trace.
For example, \texttt{funcA;funcB;funcC\ N} represents a stack trace where \texttt{funcA} calls \texttt{funcB}, which in turn calls \texttt{funcC}, and this trace occurred \texttt{N} times. We extract the Top-$k$ performance hotspots from this representation using the following formalization.

Let $\mathcal{L}$ denote the set of entries in the Flame Graph, where each element $(s, c)$ comprises a stack trace $s$ and a corresponding sample count $c \in \mathbb{N}$. Each stack trace $s = { f_1, f_2, \dots, f_k }$ is defined as an ordered sequence of function calls $f_i$:
\[
\mathcal{L} = \{ (s,c) \mid s = \{ f_1, f_2, \dots, f_k \}, c\in\mathbb{N} \}
\]
To identify the performance hotspots corresponding to the precise locations where CPU samples were collected, we proceed as follows.
Let $t$ represent a designated application-level method used to filter out system-level invocations.
For any stack trace $s$ containing $t$, we define $f_{\text{rightmost}} \in s$ as the rightmost function call in the trace—this corresponds to the location where the CPU sample was attributed.
The function $C(f_{\text{rightmost}})$ denotes the cumulative sample count associated with $f_{\text{rightmost}}$ across all relevant stack traces:
\[
\forall (s,c) \in L:\exists f \in s \mid (t \in f) \implies C(f_{\text{rightmost}}) = \sum_{f_{\text{rightmost}} \in s_i} c_i
\]

Finally, we get the set of $k$ functions $f_{\text{rightmost}}$ with the highest aggregated sample counts $C(f_{\text{rightmost}})$.
% \[
% \text{Top}_k = \{\, f \in \mathcal{F} \mid \left|\{\, g \in \mathcal{F} : C(g) > C(f) \}\right| < k \,\}.
% \]
After identifying relevant components, we extract relevant code snippets as self-contained units for optimization.
Our methodology allows both class-level and method-level optimizations.


\subsection{Diagnosis Cycle - Hypothesis Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}
In the first stage of the Diagnosis Cycle, the Advisor LLM synthesizes top-$K$ optimization hypotheses by analyzing the target code, its performance profiles, and a catalog of optimization patterns~\cref{sec:perf_optim_pattern}. These hypotheses are ranked based on their relevance to observed bottlenecks, forming the basis for subsequent implementation and evaluation.

\subsection{Diagnosis Cycle - Instrumentation}
\label{sec:Instrumentation}
In the Instrumentation stage of the Diagnosis Cycle, the system collects structural and dynamic context to support optimization. This includes the source code, its AST, and runtime performance traces represented via Flame Graphs. These inputs are passed to the Generator along with the top $K$ relevant optimization patterns. The prompt integrates this context using Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting to guide the LLM through a structured process: identify performance bottlenecks, select applicable patterns, and generate optimized code. All prompts and LLM outputs are stored in the Generator's memory to support iterative refinement.

\subsection{Diagnosis Cycle: Data Collection \& Hypothesis Refinement}
\label{sec:data-collection-refinement}
In the final stage of the Diagnosis Cycle, the Evaluator LLM analyzes the optimized code and generates natural language feedback to inform further optimization. The system first validates the code's correctness via benchmark tests. On compilation or runtime failure, the error is returned to the Generator. The process terminates if no correct version is produced within the predefined attempt or iteration limits. If validation succeeds, the system profiles the code to collect performance metrics (e.g., latency, energy) and generates a Flame Graph to identify bottlenecks. The Evaluator then compares the original and optimized code, incorporating profiling data to produce targeted feedback, which guides the next optimization.

\subsection{Prototype Implementation}
Our prototype implementation consists of approximately 3,000 lines of Python code and five prompts. Below, we describe the implementation details of each component.

\subsubsection{Program Analysis}
\label{sec:impl-analysis}
For the Java benchmarks, we generate ASTs using ANTLR4 with the \texttt{JavaLexer} and \texttt{JavaParser} modules and collect runtime performance data using \texttt{async-profiler} to produce flame graphs. For the HumanEval\_CPP benchmark, implemented in \Cpp, we extract ASTs using Clang’s AST dump and use Linux \texttt{perf} to generate corresponding flame graphs.
% For Java benchmarks, we additionally implement a code extraction module to isolate the original source code of a given method and replace it with the optimized version. This functionality is implemented in approximately 100 lines of Java code using \texttt{JavaParser}.
\subsubsection{Code Correctness}
\label{sec:impl-correctness}
We claim the generated code to be correct if it successfully compiles and passes all test cases associated with a particular benchmark.
The number of these tests differs across benchmarks. For instance, the HumanEval\_CPP benchmark includes an average of 9.6 tests, while SciMark evaluates accuracy by comparing output quality against a predefined threshold.
In the Dacapo benchmark suite, we rely on JUnit tests to verify program correctness, following previous works on real-world program optimization~\cite{Garg2022}. To further ensure correctness, we also manually inspect all outputs to verify that changes are reasonable.

\subsubsection{Performance Measurement}
\label{sec:perf measurement}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Latency:} We measure latency as the time elapsed from the start of program execution to its completion for a given input size or workload, reported in milliseconds.
\item \textbf{Memory Usage:} We measure memory usage using the Linux \texttt{/usr/bin/time} utility, which reports the peak Resident Set Size (RSS) in kilobytes.
\item \textbf{CPU Cycles:} We measure the total number of CPU cycles consumed during execution using hardware performance counters via \texttt{perf stat -e cycles}.
\item \textbf{Throughput:} We measure throughput as the amount of system work completed per unit time. In SciMark2, this metric is reported as MFLOPS (million-FLOPS). % of floating-point operations per second).
\item \textbf{Energy Consumption:} We measure energy consumption using the Intel Running Average Power Limit (RAPL), which accesses energy usage data from Model-Specific Registers (MSRs)~\cite{thorat2017energy, hahnel2012measuring, hackenberg2015energy}.
%  
\end{itemize}

\section{Experimental setup}
\label{sec:experiment}
\subsection{Evaluation Questions}
We evaluate the effectiveness of our system in optimizing software performance by addressing the following questions:
{\begin{RQList}
\item[EQ1:]	How effective is our overall LLM-based optimization approach in improving software performance compared to existing baselines?
\item[EQ2:] How well does the system balance multiple, potentially competing performance objectives, and which performance metrics are most improved?
\item[EQ3:] What is the contribution of each core component to the system’s optimization effectiveness?
\item[EQ4:] How does SysLLMatic performance vary based on the program context it receives?
%\item[EQ4:] How do program‑structure characteristics like code size and modular complexity affect the effectiveness of our system’s optimizations across the benchmark suite?
\end{RQList}}

\subsection{Datasets}
To evaluate our framework's effectiveness across languages, domains, and application scales, we use three benchmark suites:
  HumanEval\_CPP,
  SciMark2,
  and
  DaCapo.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Micro-benchmarks:} 
    %\JD{``micro-bencchmark''?}}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{HumanEval\_CPP}: HumanEval-X~\cite{zheng2023codegeex} extends HumanEval~\cite{chen2021evaluatinglargelanguagemodels} to multiple languages and includes 164 function-level tasks. We use the \Cpp version due to this language's relevance in performance-critical systems~\cite{Edwards2013}. To assess runtime efficiency, we augment it with translated stress tests from the COFFE benchmark~\cite{peng2025coffecodeefficiencybenchmark}, originally designed for HumanEval\_Python.
        \item \textbf{SciMark2}~\cite{scimark2}: A benchmark suite of five Java scientific computing kernels—FFT, LU, Monte Carlo, SOR, and SparseCompRow—targeting computation-intensive numerical workloads. Each kernel is implemented as a standalone Java file (26-340 lines).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Full-scale Benchmarks:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{DaCapo-23.11-Chopin}~\cite{Blackburn2025}: A collection of 22 multithreaded, real-world Java applications designed to benchmark full-scale systems (8k-400k lines of code).
        For this study, we selected a subset of 4 applications:
          BioJava, Fop, Pmd, and Graphchi.
          Other applications were excluded from the evaluation due to practical challenges in building and running them~\cite{garg2025rapgenapproachfixingcode, Gong2024}—such as custom build processes, complex dependency requirements, and the effort required to integrate them into our optimization pipeline. In addition, many files in DaCapo exceed 2,000 lines of code, where LLM optimizations often degrade due to lost context, leading to reduced performance and correctness. We conservatively skip files over 1,000 lines.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

