\subsection{Baselines}
We evaluate SysLLMatic against representatives from the two categories of baselines discussed in~\cref{sec:background}.
For compiler-based optimizations, we rely on standard compilers.
In particular, for the Java benchmarks we use the JIT compiler, and for the \Cpp benchmark we compile the code with \texttt{-O3} optimization.
For HumanEval\_CPP, we additionally employ OpenTuner~\cite{Ansel2014} to explore a larger configuration space of compiler flags beyond \texttt{-O3}.

For LLM-based optimizations, we compare against the SOTA training-free framework PerfCodeGen~\cite{peng2024perfcodegenimprovingperformancellm}, which also incorporates execution feedback into the optimization loop.
PerfCodeGen outperformed all against existing prompt-based optimization strategies, % and demonstrated superior performance,
so we exclude others in our study.
We apply PerfCodeGen to HumanEval\_CPP and SciMark, which align with its focus on small code units. 
We reimplemented its method using publicly available prompts and ensured a fair comparison by using the same input code, configuration, environment, and evaluation metrics.
Following their setup, we set K=1 with a single refinement iteration.
\ifEXTENDED
However, unlike PerfCodeGen, our methodology does not involve code generation.
Instead, we directly use the ground truth implementation, bypassing PerfCodeGen's code generation phase and feeding the same ground truth as the initial correctness solution input.
\fi

\subsection{Ablation Study}
To assess each component’s contribution, we perform an ablation study across all three benchmarks, incrementally enabling components to measure their individual impact.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Base:} Generator with only source code and prompt.
    \item \textbf{+Evaluator:} Generator with Evaluator (\textit{Iterative Refinement}).
    \item \textbf{+Context:} Add code analysis (\textit{Instrumentation}).
    \item \textbf{+Advisor:} Add Advisor (\textit{Hypothesis-driven optimization}).
\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.05cm}
\noindent
We also explore the effect of the number of feedback iterations.

\subsection{LLM Selection}
We use GPT-4o for optimizing our Micro-benchmarks. For the full-scale DaCapo benchmark, we use GPT-4.1 due to its superior performance on the SWEBench benchmark and larger context window~\cite{openai2024gpt4.1}. Following prior work~\cite{garg2025rapgenapproachfixingcode, shypula2024learning, Gao2025}, we set the LLM inference temperature to 0.7.

\subsection{Experiment Environment}
All LLM-generated programs are executed on a dedicated bare-metal server (Intel Xeon W-2295, 36 CPUs, 188 GB RAM) to ensure a consistent evaluation environment.

