\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:Discussion}
\subsection{How Far Are We Toward Push-Button Optimization?}

\paragraph*{Progress Beyond Small-Scale Examples} Ideally, code optimization would require only a source input, \eg a GitHub repository URL, to obtain an optimized version.
However, the controlled environments typically used in research differ substantially from production software.
Our work takes a step toward bridging this gap by moving from small-scale programs to real-world software optimization (see~\cref{sec:optimizations-made} and~\cref{sec:EQ1}).
Nonetheless, several challenges remain.
First, our approach assumes access to comprehensive test suites for correctness validation, which may not hold in practice (\cref{sec:impl-correctness}).
Second, real-world applications often have complex build and configuration dependencies; in our experiments, DaCapo applications were pre-built, but automating this for arbitrary software remains difficult~\cite{garg2025rapgenapproachfixingcode, Gong2024}.
Third, our method relies on profiling data to guide optimization;
while informative, such data can be time-consuming to collect at scale (see~\cref{sec:perf_hotspot} and~\cref{sec:impl-analysis}).
Real-world adoption must weigh the optimization gains against these computational and operational costs.

\paragraph*{Context Matters: Rethinking Function-level Code Generation with LLMs}
Prior work shows LLMs perform better at function-level code optimization than at broader scopes~\cite{Du2024}. 
However, most LLM4Code works often target isolated, self-contained functions, overlooking common scenarios where functions are embedded within larger codebases with dependency constraints~\cite{huang2024,peng2024perfcodegenimprovingperformancellm,rahman2025marcomultiagentoptimizinghpc}.
We show that ignoring such context leads to poor code optimization (see~\cref{sec:EQ4}, esp.~\cref{fig:class_method}).
%\JD{Please add a (cref) here to point to the specific findings you're talking about - looking for it right now}
Thus, we advocate for repository-level code generation and optimization as a target for future work.

\paragraph*{Toward Agentic LLMs for Optimization}
While our system uses a structured, feedback-driven loop, the LLM is not autonomous—it depends on external control. 
%where they independently decide how to revise code, when to stop iterating, and how to balance competing performance metrics.
Future work could explore an agentic approach, integrating decision-making and memory mechanisms into LLM-based optimizers, turning them into adaptive agents capable of self-directed software improvement.

