\vspace{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Costs of Applying SysLLMatic}

\paragraph*{Engineering Costs of More Rigorous Correctness Validation}
In LLM-for-Code research, like previous approaches to refactoring, test case validation is the standard for assessing code correctness.
This approach works well for competitive programming tasks and small-scale programs with controlled inputs.
However, its sufficiency is questionable when scaling to large systems (see~\cref{sec:manual-correctness}).
%\JD{Please cref to the Qualitative Analysis part where we mention this}
For example, in our experiments with DaCapoBench—a well-established benchmark suite with comprehensive JUnit test coverage—we observed that some optimized programs passed all tests but failed output validation due to parallelism-induced nondeterminism and race conditions.
This highlights the limitations of test-based validation in complex systems.
To ensure reliability in complex software, LLM-based optimization tools will require more rigorous correctness guarantees~\cite{shashidar2005}.
Future work could investigate methods such as automated test case generation, runtime monitoring, or formal verification.

\paragraph*{Computational Costs of Using SysLLMatic}
SysLLMatic is not free.
Our current approach runs the full test suite after each code modification. While optimizations like test case selection could reduce this cost, it remains non-trivial.
In addition, because SysLLMatic produces code that might be considered less maintainable (due to the standard performance-maintainability tradeoff, cf.~\cref{fig:comparative-quad-layout}), this cost might need to be incurred regularly rather than just once.

On a related note, LLM-based optimization approaches such as SysLLMatic can improve software efficiency but may incur notable energy costs.
A single ChatGPT query consumes around 0.3 Wh, according to a recent estimate~\cite{You2025}.
This cost increases with input length, model size, and repeated generation-validation cycles in our workflow.
While these upfront costs may be amortized over future executions, they raise sustainability concerns, and it is the engineering team's responsibility to assess whether such costs are worth the benefit in their context.
Future work should explore trade-offs between model size, optimization quality, and energy use, including lightweight alternatives for refinement.

\vspace{-0.1cm}
\section{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:Threats}

We discuss construct, internal, and external threats to validity~\cite{Wohlin2012}. Following the guidance of Verdecchia \etal~\cite{VERDECCHIA2023107329}, we focus on substantive threats that might influence our findings.

\underline{Construct Threats} are potential limitations of how we operationalize concepts.
We define \textit{performance} in terms of runtime metrics such as latency and memory footprint.
We omit code-level factors like readability or maintainability. 
We define ``\textit{correctness}'' based on existing test cases.
Although this is a reasonable choice and consistent with prior work~\cite{jakobs2022peqtest}, of course it may not fully capture semantic equivalence~\cite{shashidar2005}.
As a mitigation here, for DaCapo we automatically exclude files lacking unit tests from the list of optimization targets.

\underline{Internal Threats} are those that affect cause-effect relationships.
We benchmark against compiler and state-of-the-art LLM baselines under controlled settings, using fixed LLM temperature and the same environment.
To mitigate hardware-level variance, we do 2 warm-up runs followed by 5 measured runs per program, reporting the average of the latter runs.

\underline{External Threats} may impact generalizability.
We evaluated across three benchmarks, observing similar results across programming languages and software types. %both compute-intensive and transaction-processing systems.
However, there are several aspects of generalizability that are not assessed, most notably to other
  programming languages,
  application types,
  and hardware platforms.
The SysLLMatic approach may not generalize in these cases because of incomplete training data for the underlying LLM.
Another major limitation is that we used a commercial LLM in our evaluation, which let us measure on the best-performing LLM but reduces the replicability of our findings. 

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:Conclusions}

We present SysLLmatic, a performance-guided code optimization system that integrates performance domain knowledge with LLMs to improve program efficiency across diverse benchmarks, including real-world applications.
Our system outperforms both compiler-based and state-of-the-art LLM-based approaches on key metrics such as latency and throughput.
Future work includes integrating energy models to guide optimizations toward energy efficiency and enhancing correctness guarantees through formal verification.

