\section{Introduction}
Asymptotic theory describing long surface ring waves in a homogeneous fluid with the constant density $\rho_0$ propagating over a parallel depth-dependent current $u_0(z)$ was developed by \citet{J90}.  In that theory, the free surface elevation is described by the function $A(R, \xi, \theta)$, where $R = \varepsilon rk(\theta) $ is a slow variable, $\xi = rk(\theta) - st$ is a fast variable,  $r, \theta$ are polar coordinates in the reference frame moving with the speed $c$,  $\varepsilon$ is a small amplitude parameter, and $k(\theta)$ is a function describing the distortion of the wavefront caused by an underlying current, which needs to be determined as a part of the solution.  In non-dimensional variables, the bottom is at $z=0$, the unperturbed free surface is at $z=1$, and the linear long wave speed of concentric waves in the absence of any background current is $s=1$. The variations of the long wave speed of the ring wave in different directions to the current are accounted for with the help of the function $k(\theta)$, which satisfies the “generalised Burns condition” \citep{B, J90}:
\begin{equation}
(k^2+k'^2)\int_0^1\frac {1}{F^2}\,\mathrm{d}  z=1, \quad \mbox{where} \quad F = -s + (u_0 - c) (k \cos \theta - k' \sin \theta), \quad k' = \frac{\mathrm{d}k}{\mathrm{d}\theta}.
\label{GB}
\end{equation}
One needs to use the singular solution of this nonlinear first-order ordinary differential equation (i.e. the envelope of the general solution) in order to describe the ring waves. 

The general solution of  (\ref{GB}) has the form 
\begin{equation}
k(\theta) = a \cos \theta + b(a) \sin \theta, \quad \mbox{where} \quad  [a^2 + b(a)^2] \int_0^1 \frac{\mathrm{d}z}{[1-(u_0(z)-c) a]^2} =1.
\label{gs}
\end{equation}
The singular solution is then given by
\begin{eqnarray}
&&k(\theta) = a \cos \theta + b(a) \sin \theta,  \quad \mbox{where} \label{ss1}\\
&&[a^2 + b(a)^2] \int_0^1 \frac{\mathrm{d}z}{[1-(u_0(z)-c) a]^2} =1, \quad
\frac{\mathrm{d}b}{\mathrm{d}a} = - \frac{1}{\tan \theta}. \label{ss2}
\end{eqnarray}
There are no parameters in the singular solution, and the waves associated with the singular solution are the ring waves \citep{J90}. 

The amplitude equation had the form
\begin{equation}
\mu_1 A_R + \mu_2 A A_{\xi} + \mu_3 A_{\xi \xi \xi} + \mu_4 \frac{A}{R} + \mu_5 \frac{A_\theta}{R} = 0, \label{A}
\end{equation}
where the coefficients obtained by \citet{J90} were given as
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\mu_1= 2(k^2+k'^2) I_3, \label{j1}\\
&&\mu_2= -3(k^2+k'^2)^2I_4, \label{j2}\\
&&\mu_3= -(k^2+k'^2)^2 \int_{0}^1 \int_{z}^1 \int_{0}^{z_1}\frac{F^2(z_1,\theta)}{F^2(z,\theta)F^2(z_2,\theta)} \,\mathrm{d}  z_2 \,\mathrm{d}  z_1 \,\mathrm{d}  z, \label{j3}\\
&&\mu_4= \frac{-k(k+k'')}{k\cos \theta-k'\sin\theta}((k\cos\theta+3k'\sin\theta)I_2+4k'\sin\theta I_3) \nonumber \\
&& \quad -\frac{3k(k+k'')(k^2+k'^2)\sin^2\theta}{(k\cos\theta-k'\sin\theta)^2}(I_2+2I_3+I_4), \label{j4}\\
&& \mu_5 = \frac{2k}{k' \sin \theta - k \cos \theta} [k (k \sin \theta + k' \cos \theta) I_2 + (k^2 + k'^2) I_3 \sin \theta], \label{j5}\\
&&  I_n=\int_0^1\frac{\,\mathrm{d}  z}{F^n}, \label{j6}
%\quad \tilde \mu_i=\frac{\mu_i}{k^2+k'^2},
~~i=\overline{1,4}.
\end{eqnarray}
This equation was revised in 2016, when the theory was generalised to describe long surface and internal ring waves in stratified fluids with the density $\rho_0(z)$ propagating over a parallel depth-dependent current $u_0(z)$  \citep{KZ}. The generalisation was based on finding  the new far-field modal decomposition different from the known decomposition for the planes waves. In particular, the vertical particle displacement was given by the product of the amplitude function $A(R, \xi, \theta)$ and the modal function $\phi(z, \theta)$,  with the latter function satisfying the following set of modal equations:
\begin{eqnarray}
\left (\frac{\rho_0 F^2}{k^2 + k^{'2}} \phi_z\right )_z -  \rho_{0z} \phi &&= 0 \quad \mbox{for} \quad  0 < z < 1, \label{modal1} \\
\frac{F^2}{k^2 + k^{'2}} \phi_z -  \phi &&= 0 \quad \mbox{at} \quad z=1, \label{modal2} \\
\phi &&= 0 \quad \mbox{at} \quad z=0, \label{modal3}
\end{eqnarray}
where
\begin{equation}
F = -s + (u_0 - c) (k \cos \theta - k' \sin \theta).
\end{equation}
Here,  $c$ was set to be equal to the speed of the current at the bottom, i.e.~$c =u_0(0)$. 
The amplitude equation had the form (\ref{A}), but the coefficients were now given in terms of the modal function by the formulae
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\mu_1 = 2 s \int_0^1 \rho_0 F \phi_z^2 ~ \mathrm{d}z,  \label{c1}\\
&&\mu_2 = - 3 \int_0^1 \rho_0 F^2 \phi_z^3 ~ \mathrm{d}z,   \label{c2}\\
&&\mu_3 = - (k^2 + k'^2) \int_0^1  \rho_0 F^2 \phi^2 ~ \mathrm{d}z,   \label{c3}\\
&&\mu_4 = - \int_0^1 \left \{  \frac{\rho_0 \phi_z^2 k (k+k'')}{(k^2+k'^2)^2} \left ( (k^2-3k'^2) F^2 -
{4 k' (k^2 + k'^2) (u_0-c)\sin \theta} F  \right .  \right . \nonumber \\
&&\left . \left .  - \sin^2 \theta (u_0-c)^2(k^2 + k'^2)^2 \right ) 
  +  \frac{2 \rho_0 k}{k^2 + k'^2} F \phi_z \phi_{z\theta} (k' F + (k^2 + k'^2) (u_0-c) \sin \theta ) \right \} ~ \mathrm{d}z,\qquad   \label{c4}\\
&& \mu_5 = - \frac{2k}{k^2 + k'^2} \int_0^1 \rho_0 F \phi_z^2 [k' F + (u_0-c) (k^2+k'^2) \sin \theta ] ~ \mathrm{d}z.  \label{c5}
\end{eqnarray}
In the case of surface waves in a homogeneous fluid, Johnson's coefficients are recovered by taking
$\displaystyle \tilde \mu_i = \mu_i/(k^2+k'^2)$ (for details see \citet{KZ}). The new formulation has allowed to prove that, for surface ring waves in a homogeneous fluid, the coefficient $\mu_5 = 0$ for any current $u_0(z)$. Indeed, differentiating the generalised Burns condition (\ref{GB}), we obtain:
$$2 (k+k'')  \int_0^1\frac{k'F+(u_0-c)(k^2+k'^2)\sin\theta}{F^3} \,\mathrm{d}  z=0.$$
Since  $k+k''\ne 0$ on the singular solution,  then
$$\mu_5=- 2\rho_0 k (k^2+k'^2)  \int_0^1\frac{k'F+(u_0-c)(k^2+k'^2)\sin\theta}{F^3} \,\mathrm{d}  z=0.$$
Hence, the long surface ring waves in a homogeneous fluid are actually described by a simpler 1+1-dimensional cKdV-type equation
\begin{equation}
\mu_1 A_R + \mu_2 A A_{\xi} + \mu_3 A_{\xi \xi \xi} + \mu_4 \frac{A}{R}  = 0. \label{cKdV}
\end{equation}
Among some other curious features revealed using the formulation based on the far-field modal decomposition for a stratified fluid with a parallel shear current was the qualitatively different behaviour of the wavefronts of surface and interfacial waves propagating over the same currents. For example,  the wavefront of the surface ring wave in a two-layer fluid with a piecewise-constant current of increasing strength approaching a critical value was elongated in the direction of the flow, while the wavefront of the interfacial wave was squeezed. This phenomenon was linked to the presence of long-wave instability  of plane waves tangent to the ring wave and propagating in the downstream and upstream directions for a sufficiently strong current (see \citet{KZ} and  \citet{O, BM, BC, LM}). Similar effects were shown to take place in a two-layer fluid with a two-parameter family of upper-layer currents $\displaystyle u_0 = \gamma \left ( (z+d)/d \right )^\alpha$ for a sufficiently small value of $\alpha$ (with $-d$ denoting the position of the interface) by \cite{K20, HKG} and in a three-layer fluid for a current with a constant vertical shear (for the second interfacial mode) by \cite{TABK}.

Interestingly, while ring waves associated with the singular solution of the generalised Burns condition have been studied, the waves associated with the general solution were overlooked. Their role as describing plane waves tangent to the ring wave was understood only recently, in connection with the kinematic consideration of the so-called “hybrid waves” consisting of a part of a ring wave and two tangent plane waves  \citep{HKG}. One of the aims of the present study is to better understand the role of the amplitude equation corresponding to the general solution of the generalised Burns condition. The overarching aim of the paper is to study the evolution of perturbed nonlinear plane, ring and hybrid waves (consisting of a part of a ring wave and two tangent plane waves) within the scope of both the full two-dimensional Boussinesq-type equations for surface waves \citep{P} and the relevant reduced amplitude equations.

In this paper we will restrict our considerations to the case of long surface waves in a homogeneous fluid, although all constructions have a counterpart in stratified fluids, as discussed above. In Section 4 we further clarify the role of the amplitude equation corresponding to the  general solution of the generalised Burns condition by showing that it can be used to describe plane waves propagating at an angle to the current, as well as the evolution of very long transverse perturbations of line solitons, which we illustrate in the absence of a current. Importantly, the equation can be mapped to the Korteweg - de Vries equation, but with the parametric dependence on the polar angle. This allows us to make analytical predictions concerning the 2D fission of a perturbed line soliton, using the Inverse Scattering Transform \citep{GGKM}. Transverse perturbations of the line solitons modelled by the Kadomstev-Petviashvili (KP) equation (in particular, KP II equation, describing water waves) were extensively studied, both from the applied and rigorous perspectives (see \citet{KP, P91, KP00, MT, Mi, MS1, HP, MS2} and references therein). However, to the best of our knowledge, the amplitude equation discussed in our paper has not been used before. We show that it can be used instead of the KPII equation when the perturbations of line solitons are very long, but are not necessarily small. Recently, transverse amplitude and phase perturbations to line solitons were modelled numerically in the Serre-Green-Naghdi regime by \citet{GK} and the results are qualitatively similar to the plane waves propagating over localised bottom topography (see \citet{BBBD, GS}). The new equation could find useful applications in these settings.

Next, in Sections 5 and 6, we consider the perturbed ring waves and hybrid waves, respectively, in the absence of a current.  Different aspects of stability of ring waves is a topic of active current research (see \citet{K20, HKG, K24, HPS, ZHGS} and the references therein). Our simulations within the full 2D Boussinesq-type system allow us to investigate the long time evolution of the perturbations. We show that the outward propagating ring waves are stable, however, inward propagation leads to the amplification of a perturbation, transition through the origin, and subsequent decrease and splitting of the perturbation. Inward propagating ring waves that cross through the origin were modelled in the axisymmetric case by \citet{CW}. 

We finish by modelling propagation of the so-called hybrid wave. A hybrid wave in this setup consists, to leading order, of an arc of a concentric ring wave in the centre of the wavefront with the two attached tangent plane waves. SAR images of internal waves of similar shape can be seen in \citet{A2003}.  Such hybrid waves were introduced and studied by \citet{OS}. A more general kinematic construction was developed  for the waves propagating over a parallel depth-dependent shear flow \citep{HKG}. We show that the evolution of the hybrid wave is strongly dependent on whether it is a diverging or converging wave, and that the outward propagating (diverging) wave is stable. However, the inward propagating (converging)  case leads to a very peculiar instability scenario: there appears a large roque wave which disintegrates into three pieces and eventually evolves into a stable ``X-type" structure known from the theory of the KPII equation (e.g.  \citet{AC, CK, YW}). For the stable cases, qualitatively similar propagation has been seen  from a ``V-type'' initial conditions by \citet{CK09,YGJW, RMBH, RHB, BBH}. We also note that the key features of the outward propagating case can be described by the Whitham modulation theory (see \citet{BHM,HLW} and references therein). Finally, in Section 7 we make concluding remarks about some natural extensions of our present studies.







