\section{Three-dimensional plane and ring waves}

For both surface plane and ring waves on a homogeneous fluid the corresponding modal function is given by
\begin{equation}
\phi = (k^2 + k'^2) \int_0^z \frac{1}{F^2} ~ \mathrm{d}z \label{phi}
\end{equation}
\citep{KZ}.   In both cases the amplitude equation has the general form (\ref{A}), with the coefficients given by the formulae (\ref{c1}) - (\ref{c5}), where one needs to use the general solution of (\ref{GB}) for plane waves, and the singular solution for ring waves.

The waves associated with the general solution are plane waves tangent to the ring wave with the phase $\xi = rk(\theta) - st = a x + b(a) y - st$, where the constants $a$ and $b(a)$ are the two wavenumbers. The current is assumed to be in the $x$-direction, and the angle between the direction of the wavevector ${\bf k} = (a, b(a))$ and the positive $x$-direction is given by
\begin{equation}
\tan \varphi = \frac{b(a)}{a}. \label{tanphi}
\end{equation}
\begin{figure}
	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{Fig1}}
	\caption{Wavefront and wave vector of a plane wave propagating at an angle $\varphi$ to the direction of the current.}
\label{angleflowpic}
\end{figure}

Previously, we have shown that, for a homogeneous fluid with $\rho_0 = \text{const}$ and any parallel current $u_0(z)$,  $\mu_5 = 0$ for the ring waves. Using Johnson's formula (\ref{j5}) we can see that, for plane waves in a homogeneous fluid, the coefficient $\mu_4 = 0$ for any current $u_0(z)$ since $k+k'' = 0$ on the general solution.  This also follows from  (\ref{c4}), using the formula (\ref{phi}). Indeed, 
$$
\phi_{z \theta} = \frac{2  (k + k'')}{F^3} [k' F + (k^2+k'^2) (u_0(z)-c) \sin \theta] = 0.
$$
Hence, the equation describing three-dimensional plane waves in a homogeneous fluid propagating at an angle to a depth-dependent parallel current $u_0(z)$ are described by the equation
\begin{equation}
\mu_1 A_R + \mu_2 A A_{\xi} + \mu_3 A_{\xi \xi \xi} + \mu_5 \frac{A_\theta}{R}  = 0, \label{KdVTHETA}
\end{equation}
while ring waves are described by the equation
\begin{equation}
\hat \mu_1 A_R + \hat  \mu_2 A A_{\xi} + \hat \mu_3 A_{\xi \xi \xi} + \hat \mu_4 \frac{A}{R}  = 0. 
\label{CKDV}
\end{equation}
The coefficients $\mu_i$ are computed using the general solution (\ref{gs}) in the formulae (\ref{c1}) - (\ref{c3}) and (\ref{c5}), and the coefficients $\hat \mu_i$ are computed using the singular solution (\ref{ss1}) - (\ref{ss2})  in the formulae (\ref{c1}) - (\ref{c4}). Generally, the coefficients have a nontrivial dependence on $\theta$.
\begin{figure}
  \centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig2}}
  \caption{$(a)$ The general solution (\ref{gs}) for $a = 0.7$ (blue), $a=0.8$ (dashed blue) and $a=0.9$ (dashed dot blue) and its envelope the singular solution (red) all for $\gamma = 0.2$. $(b)$ The wavefronts of the corresponding general and singular solutions from $(a)$ described by $rk(\theta) = 5$.}
	\label{sing_gen_sol_fig}
\end{figure}
We note that the slow time $T = \epsilon t$ versions of the equations have the same form (i.e. $R \to T$ same to leading order). Equation (\ref{CKDV}) is a cKdV-type equation, matching the cKdV equation in the absence of a current. To the best of our knowledge, equation (\ref{KdVTHETA}) has not been used before and we will refer to it as the KdV$\theta$ equation.

For the plane surface and internal waves co-propagating with a shear flow it can be shown that the amplitude equation (\ref{A}) reduces to the previously known KdV equation (e.g. \citet{G}, evaluated for the flat bottom topography). Indeed, on the general solution $k = a \cos \theta$ and  $\xi = rk(\theta) - st = a(x - \hat{s}t)$, where  we have scaled $s \rightarrow a\hat{s}$. The modal equations (\ref{modal1})-(\ref{modal3}) reduce to 
\begin{eqnarray}
    \left[ \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s})^2 \phi_z \right]_z - \rho_{0z} \phi &&= 0 \quad \text{for} \quad 0 < z < 1, \label{gmodal1} \\
    (u_0 - \hat{s})^2 \phi_z  - \phi &&= 0 \quad \text{at} \quad z = 1. \label{gmodal2} \\
    \phi &&= 0 \quad \text{at} \quad z = 0. \label{gmodal3}
\end{eqnarray}

Next, on the general solution $k + k'' = 0$, giving $\mu_4 = 0$ from Johnson's representation of the coefficients (also, $\phi_{z \theta} = 0$ for the plane wave, and then the same conclusion follows from another representation). Also, for plane waves, of (\ref{A}) $A_{\theta} = 0$, giving the reduction of the amplitude equation to a KdV equation, while retaining this term gives a more general equation which can be used to describe some perturbations of the plane waves, which we will discuss later. Hence, for the co-propagating plane waves, scaling $\xi = rk(\theta) - st = a(x - \hat{s}t)$ to become $\hat{\xi} = x - \hat{s}t$ and computing the coefficients in (\ref{A}) we obtain
\begin{equation}
    I_1 A_T + I_2 AA_{\hat{\xi}} + I_3 A_{\hat{\xi}\hat{\xi}\hat{\xi}} = 0, \label{gKdV}
\end{equation}
where $T = \epsilon t$, $\hat{\xi} = x - \hat{s}t$ and
\begin{eqnarray}
    I_1 &&= 2 \int_0^1 \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s}) \phi_z^2 ~ \mathrm{d}z, \label{gI1} \\
    I_2 &&= - 3 \int_0^1 \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s})^2 \phi_z^3 ~ \mathrm{d}z, \label{gI2} \\
    I_3 &&= - \int_0^1 \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s})^2 \phi^2 ~ \mathrm{d}z. \label{gI3}
\end{eqnarray}
The amplitude equation (\ref{gKdV}) and coefficients (\ref{gI1})-(\ref{gI3}) match those of \citet{G} identically. 

The amplitude equation for obliquely propagating plane waves was given by \citet{M} and is also given by (\ref{gKdV}), however, $\hat{\xi} = x + b(a)y/a - \hat{s} t$ and
\begin{eqnarray}
    I_1 &&= 2 \int_0^1 \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s}) \phi_z^2 ~ \mathrm{d}z, \\
    I_2 &&= - 3 \int_0^1 \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s})^2 \phi_z^3 ~ \mathrm{d}z, \\
    I_3 &&= - \frac{(a^2 + b(a)^2)}{a^2}\int_0^1 \rho_0 (u_0 - \hat{s})^2 \phi^2 ~ \mathrm{d}z.
\end{eqnarray}
The modal equations are given by
\begin{eqnarray}
    \left[ \frac{\rho_0 a^2 (u_0 - \hat{s})^2}{a^2 + b(a)^2} \phi_z \right]_z - \rho_{0z} \phi &&= 0 \quad \text{for} \quad 0 < z < 1, \label{obmodal1} \\
    \frac{a^2(u_0 - \hat{s})^2}{{a^2 + b(a)^2}} \phi_z  - \phi &&= 0 \quad \text{at} \quad z = 1, \label{obmodal2} \\
    \phi &&= 0 \quad \text{at} \quad z = 0. \label{obmodal3}
\end{eqnarray}
Once again, for plane waves the term $A_{\theta} = 0$ since there is no change in the tangential direction along the wavefront. It is noted here that the modal equations (\ref{obmodal1})-(\ref{obmodal3}) reduce to the case of (\ref{gmodal1})-(\ref{gmodal3}) when the wave is co-propagating with the current since $a = \cos\varphi$ and $b(a) = 0$.

For example, evaluating (\ref{c1})-(\ref{c3}) and (\ref{c5}) for the surface waves in a homogeneous fluid over a linear shear flow $u_0(z) = \gamma z$ and using the general solution $k(\theta) = a \cos\theta + b(a) \sin\theta$ gives the coefficients of the amplitude equation (\ref{KdVTHETA}) to be
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mu_1 &&= \frac{2s - \gamma a}{s (s-\gamma a)^2}, \\
\mu_2 &&= \frac{(a^2+b(a)^2)^2(3s-3s\gamma a + \gamma^2 a^2)}{s^3(s-\gamma a)^3}, \\
\mu_3 &&= \frac{(a^2+b(a)^2)^2}{3s^2}, \\
\mu_5 &&= \frac{(a \cos\theta + b(a)\sin\theta)[2b(s-\gamma a) \cos\theta - (2as + a^2 \gamma - b(a)^2 \gamma)\sin \theta]}{s(s-\gamma a)^2}.
\end{eqnarray*}
When there is no shear flow the coefficients reduce to $\mu_1 = 2$, $\mu_2 = 3$, $\mu_3 = 1/3$, and $\mu_5 = -2\sin\theta\cos\theta$. If the wave has a plane wavefront, then $A_{\theta} = 0$ and the resulting equation is the KdV equation for the long surface plane waves, but the full equation (\ref{KdVTHETA}) can be used to describe the intermediate stage of the evolution of perturbed plane waves for very long transverse perturbations of arbitrary amplitude, which will be discussed in Section 4.










