\section{Perturbed line solitons}
\label{plane_waves}

In this section we apply strong transverse amplitude perturbations to the line soliton solution of the KdV$\theta$ equation, where we have changed the variables from $(R,\xi,\theta)$ to $(T=\epsilon t,\xi,\theta)$ for better accuracy when comparing numerical solutions to those of the 2D Boussinesq system, initiating both computations with the same initial condition. To leading order the change of variables $R \rightarrow T$ does not change the amplitude equation (\ref{A}). In the case of surface plane waves on a homogeneous fluid the coefficients $\mu_1$, $\mu_2$, and $\mu_3$ are constants determined by the propagation angle $\varphi$, the shear flow $\gamma$, and the coefficient $\mu_5$ is a function of $\theta$.

Here, we consider surface waves without a shear flow, and the KdV$\theta$ equation, for the waves propagating in the $x$-direction, takes the form
\begin{eqnarray}
&&A_T + \frac{3}{2}  AA_{\xi} + \frac{1}{6}  A_{\xi\xi\xi} -  \frac 12 \sin 2 \theta   \frac{A_{\theta}}{T} = 0, \quad \mbox{where} \\
&&T = \epsilon t, \quad \xi = r \cos \theta - t = x-t, \quad \theta = \arctan \frac{y}{x}.
\label{KdV}
\end{eqnarray}
A long transverse amplitude perturbation is added to the initial condition, at some $T = T_0$, by multiplying the initial condition by a suitable function of $\theta$. The unperturbed initial condition  is the $\theta$-independent exact line soliton of the KdV$\theta$ equation (\ref{KdV}), given as 
\begin{equation}
A_0(T_0,\xi,\theta) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[\xi - vT_0 - \xi_0 \right] \right),
\end{equation}
where $v>0$ and $\xi_0$ are constants.
The perturbed initial condition has the form
\begin{equation}
A(T_0,\xi,\theta) = \left(1 + \alpha \operatorname{sech}^2 \beta\theta \right) A_0(T_0,\xi,\theta), \label{pert_soliton} 
\end{equation}
where the constants $\alpha$ and $\beta$ control the amplitude and width of the perturbation, $v$ is the speed of the soliton, and $\xi_0$ is an initial phase shift. The multiplication of the original $\operatorname{sech}^2$ soliton by this $\theta$-dependent function generates a smooth, localised perturbation in the central region of the wavefront. 

The KdV$\theta$ equation  can be analysed analytically, using a mapping similar to that noted by \citet{J90} for the ring waves.  Indeed, for every value of $\theta$, the change of variables
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\theta} = T \exp \left( - \int \frac{\mu_1}{\mu_5} ~ \mathrm{d}\theta \right),
\end{equation}
maps  the KdV$\theta$ equation (\ref{KdV})  to the usual KdV equation
\begin{equation}
A_T + \frac 32 AA_{\xi} +  \frac 16 A_{\xi\xi\xi} = 0. \label{kdveqn}
\end{equation}
The change of variables, however, means that the initial condition for this KdV equation will have parametric dependence on $\tilde\theta$, and hence, on the polar angle $\theta$. 

To use the results of the Inverse Scattering Transform \citep{GGKM} we transform the KdV equation (\ref{kdveqn}) into the canonical form
\begin{equation}
U_{\tau} - 6UU_X + U_{XXX} = 0, \label{cankdv}
\end{equation}
via the scalings $\displaystyle U = - \frac 32 A,  \ \tau =  \frac 16 T,  \ X = \xi.$ The wavefield is then defined by the spectrum of the Schr\"{o}dinger equation
\begin{equation}
\Psi_{XX} + \left[\lambda - U(\tau_0,X;\tilde\theta) \right]\Psi = 0, \label{Schrodinger_eq}
\end{equation}
where the potential is given by the perturbed initial condition (\ref{pert_soliton}) 
\begin{equation}
U(\tau_0,X;\tilde\theta) = - \Lambda \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{X}{L} \right), \quad \mbox{where} \quad  \Lambda = 3v\Delta, \ L = \frac{2}{\sqrt{6v}}, \ \mbox{and}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\Delta\left(\tau_0;\tilde\theta \right) = 1 + \alpha \operatorname{sech}^2\left( \beta \arctan \frac{\tilde{\theta}}{6\tau_0} \right).
\end{equation}
The number of generated solitons depends on $\theta$ and  is given by the greatest integer satisfying the inequality
\begin{equation}
N\left(\tau_0;\tilde\theta\right) < \frac{1}{2} \left[\left(1 + 4\Lambda L^2 \right)^{\frac{1}{2}} + 1 \right]
\end{equation}
(see, e.g. \citet{LL}). Discrete eigenvalues are given by
\begin{equation}
\lambda = -k_n^2, \quad \mbox{where} 
\end{equation}
%where
\begin{eqnarray}
k_n\left(\tau_0;\tilde{\theta}\right) = \frac{1}{2L}\left[\left(1 + 4\Lambda L^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} - (2n-1) \right] > 0, \quad n = 1,2,...,N. \label{ISTk}
\end{eqnarray}
For positive $\alpha$-values and $v$-values the central perturbed region fissions into at least two solitons and a dispersive wave train. However, for small $\alpha$-values the secondary soliton is small and for a very long time  it blends with the radiation. The outer, unperturbed section of the line soliton propagates as a single soliton. We therefore expect a localised two-dimensional region of radiation behind a smaller soliton in the wake of the main perturbed soliton. The long-time asymptotics takes the form
 \begin{equation}
U\left(\tau,X;\tilde{\theta}\right) \simeq - \sum_{n=1}^N 2k_n^2 \operatorname{sech}^2 \left( k_n(X - 4k_n^2\tau - X_n)\right) + \text{radiation}, \label{ISTU}
\end{equation}
(e.g. \citet{D}), where the phase shifts are given by
\begin{eqnarray}
X_1 &&= \frac{1}{2k_1} \ln \left(\frac{c_1}{2k_1} \right), \\
X_n &&= \frac{1}{2k_n} \ln \left(\frac{c_n}{2k_n} \prod_{m=1}^{n-1} \left( \frac{k_n - k_m}{k_n + k_m} \right)^2 \right), \quad n > 1,
\end{eqnarray}
and the constants $c_n$ are found as
\begin{equation}
c_n = \left(\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Psi_n^2(x) \text{ d}x \right)^{-1},
\end{equation}
where $\Psi_n(X)$ is the eigenfunction of (\ref{Schrodinger_eq}) corresponding to the $n^{\text{th}}$ eigenvalues of $-k_n^2$,
\begin{equation}
\Psi_n = \text{const}\left(1 - \tanh^2 \frac{x}{L} \right)^{\frac{k_nL}{2}} {}_{2}F_{1}\left(1-n, 2k_nL + n,k_nL+1, \frac{1 - \tanh \frac{x}{L}}{2} \right).
\end{equation}
Here ${}_{2}F_{1}(\cdots)$ is the hypergeometric function, and the constant should be chosen to normalise the eigenfunction at  infinity,
\begin{equation}
\Psi_n \sim \exp^{-k_nx} \quad \text{as} \quad x \rightarrow + \infty.
\end{equation}
We can therefore analytically describe the main soliton and the extra solitons produced, for every direction defined by the polar angle $\theta$.

When there is no perturbation, i.e. $\Delta = 1$, the solution is simply the exact line soliton solution. When $\Delta > 1$ there is a localised perturbation to the wavefront, detectable in some region given by $-\tilde{\theta}_1 < \tilde{\theta} < \tilde{\theta}_1$ such that the amplitude $A(T,\xi,\theta) > A(T_0,\xi,\theta) + \epsilon$. The estimate for the region determining where the perturbation lies is therefore given by the formula $4 k_1(\tilde\theta)^2/3 > - 2 U(\tau_0,X;\tilde\theta)/3 + \epsilon$ arising from (\ref{ISTU}). The small secondary solitons are then detectable when $A(T,\xi,\theta) > \epsilon$ and hence when $4 k_n(\tilde\theta)^2/3 > \epsilon$.

The initial condition to the KdV$\theta$ equation (\ref{pert_soliton}) gives corresponding perturbed soliton initial condition in the $(x,y)$-plane for the Boussinesq-type system as
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta(t_0,x,y) &&= 2 \nu \left(1 + \alpha \operatorname{sech}^2 \left( \beta \arctan \frac{y}{x} \right) \right) 
%\nonumber 
%\\ &&\qquad 
 \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left(x - (1 + \epsilon v)t_0 - x_0 \right) \right), \label{Bouss_pert_soliton1} \\
u(t_0,x,y) &&= \eta(t_0,x,y), \label{Bouss_pert_soliton2} \\
v(t_0,x,y) &&= 0. \label{Bouss_pert_soliton3}
\end{eqnarray}
The computational parameters can be found in table \ref{table_soliton_perts}, and the corresponding numerical results are shown in figure \ref{pert_comps}. 
