\section{Ring waves}
\label{ring_waves}

Concentric ring waves are modelled by the cKdV equation
\begin{equation}
2\eta_T + 3\eta\eta_{\xi} + \frac 13 \eta_{\xi\xi\xi} + \frac{\eta}{T} = 0.
\end{equation}
Perturbations to the ring wave are added in the same manor as for the plane wave above. We first take the exact KdV soliton solution and use the singular solution, $k(\theta) = 1$, to map back to the $(x,y)$-plane given $\xi = rk(\theta) - t$. This gives a line soliton bent into a ring as
\begin{equation}
\eta(t_0,x,y) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left( \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} - (1 + \epsilon v)t_0 - x_0 \right] \right)
\end{equation}
and take the Cartesian velocities $u$ and $v$ to be those found in deriving the $1+1$-dimensional cKdV-type equation from 2D Boussinesq equations above, this gives that
\begin{eqnarray}
u = \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} ~ \eta, \quad
v = \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} ~ \eta.
\end{eqnarray}
The perturbed initial condition has the form
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta(t_0,x,y) &&= 2v \left(1 + \alpha H(x) \operatorname{sech}^2 \beta y \right) \operatorname{sech}^2\left( \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} - (1 + \epsilon v)t_0 - x_0 \right] \right), \\
u(t_0,x,y) &&= \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} ~ \eta(t_0,x,y), \\
v(t_0,x,y) &&= \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} ~ \eta(t_0.x,y),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ control the amplitude and width of the perturbation, respectively, and $H = H(x)$ is the Heaviside function to only give the perturbation on one side of the ring. 
