\section{Hybrid waves}
\label{hybrid_waves}

In this section we construct and model the so-called hybrid waves, generated with an arc of a ring wave and two plane waves joining at a predetermined $\theta$-angle such that the plane waves are tangent to the ring wave \citep{OS,HKG}. The two regions are constructed using the same description for plane waves and ring waves, as above, where the propagation angle for the plane waves is such that the wavefront has a continuous derivative. Surface signatures of similarly looking internal waves generated in the Strait of Gibraltar can be seen in figures 13 and 14 of \citet{A2003}. As discussed in the Introduction, all considerations of our present work can be extended to the internal waves.

We generate the hybrid wavefront from the exact solution of the KdV$\theta$ equation given as
\begin{equation}
\eta(T,\xi,\theta) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ \xi - v T - \xi_0 \right] \right).
\end{equation}
We match the two plane waves to an arc of a ring waves at the polar angles $\theta = \pm \varphi$. For the plane wave sections, defined when $|y| \geq x \tan\varphi$, we take the general solution, $k(\theta) = a \cos\theta + b(a) \sin \theta$, and hence $\xi = ax + b(a)y - t$, with $a = \cos \varphi$ and $b(a) = \sin \varphi$.  In the $(x,y)$-plane using the Cartesian velocities found in Section 3 gives the initial condition
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta(t_0,x,y) &&= 2v \operatorname{sech}^2\left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ ax \pm b(a)y - (1 + \epsilon v)t_0 - x_0 \right] \right), \\
u(t_0,x,y) &&= \cos\varphi ~ \eta(t_0,x,y), \\
v(t_0,x,y) &&= \pm \sin\varphi ~ \eta(t_0,x,y_0),
\end{eqnarray}
where $a$ and $b(a)$ are the wavenumebers of the plane wave calculated from (\ref{gs}) and (\ref{tanphi}), and the upper and lower signs in $\eta$ and the velocity $v$ are for the upper and lower plane waves respectively. When $|y| < x \tan\varphi$ we take $k(\theta)$ to be the singular solution which in the case of surface waves and no shear flow $k(\theta) = 1$, and hence $\xi = r - t$. Therefore, in the $(x,y)$-plane the ring wave initial condition is given as
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta(t_0,x,y) &&= 2v \operatorname{sech}^2\left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} - (1 + \epsilon v)t_0 - x_0 \right] \right), \\
u(t_0,x,y) &&= \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} ~ \eta(t_0,x,y), \\
v(t_0,x,y) &&= \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} ~ \eta(t_0,x,y).
\end{eqnarray}
The initial condition is therefore defined in the entire $(x,y)$-plane such that at the initial moment of time there is a smooth transition between plane and ring regimes along the $\theta$-lines at $\theta = \pm \varphi$.

We modify the initial condition to bring it to zero near the boundaries by multiplying it by the double $\tanh$-functions
\begin{eqnarray}
M_x(x) &&= \frac{1}{2} \left[\tanh \kappa(x - x_{min} - x_{\text{span}}) - \tanh \kappa(x - x_{max} + x_{\text{span}}) \right], \\
M_y(y) &&= \frac{1}{2} \left[\tanh \kappa(y - y_{min} - y_{\text{span}}) - \tanh \kappa(y - y_{max} + y_{\text{span}}) \right],
\end{eqnarray}
such that 
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta(t_0,x,y) &&= \text{min}(M_x,M_y) ~ \eta(t_0,x,y), \\
u(t_0,x,y) &&= \text{min}(M_x,M_y) ~ u(t_0,x,y), \\
v(t_0,x,y) &&= \text{min}(M_x,M_y) ~ v(t_0,x,y),
\end{eqnarray}
to ensure that the problem remains periodic for the pseudospectral solver. Typically we take the parameter values $\kappa = 0.25$, $x_{\text{span}} = (x_{\text{max}} - x_{\text{min}})/20$, and $y_{\text{span}} = (y_{\text{max}} - y_{\text{min}})/20$. Such modification to the amplitude generates swirls behind the waves, described by \citet{YW}, and we must ensure that the domain is large enough such that this behaviour does not spread into the central region of the computation. Doing so for an outward propagating hybrid wave gives figure \ref{Hybrid3DOut} for the computation parameters in table \ref{table_hybrid}.
