The initial condition evolves from a constant amplitude into a wavefront with three distinct sections and a small amount of radiation, not shown here in figure \ref{Hybrid3DOut}. The plane wave legs propagate with constant amplitude retaining their shape and are described by the exact soliton solution of the KdV$\theta$ equation. The ring wave section propagates with radial divergence and providing it is suitably far from the origin it is described numerically by the cKdV equation and concentric KPII equation \citep{STCK}. The third section is the transition region between the legs and the central ring wave and it is not described by the KdV$\theta$, nor cKdV equation, but is described by the KPII equation and potentially the modulation theory of \citet{BHM,RMBH} can be extended to do so too. 

To describe the outward propagating hybrid wave in full using the KPII equation 
\begin{equation}
\left( 2\eta_T + 3\eta\eta_{\xi} + \frac{1}{3} \eta_{\xi\xi\xi} \right)_{\xi} + \eta_{YY} = 0, \label{KP}
\end{equation}
from Section 3, one must rewrite the initial conditions and relevant regions. The plane wave section is defined when $|Y| \geq \sqrt{\epsilon} (\xi + T_0/\epsilon) \tan\varphi$ and is given as
\begin{equation}
\tilde \eta(T_0,\xi,Y) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ a \xi + \frac{b(a)}{\sqrt{\epsilon}}Y - \left(v + \frac{1 - a}{\epsilon} \right) T_0 - \xi_0 \right] \right), \label{KPplane}
\end{equation}
and the ring wave section, defined when $|Y| < \sqrt{\epsilon} (\xi + T_0/\epsilon) \tan\varphi$, and is given as
\begin{equation}
\tilde \eta(T_0,\xi,Y) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ \sqrt{ \left(\xi + \frac{T_0}{\epsilon} \right)^2 + \frac{Y^2}{\epsilon}} - \left( v + \frac{1}{\epsilon} \right) T_0 - \xi_0 \right] \right). \label{KPring}
\end{equation}
Numerically solving the KPII equation has the constraint of having to initialise the system with an initial condition that has zero mass \citep{KSM,BBH}. To abide by this we take the initial condition with mass, $\tilde\eta$, and for each $Y_i$ in $Y$-domain we add a pedestal of the form
\begin{equation}
p_i = \frac{p_0}{2} \left(\tanh \kappa (\xi - \xi_{\text{min}} - \xi_{\text{span}}) - \tanh \kappa (\xi - \xi_{\text{max}} - \xi_{\text{span}}) \right).
\end{equation}
It is then sufficient to calculate the integrals
\begin{eqnarray}
I_i &&= \int_{\xi_{\text{min}}}^{\xi_{\text{max}}} \tilde\eta(T_0,\xi,Y_i) ~ \mathrm{d}\xi,  \\
P_i &&= \int_{\xi_{\text{min}}}^{\xi_{\text{max}}} p_i(\xi) ~ \mathrm{d}\xi,
\end{eqnarray}
solve the equation $I_i = P_i$ for the constant $p_0$, and define the massless initial condition to be
\begin{equation}
\eta(T_0,\xi,Y_i) = \tilde\eta(T_0,\xi,Y_i) - p_i(\xi),
\end{equation}
where we usually take $\kappa = 0.5$, $\xi_{\text{span}} = (\xi_{\text{max}} - \xi_{\text{min}})/20$. In practice due to the length of the domain this leads to a pedestal of amplitude less than $\epsilon$ and therefore the difference in numerical solution to say that of the 2D Boussinesq equations is negligible. Further discussion of generating numerical solutions to the KP equation is given in Appendix \ref{appA}.

As the outward propagating hybrid wave moves away from the origin the effects of radial divergence dissipate and the wavefront propagates in a quasi-stationary manner. The description of the wavefront can therefore be given analytically up to the level of asymptotics using the adiabatic approximation. The plane wave legs are simply the obliquely propagating line soliton solutions but the central ring wave section can be given analytically by the asymptotic solution of \citet{J99}. Johnson's asymptotics is given for the initial value problem
\begin{eqnarray}
&& 2 \eta_T + 3\eta\eta_{\xi} + \frac{1}{3} \eta_{\xi\xi\xi} + \frac{\eta}{T} = 0, \\
&& \eta\left(T_0,\xi \right) = A \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{\sqrt{3A}}{2} \xi \right),
\end{eqnarray}
which is posed  sufficiently far away from the origin, and then the radial divergence term can be viewed as a perturbation of the KdV equation. Although originally developed for $(R,\xi)$-variables the change of variables to $(T,\xi)$-space has no effect to leading order. The three components of the wave are the primary wave, the shelf and the transition region back to an undisturbed medium. The variables
\begin{gather}
\sigma = T_0^{-1}, \quad X = \sigma T, \quad \mathcal{T} = \frac{1}{2}\sqrt{3AX^{-\frac{2}{3}}}, \quad f(X) = \frac{3}{2}A\left(X^{\frac{1}{3}} - 1 \right), \\
\Theta = \xi - \sigma^{-1} f(X), \quad \bar{s} = \operatorname{sech}(\mathcal{T}\Theta), \quad \bar{t} = \tanh(\mathcal{T} \Theta),
\end{gather}
are required to construct the three components. The primary wave is given as
\begin{equation}
\eta_{\text{primary}} = \eta_1 + \sigma \eta_2,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta_1 &&= AX^{-\frac{2}{3}}\bar{s}^2, \\
\eta_2 &&= \frac{2}{3}\frac{X^{-\frac{2}{3}}}{\sqrt{3A}} \left[-1 + \bar{t} + (3 + 2\mathcal{T}\Theta)\bar{s}^2 - \left(\frac{35}{12} + 3\mathcal{T}\Theta + \mathcal{T}^2\Theta^2 \right)\bar{t}\bar{s}^2 \right].
\end{eqnarray}
The shelf has the form
\begin{equation}
\eta_{\text{shelf}} = \eta_1 + \sigma F_1,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
F_1 = - \frac{4}{3} \left(3A + 2 \sigma \xi \right)^{-\frac{1}{2}},
\end{equation}
and finally, the transition region is given in the form
\begin{equation}
\eta_{\text{transition}} = \eta_1 + \sigma F_2,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
F_2 = - \frac{4}{3} \left(3A \right)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \left(1 - \int_{\xi (2\sigma/X)^{1/3}}^{\infty} \Ai(\xi') ~ \mathrm{d}\xi' \right),
\end{equation}
and $\Ai$ is the Airy function. The three components are therefore used to construct the entire wave in $(T,\xi)$-space for suitably small $\sigma$ and hence suitably large $T_0$. For accurate asymptotics we therefore require large $T$ values, interpreting this as a wave evolves and at the initial time, $T_0$, the wavefront takes the form of the initial condition. We can therefore describe analytically the three components of the outward propagating hybrid wave. 

We now model the inward propagating hybrid wave. The wave is generated by the same set of initial conditions in the same regions, however, the velocities are multiplied by minus one to reverse the direction of propagation, and similarly brought to zero near the boundaries. Solving the 2D Boussinesq equations for this initial data gives figures \ref{Hybrid3DIn}, \ref{L_infty_norm} and \ref{hybrid_y0}.
