	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig11}}
	\caption{3D plot of an inward propagating hybrid wave in the 2D Boussinesq regime for the parameter values $\epsilon = 0.01$, $v = 0.5$, $\varphi = \pm \pi/4$, and $x_0 = 100$ at the times  $t = 0, 50, 100$, and $150$.}
	\label{Hybrid3DIn}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}
	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig12}}
	\caption{The $L^\infty$ norm of $\eta$ from $t=0$ to $t = 150$ for the inward propagating hybrid wave with $\epsilon = 0.01$ and $\varphi = \pi/4$ where $\Delta x = \Delta y = 0.35$ (black),  $\Delta x = \Delta y = 0.275$ (blue),  $\Delta x = \Delta y = 0.2$ (green), and  $\Delta x = \Delta y = 0.125$ (magenta).}
	\label{L_infty_norm}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}
	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig13}}
	\caption{Cross-section plot along $x = 0$ of the domain plotted at 5 times from $t = 0$ to $t = 200$.}
	\label{hybrid_y0}
\end{figure}

The intermediate evolution of the wavefront is dominated by the ring wave section focusing in on itself, increasing the amplitude dramatically as shown by the $L_{\infty}$ norm in figure \ref{L_infty_norm}. However, this is not a finite time blow up of the numerical solution, since as the resolution of the mesh is increased the amplitude is certainly converging to one and the same value for each $t$ value. The converging wavefront generates a very large rogue wave, which then disintegrates into three pieces and eventually generates a stable ``X-type" formation similar to that observed within the scope of the KPII equation in figure 1 of \citet{AB}, and studied in \citet{CK09,CK,CLM}, plus an arc of the opposite curvature of the initial condition connecting two lump-like structures on the legs behind the \textit{X}. Previous studies have focused on ``V-type" initial data for the KPII equation rather than our hybrid wave formation. These studies have shown similar ``X-type" formations obtained numerically without a noticeable phase shift, for example see \citet{CK09,RMBH,RHB}. A weaker arc of opposite curvature to the initial condition connecting the two legs is observed  by \citet{RMBH,YGJW} in the KPII equation and from the cross-section in figure \ref{hybrid_y0} seems to represent the plane wave derivative. To the best of our knowledge, the overall behaviour of the inward propagating ring wave, including the generation of the large rogue wave and its disintegration into the three pieces, has not been reported in the previous studies. Although the KPII equation does not support the exact lump solutions, unlike the KPI equation, our study shows that lumps can exist as transient states in the evolution of curved solitons, contributing to the mechanisms of generation of rogue waves (see, for example, \citet{KPS2008, ORBMA2013, SP2016, HC2025} and references therein).

To construct the ``X-type'' interaction observed from the evolution of the inward propagating ring using the KPII equation one should take its two-soliton solution given by
\begin{equation}
\eta = \frac{4}{3} \frac{\partial^2}{\partial\xi^2} \log \left( F_2 \right),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{eqnarray}
F_2 &&= 1 + e^{\eta_1} + e^{\eta_2} + e^{\eta_1 + \eta_2 + A_{12}}, \\
\eta_i &&= 6k_i \left( \xi + 6P_iY - 6\left( k_i^2 + 3 P_i^2 \right) T \right) + c_i, \quad i = 1,2, \\
e^{A_{12}} &&= \frac{(k_1 - k_2)^2 - (P_1 - P_2)^2}{(k_1 + k_2)^2 - (P_1 - P_2)^2},
\end{eqnarray}
such that $k_i$ and $P_i$ are constants determining the propagation angle and $c_i$ is an arbitrary constant to move the wavefront \citep{AB}. To reconstruct the ``X-type'' formation seen in figure \ref{Hybrid3DIn} such that the crossing angle is $\pi/4$ one should take the parameter values $6k_1 = 6k_2 = 1.1314$, $6P_1 = -6P_2 = \epsilon^{-1/2}$ and $c_1 = c_2 = 71.75k_1$. The value of $e^{A_{12}}$ is indicative of the behaviour of the wavefront where the two solitons cross. For the case seen here $e^{A_{12}} = 1.013$, implying an ``X-type'' interaction with a very short stem between the legs, as seen from evolved state of the inward propagating hybrid wavefront. Indeed, there is no observable phase shift and the comparison between the KPII two-soliton solution and the inward propagating hybrid wave is given in figure \ref{KP_X_comp}. The amplitude of the two-soliton cross is dependent on propagation angle and a discussion on how to obtain the maximum of the cross is discussed by \citet{CLM}.
\begin{figure}
	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig14}}
	\caption{Contour plots of \textit{(a)} the inward propagating hybrid wave at $t = 150$ from figure \ref{Hybrid3DIn} and \textit{(b)} the parameter-matched two-soliton solution of the KP equation.}
	\label{KP_X_comp}
\end{figure}

To describe the entire process of the inward propagating hybrid wave seen in figure \ref{Hybrid3DIn} with the KPII equation one must first reverse the direction of propagation such that the hybrid wave becomes left propagating and propagates in on itself. Therefore, for $T$ reducing, one should map $t \rightarrow -t$ and hence $\partial/\partial T = - \partial/\partial T$  and $\xi \rightarrow \zeta = x + t$, hence the inward propagating KP equation
\begin{equation}
\left( 2\eta_T - 3\eta\eta_{\zeta} - \frac{1}{3} \eta_{\zeta\zeta\zeta} \right)_{\zeta} - \eta_{YY} = 0, \label{KPin}
\end{equation}
is obtained. The initial condition is defined when $|Y| \geq \sqrt{\epsilon} (\zeta - T_0/\epsilon) \tan\varphi$ and is given as
\begin{equation}
\tilde \eta(T_0,\zeta,Y) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ a \zeta + \frac{b(a)}{\sqrt{\epsilon}}Y - \left(v + \frac{1 + a}{\epsilon} \right) T_0  - \xi_0 \right] \right), \label{KPplanein}
\end{equation}
and the ring wave section, defined when $|Y| < \sqrt{\epsilon} (\zeta - T_0/\epsilon) \tan\varphi$, and is given as
\begin{equation}
\tilde \eta(T_0,\zeta,Y) = 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ \sqrt{ \left(\zeta - \frac{T_0}{\epsilon} \right)^2 + \frac{Y^2}{\epsilon}} - \left( v + \frac{1}{\epsilon} \right) T_0 - \xi_0 \right] \right), \label{KPringin}
\end{equation}
where again before solving the mass of the solution should be removed to satisfy the zero-mass constraint of the KPII equation as before such that $\eta = \tilde \eta - p(\xi)$ for the pedestal $p(\xi)$. Solving (\ref{KP}) and (\ref{KPin}) with the initial data of (\ref{KPplane}), (\ref{KPring}), and (\ref{KPplanein}), (\ref{KPringin}), respectively, for the parameter values given to match figures \ref{Hybrid3DOut} and \ref{Hybrid3DIn}, is shown in figure \ref{KP_hybrid_in}.
\begin{figure}
	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig15}}
	\caption{3D plots of the numerical solution to the KP equation for both the outward and inward propagating hybrid waves solved for $T \in [0,1.5]$, $\epsilon = 0.01$ and $v=0.5$ where for \textit{(a)} $\varphi = \pi/8$ and $\xi_0 = 150$, and for \textit{(b)} $\varphi = \pi/4$ and $\xi_0 = 100$.}
	\label{KP_hybrid_in}
\end{figure}
The computations in figure \ref{KP_hybrid_in} gives all of the same structures as in the parent system of the 2D Boussinesq system, to within tolerances of the reduced model. Notably the transient lump-like structures on the end of the legs are present, as in figure \ref{Hybrid3DIn} despite the KPII equation for weak surface tension used here not possessing lump soliton solutions. The transient lumps are constantly decreasing in amplitude and converting their energy to extend the legs behind the \textit{X}. We also note that some radiation has escaped and re-entered the periodic domain of the numerical solver which is the cause of some small defects, however, inclusion of a sponge layer would remove these artefacts.

We note that the $2+1$-dimensional cKdV-type equation (\ref{A}) can be used to describe the propagation of hybrid waves on a current. When there is no shear flow, to leading order,  the line solitons are tangent to the ring wave at their propagation angle. The angle at which the waves are matched in the presence of stratification and shear flow  is given in  \citet{HKG}. 










