\section{Discussion}

In this paper we investigated the evolution of perturbed long plane, ring and hybrid waves using both the relevant reduced models \citep{J90, J99, KZ, HKG, TABK} and full numerical simulations of the parent Boussinesq-Peregrine system \citep{P}. We showed that the corresponding $2+1$-dimensional cKdV-type amplitude equation correctly reduces to the known amplitude equations for plane waves in a stratified fluid on a parallel shear flow.  We then explored the role of the general and singular solutions of the generalised Burns condition, derived in the context of the surface ring waves.  We  showed that the general solution 
leads to a new amplitude equation, the {KdV$\theta$} equation
\begin{equation}
\mu_1 A_T + \mu_2 AA_{\xi} + \mu_3 A_{\xi\xi\xi} + \mu_5 \frac{A_{\theta}}{T} = 0,
\end{equation}
which can accurately describe the intermediate propagation of plane waves with sufficiently long, yet arbitrarily strong transverse amplitude perturbations. This was verified numerically using comparisons to the solution of the parent 2D Boussinesq-Peregrine equations, where after sufficient time  the perturbation splits and travels sideways along the wavefront in the 2D Boussinesq solution. Up to this point the KdV$\theta$ equation is a simpler model to solve than the KPII equation and accurately describes the behaviour of solutions. Moreover,  the KdV$\theta$ equation has been mapped to the KdV equation with parametric dependence on the polar angle, allowing us to obtain analytical results for the long-time asymptotics of perturbed line solitons using the Inverse Scattering Transform \citep{GGKM}.

We then applied strong transverse amplitude perturbations to ring waves in the scope of the 2D Boussinesq-Peregirne equations. It was shown that stability depends on the width of the perturbation. When the width of the perturbation is of the order of the wavelength then the perturbation quickly becomes unstable, splitting and travelling sideways along the wavefront for the inward and outward propagating case. However, when the width of the perturbation is comparable to the radius of the ring for the outward propagating case the perturbation slowly reduces in amplitude and in the long time solution approaches an outward propagating ring wave. When the ring wave is instead inward propagating the perturbation grows as the wave reaches the origin but does not become unstable, and instead the perturbation passes through the origin and generates an outward propagating perturbed ring wave. For narrower perturbation, once the wave has passed through the origin the perturbation begins to destruct the wavefront resulting in an unstable outward propagating ring wave. Interestingly periodic perturbations to the ring wave are dependent upon the frequency of the perturbation, with high frequency perturbations not growing compared to the wavefront and low frequency perturbations growing. This is numerical confirmation of the predictions made by \citet{OS,SP}. 

The singular solution of the generalised Burns condition leads to the cKdV-type equation \cite{J90, KZ}:
\begin{align*}
\tilde \mu_1 A_T + \tilde \mu_2 AA_{\xi} + \tilde \mu_3 A_{\xi\xi\xi} + \tilde \mu_4 \frac{A}{T} = 0.
\end{align*}
Generally, the $2+1$-dimensional cKdV-type equation
\begin{align*}
\mu_1 A_T + \mu_2 AA_{\xi} + \mu_3 A_{\xi\xi\xi} + \mu_4 \frac{A}{T} + \mu_5 \frac{A_{\theta}}{T} = 0,
\end{align*}
 has a duality of the solutions, depending upon the choice of singular or general solution of the equation for the function $k(\theta)$. This property was behind the kinematic construction of the general hybrid wavefronts on a parallel shear flow in \citep{HKG}. Here, we confirmed the stable propagation of the hybrid solitary waves in the setting of surface waves with no background shear flow. The 2D Boussinesq-Peregrine equations have been used as the parent system. We have shown, that the evolution of perturbed ring and hybrid waves is strongly dependent upon the direction of propagation (outwards or inwards), and explored in detail how the features observed in these numerical simulations can be described using the reduced amplitude equations, both for plane and ring waves. It would  be interesting to extend this work to the case of stratified fluids with a shear flow.










