\appendix
\section{Numerical Method}\label{appA}

In this section we discuss the numerical solutions of the KdV$\theta$, KP, and 2D Boussinesq equations. Numerical solutions are calculated using an efficient pseudospectral method to approximate the spacial derivatives and the temporal derivatives are calculated via a Runge-Kutta $4^{\text{th}}$-order scheme for its preferable accuracy.

To use this periodic pseudospectral method we employ the inbuilt MATLAB functions for the 2D discrete Fourier transform to approximate the spacial derivatives. For a given discretised field $\eta$ the derivatives are approximated as
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial^n \eta}{\partial x^n} &&= \mathcal{F}^{-1}\left[ (ik_x)^n \mathcal{F}[\eta] \right], \\
\frac{\partial^n \eta}{\partial y^n} &&= \mathcal{F}^{-1}\left[ (ik_y)^n \mathcal{F}[\eta] \right],
\end{eqnarray}
where $k_x$ and $k_y$ are the wavenumbers, $\mathcal{F}$ is the 2D discrete Fourier transform and $\mathcal{F}^{-1}$ is the 2D inverse discrete Fourier transform. The wavenumbers for computation are matrices of size $N_y \times N_x$ where the rows of $k_x$ are given by
\begin{equation}
0,1,\cdots, \frac{N_x}{2}, -\frac{N_x}{2}+1, -\frac{N_x}{2}+2, \cdots, -1,
\end{equation}
and the columns of $k_y$ by
\begin{equation}
0,1,\cdots, \frac{N_y}{2}, -\frac{N_y}{2}+1, -\frac{N_y}{2}+2, \cdots, -1
\end{equation}
\citep{T}. The Fourier transform of a quantity is denoted below as $\mathcal{F}[\eta] = \hat\eta$. We solve in a arbitrary rectangular domain so we scale the equation from the domain $[-\pi,\pi] \times [-\pi,\pi]$ to $[x_{\text{min}},x_{\text{max}}] \times [y_{\text{min}},y_{\text{max}}]$, or $\xi$ and $\theta$ for the KdV$\theta$ equation, and $\xi$ and $Y$ for the KP equation, respectively. However, we stick to the notation of $x$ and $y$ for simplicity but of course everything can be rewritten in $(T,\xi,Y)$ or $(T,\xi,\theta)$. Performing this scaling has the effect of scaling $k_x$ and $k_y$ wavenumbers by $2\pi/(x_{\text{max}}-x_{\text{min}})$ and $2\pi/(y_{\text{max}}-y_{\text{min}})$, respectively. The domains are therefore given by
\begin{eqnarray}
x_i &&= x_{\text{min}} + n \Delta x, \quad i = 0,1, \cdots, N_x-1, \\
y_j &&= y_{\text{min}} + m \Delta y, \quad j = 0,1, \cdots, N_y-1,
\end{eqnarray}
such that $\eta$, $u$ and $v$ are also discretised into $N_y \times N_x$ grids.

The temporal discretisation used is simply $\Delta t = (t_{\text{max}}-t_{\text{min}})/N_t$ such that $t_n = t_{\text{min}} + n \Delta t$, for $n = 0,1,\cdots, N_t$, and is computed via the Runge-Kutta $4^{\text{th}}$-order scheme such that for a problem in the form
\begin{align*}
\eta_t = f(t,x,y),
\end{align*}
the next time step, $\eta^{n+1}$, is given as
\begin{align*}
\eta^{n+1} = \eta^n + \frac{\Delta t}{6} \left( k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4 \right),
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
k_1 &= f\left( t_n, x_n, y_n \right), \\
k_2 &= f\left( t_n + \frac{\Delta t}{2}, x_n + \frac{k_1 \Delta t}{2}, y_n + \frac{k_1 \Delta t}{2} \right), \\
k_3 &= f\left( t_n + \frac{\Delta t}{2}, x_n + \frac{k_2 \Delta t}{2}, y_n + \frac{k_2 \Delta t}{2} \right), \\
k_4 &= f\left( t_n + \Delta t, x_n + k_3 \Delta t, y_n + k_3 \Delta t \right).
\end{align*}
We choose this time-stepping scheme for its preferable local truncation error of $O(\Delta t^5)$ and simple implementation.


The 2D Boussinesq equations are then solved using the method presented by \citet{ES, SSL} to deal with the two separate time derivatives in (\ref{Bouss2}). This is done by introducing a new variable $q$ such that $q = \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_t$. Upon taking the divergence of (\ref{Bouss2}) a third coupled equation is generated and gives the new system of equations with an additional auxiliary equation as
\begin{eqnarray}
\eta_t + \nabla \cdot \{(1 + \epsilon \eta) \mathbf{u} \} &&= 0, \label{NBouss1}\\
\mathbf{u}_t + \epsilon (\mathbf{u} \cdot \nabla ) \mathbf{u} + \nabla \eta - \frac{\delta^2}{3} \nabla q &&= \mathbf{0}, \label{NBouss2} \\
\frac{\delta^2}{3} \nabla \cdot (\nabla q) - q &&= \nabla \cdot \left[\epsilon (\mathbf{u} \cdot \nabla) \mathbf{u} + \nabla \eta \right]. \label{qeqn}
\end{eqnarray}
At each time step the auxiliary equation (\ref{qeqn}) is factorised into the form $Lq = d$, which in Fourier space with flat bottom topography is efficiently inverted to calculate $q$ \citep{SSL} and is then used in the simultaneous time stepping of (\ref{NBouss1}) and (\ref{NBouss2}). The system (\ref{NBouss1})-(\ref{qeqn}) is therefore rewritten in Fourier space as
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat\eta_t &&= - ik_x (1+\epsilon \hat\eta)\hat u - ik_y (1+\epsilon \hat\eta)\hat v, \label{bouss1a} \\
\hat u_t   &&= - i\epsilon (k_x \hat u^2 + k_y \hat v \hat u ) - ik_x \hat\eta + \frac{ik_x \delta^2}{3} \hat q, \label{bouss2a} \\
\hat v_t   &&= - i\epsilon (k_x \hat u \hat v + k_y \hat v^2) - ik_y \hat\eta + \frac{ik_y \delta^2}{3} \hat q, \label{bouss3a} \\
\hat q &&= L_q^{-1} \left( k_x \left[\epsilon (k_x \hat u^2 + k_y \hat v \hat u ) + k_x \hat\eta \right] + k_y \left[ \epsilon (k_x \hat u \hat v + k_y \hat v^2) + k_y \hat\eta \right] \right), \label{qa}
\end{eqnarray}
where $L_q^{-1}$ is the Hadamard inverse of $L_q$ when
\begin{equation}
L_q = \left[\frac{\delta^2}{3} (k_x^2 + k_y^2) + 1 \right].
\end{equation}
The numerical solution is then calculated by iterating (\ref{bouss1a})-(\ref{bouss3a}) in time using the RK4 algorithm and at each time step evaluating (\ref{qa}).

The KdV$\theta$ equation has the form
\begin{equation}
\mu_1 A_T + \mu_2 AA_{\xi} + \mu_3 A_{\xi\xi\xi} + \mu_5 \frac{A_{\theta}}{T} = 0,
\end{equation}
from which we apply the 2D Discrete Fourier transform giving
\begin{equation}
\hat A_T = - \frac{ik_{\xi} \mu_2}{2 \mu_1} \mathcal{F}\left[ A^2 \right] + \frac{ik_{\xi}^3 \mu_3}{\mu_1} \hat A - \frac{ik_{\theta} \mu_5}{\mu_1} \frac{\hat A}{T},
\end{equation}
which is then efficiently iterated in time using the RK4 algorithm above. One must choose the values of $T$ to not include zero due to the division by zero this introduces, similar to the asymptote at the origin for $R = 0$ for the radial case.

To discretise and numerically solve the KP equation we follow the method presented by \citet{KSM,BBH}. To account for both the inward and outward propagating versions we rewrite the KP equation as
\begin{equation}
\left( 2\eta_T + \frac{3\sigma}{2} \left(\eta^2 \right)_{\xi} + \frac{\sigma}{3}\eta_{\xi\xi\xi}\right)_{\xi} + \sigma \eta_{YY} = 0, \label{appKP}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma = \pm 1$ for the upper sign being outward propagation, the lower sign being inward propagation, and therefore $\xi = x - \sigma t$. Applying the 2D Fourier transform to (\ref{appKP}) and rearranging gives
\begin{equation}
\hat\eta_T = - \frac{3\sigma ik_{\xi}}{4} \mathcal{F}\left[\eta^2 \right] + \frac{\sigma ik_{\xi}^3}{6} \hat\eta - \frac{\sigma ik_Y^2}{2k_{\xi}} \hat\eta. \label{discKP}
\end{equation}
It is noted that the form of (\ref{discKP}) includes division by zero for the zero-wavenumber in $k_{\xi}$, however, this relates to the zero mass condition of the KP equation. If the zero mass condition is satisfied at $T_0$ then there is no growth in the zero mode for all $T^n$, and so it is satisfactory to remove the mass from the initial condition and at each time step set $\hat \eta (T^n,0,k_Y) = 0$, see \citet{KSM,BBH} and the references therein for details. Due to the form (\ref{discKP}) it is convenient to introduce the integrating factor
\begin{equation}
\Lambda = \exp \left(-\sigma i \left( \frac{k_{\xi}^3}{6} - \frac{k_Y^2}{2k_{\xi}} \right)T \right), \label{intfac}
\end{equation}
such that $\hat q = \Lambda \hat\eta$. Substituting this expression for $\hat \eta$ into (\ref{discKP}) removes the stiff terms from the problem and we obtain
\begin{equation}
\hat q_T = - \frac{3\sigma ik_{\xi}}{4 \Lambda} \mathcal{F}\left[ \eta^2 \right],
\end{equation}
which is a simple ODE for $\hat q$ which we iterate in time using RK4 time stepping. At each time step we calculate $\eta^n$ from $\hat q^n = \Lambda \hat\eta^n$ and then calculate $\hat q^{n+1}$. To save the data at a specific time we invert $\hat q^n = \Lambda \hat\eta^n$ for $\hat\eta^n
$ and transform back to the real space to obtain $\eta(T^n,\xi,Y)$.

We take the step size for the 2D Boussinesq system to be $\Delta t = 10^{-2}$, for the KdV$\theta$ equation to be $\Delta T = 10^{-5}$, and for the KP equation to be $\Delta T = 10^{-3}$. Introducing an integrating factor for the KdV$\theta$ equation would increase the maximum step size required for stability of the numerical solution but we found solutions to be generated in reasonable time that this was not necessary to explore, however, an integrating factor was required for the KP equation to drastically reduce the computation time which otherwise would have been on par with the time to solve the full 2D Boussinesq equations. Numerically solving these systems for the problems covered here using MATLAB R2024a on a 2023 M2 chip Mac mini took between 12 and 24 hours for the 2D Boussinesq equations, 80 minutes for the KdV$\theta$ equation, and 20 minutes for the KP equation.


Solving the 2D Boussinesq equations for the line soliton initial condition
\begin{eqnarray}
    \eta(0,x,y) &&= 2v \operatorname{sech}^2 \left(\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{6v} \left[ x - x_0 \right] \right), \\
    u(0,x,y) &&= \eta(0,x,y), \\
    v(0,x,y) &&= 0,
\end{eqnarray}
and the computation parameters $v = 0.5$, $x_0 = 10$, $640 \times 256$ points in the $x \times y$ domain $[0,80] \times [-20, 20]$ and $N_t = 5000$ for $t \in [0,50]$ gives figure \ref{soliton_quantities} for varying values of $\epsilon$ from $\epsilon = 0.001$ to $\epsilon = 0.1$
\begin{figure}
	\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig16}}
	\caption{Conservation of mass, momentum and energy for the line soliton initial condition where (\textit{a}) the conservation of the mass for $\epsilon = 0.01$ and (\textit{b}) the log of the time derivative of the momentum (red), and energy (black), against the log of various $\epsilon$ values. The energy has a gradient of $1.025$ and the momentum $2.015$.}
	\label{soliton_quantities}
\end{figure}

For small $\epsilon$ the difference between the 2D Boussinesq solution and the exact soliton solution are negligible. The 2D Boussinesq solution of the line soliton conserves mass of the initial condition to machine double precision as shown in figure \ref{soliton_quantities}(\textit{a}). The figure \ref{soliton_quantities}(\textit{b}) depicts the convergence of momentum and energy as $\epsilon$ decreases. The red and black lines are given by the MATLAB \textit{polyfit} function where the momentum converges with gradient $2$ and hence of \textit{O}$(\epsilon^2)$ and the energy converges with gradient $1$ and hence of \textit{O}$(\epsilon)$.

For all numerical simulations in this publication the mass, momentum and energy are computed for computation parameters such that the quantities listed above are conserved to the precision of the model.


\section{Derivation of conservation laws of the 2D Boussinesq-Peregrine Equations}\label{appB}

We calculate the conserved quantities of the 2D Boussinesq equations in the rectangular domain, D, bounded in the $x$-direction by the lines $x = x_1$ and $x = x_2$, and the $y$-direction bounded by the lines $y = y_1$ and $y = y_2$. We also assume that the boundaries defined by these lines are periodic.

The conservation of mass in the system is found directly from (\ref{Bouss1}). By taking the double integral across the rectangular computational domain in both the $x$ and $y$ directions (\ref{Bouss1}) can be written as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  \eta ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y = - \iint_D  \left[ ((1+\epsilon \eta)u)_x + ((1 + \epsilon \eta)v)_y \right] ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y, \label{mass1}
\end{equation}
where $u$ and $v$ are the Cartesian coordinate velocity components of $\mathbf{u}$ and subscripts refer to partial derivatives with respect to that variable. From Green's theorem (\ref{mass1}) can be rewritten as the contour integral around the rectangular domain such that
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  \eta ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y &&= \oint_C \left[ (1+\epsilon \eta) u ~ 
 \mathrm{d}x - (1 + \epsilon \eta) v ~ \mathrm{d} y \right], \\
 &&= \int_{x_1}^{x_2} (1+\epsilon \eta) v \vert_{y=y_1} ~ \mathrm{d}x + \int_{x_2}^{x_1} (1+\epsilon \eta) v \vert_{y=y_2} ~ \mathrm{d}x \nonumber \\
 &&\quad - \int_{y_1}^{y_2} (1+\epsilon \eta) u \vert_{x=x_2} ~ \mathrm{d}y - \int_{y_2}^{y_1} (1+\epsilon \eta) u \vert_{x=x_1} ~ \mathrm{d}y \\
 &&= 0
\end{eqnarray}
if the domain is taken to be periodic.

The momentum of the system, a vector quantity, is given by the double integral of $(1+\epsilon\eta)\mathbf{u}$. To obtain an expression for this we apply the operator $1 + \frac{\delta^2}{3}\nabla (\nabla \cdot )$ to (\ref{Bouss2}) and multiply by $1 + \epsilon \eta$, which after rearranging gives
\begin{eqnarray}
(1 + \epsilon \eta) u_t &&= -\epsilon(uu_x + vu_y) - (1 + \epsilon \eta) \eta_x + \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xxx} + \eta_{xxy}) + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon\delta^2,\delta^4), \label{momentum1} \\
(1 + \epsilon \eta) v_t &&= -\epsilon(uv_x + vv_y) - (1 + \epsilon \eta) \eta_y + \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xyy} + \eta_{yyy}) + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon\delta^2,\delta^4), \label{momentum2}
\end{eqnarray}
and we also take $\epsilon \mathbf{u}$ multiplied by (\ref{Bouss1}) to give
\begin{eqnarray}
\epsilon u \eta_t &&= - \epsilon(uu_x + uv_y) + O(\epsilon^2), \label{momentum3} \\
\epsilon v \eta_t &&= - \epsilon(vu_x + vv_y) + O(\epsilon^2). \label{momentum4}
\end{eqnarray}
It is noted that in the derivation of the 2D Boussinesq-Peregrine system $\delta^2 = O(\epsilon)$ and so one must keep equivalent orders in the conserved form consistent with those truncated. Taking the sum of (\ref{momentum1}) and (\ref{momentum3}), and (\ref{momentum2}) and (\ref{momentum4}), respectively, and taking the double integral across the domain we can write the result in conservation form as
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  (1+ \epsilon \eta) u ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y &&= \iint_D \biggl( \left[ \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xx} + \eta_{xy}) - \eta - \epsilon \left(u^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) \right]_x \nonumber \\
&&\quad - \epsilon \left[ uv \right]_y \biggr) + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4), \label{momentum5} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  (1+ \epsilon \eta) v ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y &&= \iint_D \biggl( \left[ \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xy} + \eta_{yy}) - \eta - \epsilon \left( v^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) \right]_y \nonumber \\
&&\quad - \epsilon \left[ uv \right]_x \biggr) + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4). \label{momentum6}
\end{eqnarray}
We can then rewrite (\ref{momentum5}) and (\ref{momentum6}) as contour integrals via Green's theorem as
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  (1+ \epsilon \eta) u ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y &&= \oint_C \biggl[ \left\{ \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xx} + \eta_{xy}) - \eta - \epsilon \left(u^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) \right\} ~ \mathrm{d}y \nonumber \\
&&\quad + \epsilon uv ~ \mathrm{d}x \biggr] + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4), \\
&&= \int_{y_1}^{y_2} \left[ \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xx} + \eta_{xy}) - \eta - \epsilon \left(u^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) \right]_{x = x_2} ~ \mathrm{d}y \nonumber \\
&&\quad + \int_{y_2}^{y_1} \left[ \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xx} + \eta_{xy}) - \eta - \epsilon \left(u^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) \right]_{x = x_1} ~ \mathrm{d}y \nonumber \\
&&\quad + \int_{x_1}^{x_2} uv \vert_{y=y_1} ~ \mathrm{d}x + \int_{x_2}^{x_1} uv \vert_{y=y_2} ~ \mathrm{d}x + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4), \\
&&= O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4), \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  (1+ \epsilon \eta) v ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y &&= \oint_C \biggl[ \left\{ \eta + \epsilon \left(v^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) - \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xy} + \eta_{yy}) \right\} ~ \mathrm{d}y \nonumber \\
&&\quad - \epsilon uv ~ \mathrm{d}x \biggr] + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4), \\
&&= \int_{x_1}^{x_2} \left[ \eta + \epsilon \left(v^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) - \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xy} + \eta_{yy}) \right]_{y = y_1} ~ \mathrm{d}x \nonumber \\
&&\quad + \int_{x_2}^{x_1} \left[ \eta + \epsilon \left(v^2 + \frac{1}{2}\eta^2 \right) - \frac{\delta^2}{3} (\eta_{xy} + \eta_{yy}) \right]_{y = y_2} ~ \mathrm{d}x \nonumber \\
&&\quad - \int_{y_1}^{y_2} uv \vert_{x=x_2} ~ \mathrm{d}y - \int_{y_2}^{y_1} uv \vert_{x=x_2} ~ \mathrm{d}y + O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4), \\
&&= O(\epsilon^2, \epsilon \delta^2, \delta^4).
\end{eqnarray}
Hence momentum is conserved in both the $x$- and $y$-directions to the second order of the expansions used to derive the 2D Boussinesq equations.

The energy of the system is given by the double integral of $\frac{1}{2}(u^2 + v^2 + \eta^2)$. It is therefore necessary to multiply (\ref{Bouss1}) by $\eta$ and (\ref{Bouss2}) by $\mathbf{u}$ and combine the three subsequent equations. Taking the time derivative of this gives
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  \frac{1}{2}\left( u^2 + v^2 + \eta^2 \right) ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y = - \iint_D  \left[ \left(u \eta \right)_x + \left(v \eta \right)_y \right] ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y + O(\epsilon,\delta^2),
\end{equation}
which we again rewrite as a contour integral around the rectangular computational domain such that
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \iint_D  \frac{1}{2}\left( u^2 + v^2 + \eta^2 \right) ~ \mathrm{d}x \mathrm{d}y &&= \oint_C \left[ v\eta ~ \mathrm{d}x - u \eta ~ \mathrm{d} y \right] + O(\epsilon,\delta^2), \\
&&= \int_{x_1}^{x_2} v \eta \vert_{y=y_1} ~ \mathrm{d}x + \int_{x_2}^{x_1} v \eta \vert_{y=y_2} ~ \mathrm{d}x \nonumber \\
&&\quad - \int_{y_1}^{y_2} u \eta \vert_{x=x_2} ~ \mathrm{d}y - \int_{y_2}^{y_1} u \eta \vert_{x=x_1} ~ \mathrm{d}y + O(\epsilon,\delta^2) \\
&&= O(\epsilon,\delta^2),
\end{eqnarray}
 and hence the energy is conserved to the first order of the expansions used to derive the 2D Boussinesq equations.



