%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Definitions and foundations}~\label{def}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

All graphs considered in this paper are finite, simple, and undirected.
Let $G$ be a graph.
We let $V(G)$ and $E(G)$ denote the set of vertices and the set of edges of $G$, respectively.
For $x\in V(G)$, let $N_G(x)$, $N_G[x]$ and $d_G(x)$ denote the \emph{open neighborhood}, the \emph{closed neighborhood}, and the \emph{degree} of $x$, respectively; thus $N_G(x)=\{y\in V(G) \mid xy\in E(G)\}$, $N_G[x]=N_G(x)\cup\{x\}$ and $d_G(x)=|N_G(x)|$.
For a subset $X$ of $V(G)$, we let $G[X]$ denote the subgraph of $G$ induced by $X$.
We let $K_n$ denote the complete graph of order $n$
and let $K_{m,n}$ denote the complete bipartite graph consisting of two partite sets of order $m$ and $n$.
A vertex of degree $k$ is said to be a \emph{$k$-vertex},
and a cycle of length $k$ is said to be a \emph{$k$-cycle}.

A \emph{planar graph} is a graph that can be drawn on the plane without edges crossing. 
If such a graph is already drawn on the plane, then the graph is called a \emph{plane graph}.
A \emph{plane triangulation} $G$ is an edge-maximal plane graph,
that is, for any non-adjacent vertices $u,v$, $G + uv$ is non-planar.
An \emph{outerplanar graph} is a planar graph
that can be drawn on the plane such that all vertices appear on the boundary of the infinite face.
Similarly to the plane graph, such a graph is called an \emph{outerplane graph} 
if it is already drawn on the plane as above.
Moreover, an outerplanar (or outerplane) graph is \emph{maximal}
if it is edge-maximal as well as a plane triangulation.

A \emph{minor} of a graph $G$ is a graph that can be obtained from $G$ by removing vertices and removing or contracting edges.
For a graph $H$, a graph $G$ is \emph{$H$-minor free} if no minor of $G$ is isomorphic to $H$.
A  $K_n$-minor free graph $G$ is \emph{maximal} if $G + uv$ is not a $K_n$-minor free graph for any two non-adjacent vertices $u$ and $v$ of $G$.
For an integer $k \ge 1$,
a \emph{$k$-tree} is recursively defined as follows:
A complete graph $K_{k+1}$ is a $k$-tree.
Any graph obtained from a $k$-tree by adding a new vertex $v$ and making it adjacent to exactly $k$ vertices, such that they together $v$ induce $K_{k+1}$, is also a $k$-tree.
It is well known that the maximal $K_4$-minor free graphs are exactly the 2-trees.
Note that a maximal outerplanar graph must be a maximal $K_4$-minor free graph, i.e., a 2-tree.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:treewidth}}\label{sec:proofthm1}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In this section,
we prove the following theorem stronger than Theorem~\ref{thm:treewidth}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{thm}
\label{th:ktree}
Every $k$-tree $G$ has the unique partition of $V(G)$ into $k+1$ independent sets for any integer $k \ge 1$.
Moreover, for any integer $2\leq t \leq k+1$, the union of any $t$ subsets in this partition induces a $(t-1)$-tree.
\end{thm}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\proof{} %of Theorem~\ref{th:ktree}}
Let $G$ be a $k$-tree for an integer $k \ge 1$.
Let $K_{k+1} = G_0, G_1, \dots, G_m = G$
be a sequence of $k$-trees such that 
for each $0 \le i \le m-1$,
$G_{i+1}$ is obtained from $G_i$ 
by adding a new vertex and joining it to exactly $k$ vertices of a $K_k$ in $G_i$.
We first show by induction on $m$ that $G$ has a unique partition of $V(G)$ into $k+1$ independent sets.
When $m=0$, the claim trivially holds. So suppose $m \ge 1$.
Let $v \in V(G) \setminus V(G_{m-1})$ and $S$ be the set of $k$ vertices adjacent to $v$ in $G$.
By the induction hypothesis,
$G_{m-1}$ has the unique partition $U'_1,U'_2,\dots,U'_{k+1}$ of $V(G_{m-1})$ each of which is independent.
Without loss of generality,
we may suppose that $|S \cap U'_i|=1$ for each $1 \le i \le k$
and $S \cap U'_{k+1} = \emptyset$.
Let $U_1,U_2,\dots,U_{k+1}$ be a partition of $V(G)$ each of which is independent.
Similarly to the above, we may suppose that
$S \cap U_i = S \cap U'_i$ for each integer $1 \le i \le k$
and $S \cap U_{k+1} = \emptyset$.
Since $V(G_{m-1})$ is uniquely partitionable,
the partition $U_1,U_2,\dots,U_{k+1}$ is uniquely determined in $V(G) \setminus \{v\}$.
Moreover, since $v$ is adjacent to all vertices in $S$, $v$ must be in $U_{k+1}$.
Thus, the partition $U_1,U_2,\dots,U_{k+1}$ is uniquely determined.

Next, we show that for any integer $2\leq t \leq k+1$, the union of any $t$ subsets in the unique partition induces a $(t-1)$-tree $H$.
Let $U_1,U_2,\dots,U_{k+1}$ be the unique partition of $V(G)$ each of which is independent.
It suffices to prove that $G'=G[V(G) \setminus U_{k+1}]$ is a $(k-1)$-tree.
We consider the process of building $G$,
say $K_{k+1} = G_0, G_1, \dots, G_m = G$.
Let $K_k = G'_0, G'_1, \dots, G'_m = G'$ be the process of building $G'$
obtained from the above process of $G$ by deleting vertices in $U_{k+1}$.
If $G_{i+1}$ is obtained from $G_i$ by adding a vertex $v$ in $U_j$ for $j \in \{1,2,\dots,k\}$,
then $v$ is adjacent to exactly $k-1$ vertices in $G'_{i+1}$ in which they together $v$ form a $K_{k}$.
Otherwise, if $G_{i+1}$ is obtained from $G_i$ by adding a vertex $v$ in $U_{k+1}$,
we have $G'_{i+1} = G'_{i}$.
Thus, every graph generated in the process $K_k = G'_0, G'_1, \dots, G'_m = G'$ is a $(k-1)$-tree.
\qed

It is well known that 
a $k$-tree is an edge-maximal graph with treewidth $k$,
i.e., 
every graph with treewidth $k$ is a subgraph of a $k$-tree.
By Theorem~\ref{th:ktree},
if a graph $G$ has treewidth at most $s$,
$G$ has a partition of $V(G)$ into $s+1$ independent sets $U_1,U_2,\dots,U_{s+1}$
with $|U_1| \ge |U_2| \ge \cdots \ge |U_{s+1}|$
(where some of them are possibly empty).
Every $t+1$ subsets in the partition induces a graph with treewidth at most $t$ by Theorem~\ref{th:ktree}.
Therefore, an induced subgraph $H = G[U_1 \cup U_2 \cup \cdots \cup U_{t+1}]$
has treewidth at most $t$ and its order is at least $\frac{(t+1)|V(G)|}{s+1}$,
and then Theorem~\ref{thm:treewidth} follows.



