\section{Immediate results from control theory} 

The most obvious use of the noise-control mapping is to take concepts, intuitions, and theorems from optimal control theory \cite{Sontag13} and see what they say about stochastic systems in the weak-noise limit. 

%A basic notion is controllability. Colloquially, a system is controllable if it is possible to go from any state to any other by some trajectory, in a finite time. In stochastic physical systems this usually holds, since the noise is only continuous, and usually the state space is connected (it can fail when the control is limited, like parking a car in a tight spot). However, for CRNs in the macroscopic limit we must be careful about the limits $t \to \infty$ and $\Omega \to \infty$. If $\Omega \to \infty$ at finite $t$, then the system is contained in a single basin of attraction and cut off from any others; instead if $t \to \infty$ at finite $\Omega$, then the system can explore all basins. We return to this multistable regime later.

A famous result is the Pontryagin maximum principle (PMP), which states that, for Eqs.(\ref{eq1},\ref{eq2}) and general $L$, the optimal control must have a Hamiltonian form, equivalent to Eq.\eqref{ham} (see Appendix 3), but obtained without path integral manipulations. Importantly, the PMP does not assume that the optimal control is smooth, and in fact it often has a pointwise behavior with discontinuities, particularly when the control is limited to a finite or closed set. Such functions are approximated arbitrarily well by Brownian $\xiv(t)$ integrated over in a standard Langevin approach. This is shown explicitly for the Goddard problem in Appendix 1, whose optimal control is discontinuous. We will see later that non-smooth controls are generic in nonequilibrium physical systems. 

