}
where the constants $\{ \alpha_\gamma \}$ are fixed by a jump process over attractors. The sum $-(\alpha_\gamma+I_{ss}^{(\gamma)}(\nv))$ is interpreted as the log-probability to be in attractor $\gamma$, plus the log-probability to reach $\nv$ from the fixed point in $\gamma$, all divided by $\Omega$. Thus the optimization problem involves both a local component, fixing $I_{ss}^{(\gamma)}$, and a global component, fixing the $\{ \alpha_\gamma \}$. See Fig.\ref{fig_Iss} (top) for an illustration, { where the plotted coordinate $x$ could be one coordinate of many in a high-dimensional space.}

