The structure of this paper is as follows: Section \ref{sec:model} introduces the mathematical model, outlining the characteristics of market participants and the auction's clearing price rule. Section \ref{sec:principalagent} presents the principal-agent framework, with Section \ref{sec:agentprb} detailing the agent's problem, Section \ref{sec:nash} discussing the competition among agents, and Section \ref{sec:principalprb} focusing on the exchange's problem to design optimal optimal rebates and transaction fees policies. Section \ref{sec:neural} applies the Deep BSDE method to solve and visualize the optimal solution.



\section{The auction market model}
\label{sec:model}
\subsection{The auction market}
In this section we define \textit{informally} the auction market we are considering. The mathematical definition is presented in the next section. We consider a financial asset with efficient price $P^*$ traded in a batch auction with a fixed duration $T>0$. In this auction, we consider two types of traders: strategic market makers, choosing their bidding prices and controlling their intensity of arrivals along the auction duration and investors sending market order to buy or sell the asset with a fix volume.

\paragraph{Efficient price}
 The market trades a risky asset with efficient price $P^*$ defined as: $$d P_t^* = \sigma dW_t, \quad t \in [0,T],$$ where $W$ is a Brownian motion and $\sigma$ a fixed constant. We denote $P_T^{cl}$ to be the clearing price of the auction determined by the stock exchange. This notion of efficient price can be seen has the price induced by the Efficient Market Hypothesis (no-arbitrage price), or more realistically the mid price in a limit order book. 
   \vspace{0.5em}

\subsubsection{Strategic trader: auction market makers}
We consider two strategic traders in this auction: Market Maker $p$ and Market Maker $q$ sending orders according to right-continuous and adapted counting processes $N^p$ and $N^q$ with intensity process $\lambda^p$ and $\lambda^q$ respectively. Note that $N^p_t$ and $N^q_t$ denote the total number of orders the traders submit in the auction. For example, if $N^p_t$ has a jump at time $t$, \textit{i.e.} $N^p_t - N^p_{t^-} =1$, then the market maker $p$ submits an order at time $t$. In addition, we assume that a strategic traders do not want the intensity process to deviate too much from some target intensity $\lambda_0$ fixed. This reference can bee seen as a benchmark trading strategy \`a la Almgren and Chriss, that the trader wants to follow. We also assume that these strategic traders chooses continuously their bidding prices, denoted respectively by $P^p_t$ or $P^q_t$ at time $t$. These prices deviates from the efficient price $P^*$ given the signal received by the market makers. We denote by $P^{*,p}$ and $P^{*,q}$ two copies of $P^*$. We define $ dP^i_t= \mu^i_t dt + dP_t^{*,i}$ for $i=p,q$ and we assume that each trader quotes a spread to the efficient price to favor their positions. \vspace{0.5em}


The strategic traders are called ''strategic'' because they control both their arriving intensity $\lambda$ and the spread $\mu$. We call them market makers since they drive the price of the auction through their bidding prices $P^p,P^q$.  \vspace{0.5em}

Let $P^{cl}_T$ be the clearing price of the auction at the clearing time $T$. The quantity a market maker's order would be settled depends on the clearing price $P^{cl}_T$ and is assumed to follow a linear demand/supply function. In other words, the volume sent by each market maker is given by $Q^i(P^{cl}_T)$ where $Q^i(x)=K^i(P^i_t-x)$ for $i=p,q$. If $P^{cl}_T \leq P^i_t$,  we have $Q \geq 0$ which implies that the order is a buy limit order in the auction since its price is above the clearing price.  If $P^{cl}_T > P^i_t$,  we have $Q < 0$ which implies that the order is a sell limit order. At the end of an auction, the total quantity the trader $i$ would be settled with is $\int_0^T K^i(P^i_{t} - P^{cl}_T)dN^i_t = \sum_{0 < t \leq T}  K^i(P^i_{t} - P^{cl}_T)(N^i_t - N^i_{t^{-}})$. \vspace{0.5em}
%Note that the slope $K$ of the supply/demand function for each market maker is independent of $i$. We implicitly assume that both market makers are symmetrical.  

 To differentiate the two strategic traders, we denote their parameters to be $(\alpha^p,N^{p}, P^{p}, P^{*,p}, W^{p})$ and $(\alpha^q,N^{q}, P^{q},P^{*,q},W^{q})$, where $\alpha^p = (\lambda^p,\mu^p)$ and $\alpha^q = (\lambda^q,\mu^q)$ are the controls of the two traders. 


\subsubsection{Spontaneous buyer/seller auctions' investors}
We assume that non-strategic buyers and sellers also take part in this auction. We assume they arrive according to right-continuous and adapted counting processes and submit only market orders with a fixed volume to buy or sell the asset at the end of the auction. We denote $N_t^{a}$ to be the number of buyers arrived up to $t$ seconds and $N_t^{b}$ to be the number of sellers arrived up to $t$ seconds, where 'a' refers to 'ask' and 'b' refers to 'bid'. We assume $N_t^{a}$ and $N_t^{b}$ are independent counting processes with  intensity processes $\lambda^a$ and $\lambda^b$ respectively. To encompass the main empirical facts and realism of auction market, we enforce the following assumptions:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Orders cancellation.} We assume these market participants might cancel their orders before the auction closes. Let $\theta: \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ be a non-increasing function with $\theta(0) \geq 1$ and let $(A_i)_i, (B_i)_i$ be independent and identically distributed uniform random variables in $[0,1]$. The $i-th$ buyer submits an order of size $v_i^a \mathbf 1_{A_i \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)}$ and the $i-th$ seller submits an order of size $v_i^b \mathbf 1_{B_i \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)}$, where the characteristic function represents the cancellation options of these participants and $v^a_i$ and $v^b_i$ are bounded by some positvie constant $v^a$ and $v^b$ respectively. 
\item \textit{Market impact and order flow.}
We assume that the trading behavior of the auction market makers influences the order flow of investors in the auction as follow: the higher the price proposed by the strategic traders, the more sellers and less buyers come to the market; the lower the price proposed by the strategic traders, the more buyers and less sellers come to the market. 
\item \textit{Transaction fees.} Non-strategic traders, on the other hand, incur a fixed transaction fee $d$ per order at the end of the auction, for some $d \geq 0$.
\end{itemize}


To characterize the market impact of the strategic traders and the impact of the fixed incentive $d$ on non-strategic traders, we assume $$\lambda^a_t =\lambda^a(\mu_t,d):= \lambda_0 i^a(\mu^p_t,\mu^q_t)\iota(d)\text{ and }\lambda^b_t =\lambda^b(\mu_t,d):=  \lambda_0 i^b(\mu^p_t,\mu^q_t)\iota(d),$$ for some constant $\lambda_0$, some monotonically non-increasing function $i^a$, non-decreasing function $i^b$ and non-increasing function  $\iota$  with $i^a(0,0) = i^b(0,0) = \iota(0) = 1$. For example, setting $\lambda^a_t = \lambda_0 e^{-c_1\mu^p_t - c_2\mu^q_t} e^{-d} \text{ and }\lambda^b_t = \lambda_0 e^{c_3\mu^p_t + c^4 \mu^q_t}e^{-d},$ for some non-negative constants $c_1$, $c_2$, $c_3$, and $c_4$, would satisfy the requirement. \vspace{0.5em}

To initiate a periodic auction, we assume that there is always a block trade to be executed in the market at time $t=0$. This block trades is submitted at a price $P_0^*$ and will be settled with a quantity $K_0(P_0^* - P_T^{cl})$ at the close of the auction. This setup is to ensure there is always liquidity in an auction market. This assumption models efficiently the mechanism of a `closing auction' at the end of the day, considering all the limit order not executed in the limit order book. 


\subsubsection{Clearing Price Rule}

At the end of the auction $T>0$, the exchange has to determine the price $x$ of the asset to maximize the volume traded. Given $x>0$, the volume of limit orders received is
\[LM_T(x)=\int_0^T K^p(P_s^p-x)dN_s^p+\int_0^T K^q(P_s^q-x)dN_s^q,\]
while the number of auction market orders by  buyer/seller investor  is
\[MO_T=\sum_{i=1}^{N_T^a}v_i^a \mathbf 1_{A_i\leq \theta(T-\tau_i)}- \sum_{j=1}^{N_T^b}v_j^a \mathbf 1_{B_j\leq \theta(T-\tau_j)}. \]
The clearing price is set by the stock exchange to maximize the total number of traded volume for an auction. Denote $P^{cl}$ to be the clearing price of an auction if it ends at time $T$. Given the traded volume of each strategic trader is a linear function of the clearing price, $P^{cl}$ is the equilibrium point of the supply-demand curve of the auction order flow at the clearig time $T$. Similarly to \cite{du2017optimal} and \cite[Section 2.3]{paul2021optimal}, it is given by $LM_T(P^{cl})+MO_T=0$ or equivalently
\[\sum_{i=1}^{N_T^a} v^a_i \mathbf 1_{A_i \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)} - \sum_{j=1}^{N_T^b} v^b_j \mathbf 1_{B_j \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)} + \int_0^T K^p(P^{p}_s- P^{cl} ) dN^{p}_s+\int_0^T K^q(P^{q}_s- P_T^{cl} ) dN^{q}_s + K^0(P_0^* - P^{cl}) = 0.\]

Therefore, and due to the linearity of the supply demand considered, we get
\begin{equation}
    P^{cl}_T = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_T^a} v^a_i \mathbf 1_{A_i \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)} - \sum_{j=1}^{N_T^b} v^b_j \mathbf 1_{B_j \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)} +  \int_0^t K^p P^{p}_s dN^{p}_s +\int_0^T K^q P^{q}_s dN^{q}_s + K^0P_0^*} {K^p N^{p}_T +K^q N^{q}_T + K^0}.
    \label{eq:pclearing}
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{Rebates policy}
The stock exchange reallocates the transaction fees $d$ paid by the investors, buyers and sellers, to auction's market makers offers. These incentives, taking the form of rebates $\xi^p,\xi^q$ offered to the market makers are received at the end of the auction based on the auction market activity. Note that if $\xi^i <0$, it represents a fee paid to the exchange, while $\xi^i >0$ indicates a compensation received from the exchange, indexed on the market characteristics. These rebates are announced at time $0$ and paid at time $T>0$ indexed on the market characteristics.






\subsection{Mathematical framework}

We define $\Omega= \mathcal S \times \mathcal S \times C([0,T],\mathbb R)\times \mathcal S \times C([0,T],\mathbb R) \times \mathcal S \times C([0,T],\mathbb R)$ where $\mathcal S$ is the set of left limit and right continuous non-decreasing function from $[0,T]$ into $\mathbb N$ and $C([0,T],\mathbb R)$ is the set of continuous function from $[0,T]$ into $\mathbb R$. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be the Borel algebra of $\Omega$. The canonical process on $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$ is denoted by $C_t= (C^1_t, C^2_t, C^3_t, C^4_t,C^5_t,C^6_t,C^7_t):= (N_t^a , N_t^b, W_t,N_t^{p},W_t^p, N_t^{q},W_t^q)$. We define the set of $\sigma-$algebra $\mathbb{F} = (\mathcal{F}_t)_t$ to be the natural filtration of $(C_t)_t$. We introduce $\mathbb{P}^{0}$ to be the measure under which $W_t$, $W_t^p$, and $W_t^q$ are independent standard Brownian motion and under which $N_t^{a}$, $N_t^{b}$, $N_t^{p}$ and $N_t^{q}$  are independent Poisson processes with some fixed non-random intensity $\lambda_0>0$. 

\begin{definition}[Auction market]
The space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P^0)$ is called an auction market where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $N^a,N^b$ modeled the arriving of buy and sell orders in the auction with intensity $\lambda_0$ under the market probability $\mathbb P^0$,
    \item $\sigma W$ is the randomness resulting from the efficient price of the traded asset in the auction, for some constant $\sigma>0$,
    \item $N^p,N^q$ denotes the respective order flow sent by Market Maker $p$ and Market Maker $q$ in the auctions, at a rate $\lambda_0$ under $\mathbb P^0$ while $\sigma W^p,\sigma W^q$ represent their respective limit order bidding prices in the auction. 
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}


In order to build a controlled market probability and auction market model, we introduce the following technical result,  adapted from \cite[Theorem 5.3 and Theorem 5.4]{bjork2021point}. 
\begin{theorem}[Change of probability measure and Girsanov's Theorem]\label{thm:com}
\label{thm:girsanoc}
Fix $(\Omega, \mathcal{F},\mathbb{P},\mathbb{F})$ a filtered probability space. We define $N^1$, ..., $N^k$ a sequence of mutually independent optional counting processes with intensities $\lambda^1$, ..., $\lambda^k$ which are non-negative optional processes on this space. Let $W^1$, ..., $W^\ell$ be standard mutually independent Brownian motions and $\phi^1$, ..., $\phi^k$ be predictable processes with $\phi_t^i \geq -1,$ for any $ i = 1, ..., k, \mathbb{P}-a.s.$. Let $\delta^1$, ...,$\delta^\ell$ be $\mathbb F-$adapted processes. 
We assume that 

\begin{equation}\label{novikov}\mathbb E\Big[\exp\Big(\frac12 \int_0^T\sum_{i=1}^\ell |\delta^i_t|^2 dt+\sum_{j=1}^k\int_0^T ((1+\phi_t^j)\ln(1+\phi_t^j) +\phi_t^j)dt\Big) \Big]<\infty.\end{equation}




Then \begin{itemize}
    \item[i.] There exists a unique solution
    $L$ to
 $$dL_t = L_t \sum_{i=1}^\ell \delta_t^i dW_t^i + L_{t^-} \sum_{j=1}^k \phi_t^j (dN_t^j - \lambda_t^j dt), \quad L_0 = 1,$$ with $\mathbb{P}(L_t > 0) =1$, for every $t \in [0,T]$ and such that $L$ is a $\mathbb{P}$-martingale and there exists a probability measure $\mathbb{Q}$ on $\mathcal{F}_T$ defined by $\frac{d\mathbb{Q}}{d\mathbb{P}}|_{\mathcal{F}_T} = L_T$,  and $\frac{d\mathbb{Q}}{d\mathbb{P}}|_{\mathcal{F}_t} = L_t$;
    \item[ii.] Under the probability measure $\mathbb{Q}$, $N^1$, ..., $N^k$ are counting processes with compensators $(\lambda_t^i)(1+\phi^i_t)$, for $i = 1, ..., k$;
    \item[iii.] The processes $\tilde{W}^1$, ..., $\tilde{W}^\ell$ defined by by $d\tilde{W}_t^i =  dW_t^i-\delta_t^i dt$, for $i= 1, ..., \ell$, are standard Brownian motions under $\mathbb Q$.
    \item[iv.] Under the probability measure measure $\mathbb{Q}$, $N^1$, ..., $N^k$ do not have common jumps, \textit{i.e.} $\mathbb{Q}(\Delta N_t^i \Delta N_t^j = 0) = 1$ for every $t$ and every $i \ne j \in \{1, ..., k\}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}



\textit{Proof of i.} This is a direct consequence of \cite[Proposition 2.10]{bjork2021point} or \cite[Theorem 1.36]{oksendal2019stochastic}.


\textit{Proof of ii.} By Definition 4.1 in \cite{bjork2021point}, it is sufficient to show that for every non-negative predictable process $h$ and every $i \in \{1, ..., k\}$, $\mathbb{E}^\mathbb{Q} \int_0^T h_t\lambda_t^i(1+\phi^i_t) ds = \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{Q} \int_0^T h_t dN^i_t $.

We have \begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{Q} \int_0^T h_t dN^i_t  = \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{P} [L_T\int_0^T h_t dN^i_t].
\end{align*}


Define $Z_t : = L_t Y_t$, where $Y_t := \int_0^t h_s dN^i_s$. By Ito's formula, 
\begin{align*}
    Z_t = &Z_0 + \int_0^t Y_s dL_s^c + \sum_{0<s\leq t} (L_sY_s - L_{s^-}Y_{s^-})\\
    = &\int_0^t Y_{s^-} dL_s + \int_0^t L_{s^-} h_s dN^i_s + \sum_{0<s\leq t} (L_{s^-} \sum_{j=1}^k \phi_s^j \Delta N_s^j) (h_s \Delta N^i_s),
\end{align*}

