By part i, we have $\mathbb{E}^\mathbb{P} \Bigr[\int_0^T Y_{s^-} dL_s\Bigr] =0$. By mutually independence of $N^1$, ..., $N^k$, we have $\mathbb{E}^\mathbb{P} [\sum_{0<s\leq t} \Delta N_s^j \Delta N^i_s] = 0 $, for $i \ne j$.


Then \begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{Q} \int_0^T h_t dN^i_t & = \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{P} \Bigr[L_T\int_0^T h_t dN^i_t \Bigr]\\
    & = \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{P} \Bigr[Z_T \Bigr] \\
    & = \mathbb{E}^\mathbb{P} \Bigr[\int_0^T Y_{s^-} dL_s + \int_0^T L_{s^-} h_s dN^i_s + \sum_{0<s\leq t}(L_{s^-} \sum_{j=1}^k \phi_s^j \Delta N_s^j) (h_s \Delta N^i_s)\Bigr] \\
