In our auction model, we are considering two market makers, Market Maker $p$ and Market Maker $q$ controlling their bidding prices through the deviation from the efficient price with control $\mu^p,\mu^q$ and controlled trading intensities $\lambda^p,\lambda^q$ respectively. The following corollary motivates the study. We refer to \cite[Section 2.2]{euch2021optimal} for a similar definition in a limit order book market model. We denotes by $\mathcal A$ the set of control $\alpha=(\alpha^p,\alpha^q)$ where $\alpha^i=(\mu^i,\lambda^i),\; i\in \{p,q\}$, $\mu^i$ is a $B-$ valued $\mathbb F-$predicable process and $\lambda^i$ is an $\Lambda-$ valued $\mathbb F-$predicable process with $B$ a bounded subset of $\mathbb R$ and $\Lambda$ is a bounded subset of $\mathbb R_+$. Consequently, note that $(\mu^p,\lambda^p;\mu^q,\lambda^q)$ satisfy \eqref{novikov} with $\delta^i=\frac{\mu^i}\sigma$ and $\phi^i=\frac{\lambda^i_t - \lambda_0}{\lambda_0}$ for $i=p,q$. We denote by $\mathcal A^i$ the set of $\alpha^i$ as the $i$th component of a control $\alpha\in \mathcal A$ with $i=p,q$. Note that $L^\alpha$ is thus the solution to      \begin{align*}
    dL_t^{\alpha} = L_t^{\alpha}\sum_{i=p,q}\frac{\mu_t^i}{\sigma}dW_t^i +L_{t^-}^{\alpha}\sum_{i=p,q, a, b} \frac{\lambda^i_t - \lambda_0}{\lambda_0} (dN_t^i - \lambda_0 dt),\; L^\alpha_0=1,
\end{align*}
with an explicit solution, see \cite[Theorem 11.6.10]{shreve2004stochastic}, 
\begin{align*}
    L_t^{\alpha} = &\exp \Bigr(\sum_{i=p,q} \int_0^t \Bigr[ -\frac{\mu_s^i}{\sigma}dW^i_s -  \frac{(\mu_s^i)^2}{2\sigma^2}ds \Bigr] +  \sum_{i=a, b,p,q} \int_0^t  \Bigr[ \ln(\frac{\lambda^i_t}{\lambda_0} )dN_s^i +(\lambda_0 - \lambda^i_t)ds\Bigr]  \Bigr), \quad t \in [0,T].
\end{align*}
Moreover and due to the bounded property of $\lambda,\mu$, $L^\alpha$ admits moment of any orders.
\begin{corollary}[Controlled auction market]
Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P^0)$ be an auction market. Let $\alpha\in \mathcal A$. There exists a control market probability $\mathbb P^\alpha$ and a tuple $(N^a,N^b,P^\star,N^p,P^p,N^q,P^q)$ such that   \[dP^*_t = \sigma dW_t, \;dP^p_t = \mu^p_t dt + \sigma d\tilde W^p_t, \text{ and } dP^q_t = \mu^q_t dt + \sigma d\tilde W^q_t,\] where $W$, $\tilde W^p$, $\tilde W^q$ are independent standard Brownian motions under the measure $\mathbb{P}^{\alpha}$;  and $N_t^{a}$, $N_t^{b}$, $N_t^{p}$ and $N_t^{q}$ are counting process with respective intensities  $\lambda^a_t, \lambda^b_t,\lambda^p_t, \lambda^q_t$ respectively, under $\mathbb{P}^{\alpha}$. In addition, $N_t^{a}$, $N_t^{b}$, $N_t^{p}$ and $N_t^{q}$ do not jump together $\mathbb{P}^{\alpha}-a.s.$. The tuple $(N^a,N^b,P^\star,N^p,P^p,N^q,P^q)$ defined by the previous corollary and controlled by $\alpha$ is called a controlled auction market model while $\mathbb P^\alpha$ is the controlled market probability. 
\end{corollary} 


\begin{remark}Integrable spread and bounded trading intensity. 
 Assuming that $\mu$ and $\lambda$ are bounded is stronger that a Novikov condition type to well-define $L^\alpha$ but required to ensure that $L^\alpha$ has moment of any orders for the technical details in the proof below. It is first realistically reasonable to assume $\lambda$ bounded since traders are limited in their speed of execution due to technological constraints. 

Note now that spreads may not be bounded and defined as signal received by the strategic traders, see for example \cite{derchu2023equilibria}. In this case, we should assume for example a BMO property of $L^\alpha$ to ensure that $L^\alpha$ has moment of any order, see \cite[Chapter 3]{kazamaki2006continuous}.
\end{remark}


We denote $\mathbb{E}^0$ to be the expectation with regard to measure $\mathbb{P}^0$ and denote $\mathbb{E}^{\alpha}$ to be the expectation with regard to measure $\mathbb{P}^{\alpha}$.


\section{Rebates design: a contracting Principal-multi Agents problem}
\label{sec:principalagent}

We are considering an exchange (the principal) and a few stock traders divided in two main categories: investors (buyers or sellers) and strategic traders (the agents, also the market makers). The stock exchange proposes an incentive (rebates) to each strategic traders (market maker in this work) and proposes a uniform transaction fee to investors. The exchange aims at designing the optimal rebates scheme and the optimal transaction fee that should be offered to these traders. 


We first introduce the auction's market characteristics as a tuple $X_t = (X^1_t, X^2_t, X^3_t, X^{4}_t,X^{5}_t,X^{6}_t,X^{7}_t)$ where
$X^1_t:= \sum_{i=1}^{N_t^a} v^a_i \mathbf 1_{A_i \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)}$ and $X^2_t:= \sum_{j=1}^{N_t^b} v^b_j \mathbf 1_{B_j \leq \theta(T - \tau_i)},$ denotes the volumes sent by buyers and sellers investors respectively in the auction at time $t$;  $X_t^3:=P_t^{*}$, $X^4_t:= \int_0^t P^p_s dN^p_s$ and $X^6_t:=\int_0^t P^{q}_s dN^{q}_s$ are the respective cumulative bidings sent by Market Maker $p$ and Market Maker $q$ while $X^5_t:=N_t^p$ and $X^7_t:=N_t^q$ is the number of orders sent by Market Maker $p$ and Market Maker $q$. We set $P= (P^p, P^q)$ the price processes sent by both market makers.


\textbf{A rebates} $\xi = (\xi^p,\xi^q)$ is a pair of $\mathcal F_T-$measurable random variable offered to Trader $p$ and Trader $q$. We define the set of \textbf{admissible incentives} by 
\[\mathcal{C}^{R_0} = \{\xi: \mathcal{F}_T- \text{ measurable, } \sup_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A} } \mathbb{E}^{\alpha}[|\xi^p|]< \infty,  \text{and } \sup_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A} } \mathbb{E}^{\alpha}[|\xi^q|] < \infty,\;  V^i_0(\xi^i,d,\alpha^j; \alpha^i)\geq R_0^i, i\neq j\in\{p,q\} \},\]
where  $V^i_0(\xi^i,d,\alpha^j; \alpha^i)$ denotes the value function, as the expected profit and loss of Market Maker $i=p,q$, defined rigorously below and $R_0:=(R_0^p,R_0^q)$ is the vector of reservation utilities of Market Makers $p$ and $q$. We denotes $\mathcal{C}^{R_0}_i$ the set of rebates $\xi^i$ as the $i$th component of a rebate $\xi\in \mathcal C^{R_0}$ for $i=p,q$.




