\subsection{Market makers' Nash equilibrium}
\label{sec:agentprb}

Strategic traders aim to maximize simultaneously and without communication their gains by controlling arrival intensity and proposed price. A strategic trader's gain is composed of: 
\begin{itemize}
\item a payoff $g^i(X_T)$ for $i=p,q$, resulting from the trades in the auctions, 
\item the incentive (rebates) from the exchange;
\item and a penalty term relating to their arriving intensity, recalling that each strategic trader has a target intensity $\lambda_0$ which they shouldn't deviate too much from.
\end{itemize}


For each order $P^i_t$ submitted, the payoff of Market Maker $i$ is $K(P^{cl} - P^i_t)(P^{cl} - P^{*}_T)$, since if $P^{cl}_T \leq P^i_t$, the trader $i$ sells at $P^{cl}$ and buys at $P^{*}_T$ to close the position. Thus, the trader gains $(P^{cl} - P^{*}_T)$ per order and gains $K(P^{cl} - P^i_t)(P^{cl} - P^{*}_T)$ in total. Conversely, if $P^{cl} > P^i_t$, the trader becomes a buyer in the auction market. The trader buys at the clearing price and sells at the efficient price at time $T$, so gains $(P^{*}_T-P^{cl})$ per order and gains $K(P^i_t -P^{cl})(P^{*}_T-P^{cl})$ in total. In both situations, the payoff functions are $K(P^{cl}- P^i_t)(P^{cl} - P^{*}_T)$. Thus, $g^i(X_T)$ would be the trader $i$'s total payoff from trade at the end of an auction and given by
\begin{align*}
\begin{split}
    g^i(X_T) : &= \int_0^T K^i(P^{cl} - P^i_{s})(P^{cl} - P^{*}_T)dN^i_s\\
     &= K^i(P^{cl} - P^{*}_T)(P^{cl}N^i_T -  \int_0^T P^i_{s}dN^i_s),
\end{split}
\end{align*}

where
\begin{align*}
    g^p(X_T)  =K^p(\frac{X_T^1 - X_T^2 + K^pX_T^4 + K^{q}X_T^6 +K^0P_0^*}{K^pX_T^5+K^{q}X_T^7 + K^0} - X_T^3)(X_T^5\frac{X_T^1 - X_T^2 + K^pX_T^4 + K^{q}X_T^6 +K^0P_0^*}{K^pX_T^5+K^{q}X_T^7 + K^0} - X_T^4),
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\begin{split}
    g^q(X_T) =K^q(\frac{X_T^1 - X_T^2 + K^pX_T^4 + K^{q}X_T^6 +K^0P_0^*}{K^pX_T^5+K^{q}X_T^7 + K^0} - X_T^3)(X_T^7\frac{X_T^1 - X_T^2 + K^pX_T^4 + K^{q}X_T^6 +K^0P_0^*}{K^pX_T^5+K^{q}X_T^7 + K^0} - X_T^6).
\end{split}
\end{align*}







Assume that Market Maker $i=p,q$ chooses the control $\alpha^i=(\lambda^i,\mu^i)$ while their opponent Market Maker $j\neq i$ is choosing $\alpha^j=(\lambda^j,\mu^j)$ and assume that the stock exchange proposes rebates $\xi=(\xi^p,\xi^q)$ and transaction fees scheme $d$. The value function of Market Maker $i$
 is then given by 
 \[ V^i_0(\xi^i,d,\alpha^j; \alpha^i):=  \mathbb{E}^{\alpha} \left[g^i(X_T) + \xi^i - \int_0^T \frac{(\lambda^i_s - \lambda_0 )^2}{2} ds \right]. \]
\begin{remark}
Note that $d$ affects the processes $N^a,N^b$ and so the components $X^1,X^2$ and the clearing price. Therefore, it affects the value functions of the Market Makers while they do not pay $d$ directly unlike the investors.
\end{remark}
 Given fix rebates $\xi=(\xi^p,\xi^q)$ and transaction fees scheme $d$, the Market Makers' problem is to find simultaneously a control $\alpha^\star=(\alpha^{p,\star},\alpha^{q,\star})$ such that

\[NE(\xi,d)
\begin{cases}
V^{p}_0(\xi^p,d,\alpha^{q,\star};\alpha^{p,\star}): &= \sup_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A},\; \alpha=(\alpha^p,\alpha^{q,\star})} V^p_0(\xi^p,d,\alpha^{q,\star}; \alpha^p)\\
V^{q}_0(\xi^q,d,\alpha^{p,\star};\alpha^{q,\star}): &= \sup_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A},\; \alpha=(\alpha^{p,\star},\alpha^{q})} V^q_0(\xi^q,d,\alpha^{p,\star}; \alpha^q).

\end{cases}\]

Such $\alpha^\star$ satisfying $NE(\xi,d)$ defines a Nash Equilibrium composed with spread $\mu^p,\mu^q$ and trading intensities $\lambda^p,\lambda^q$ under rebates and transaction fees scheme.




\subsubsection{Best response of a representative Market Maker} To give a characterization of the Nash equilibrium $NE(\xi,d)$ under an agreement composed with rebates and transaction fees scheme, we start to study the behavior of one strategic trader, Market Maker $p$ for example when $\alpha^q$ is fixed and known, that is to find $\alpha^{p,\star}$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:traderproblem}V^{p}_0(\xi^p,d,\alpha^{q};\alpha^{p,\star})= \sup_{\alpha^p \in \mathcal{A}^p} V^p_0(\xi^p,d,\alpha^{q}; \alpha^p)= \sup_{\alpha^p \text{ s.t.} ( \alpha^p,\alpha^q)\in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}^{\alpha} \left[g^p(X_T) + \xi^p - \int_0^T \frac{(\lambda^p_s - \lambda_0 )^2}{2} ds \right].
\end{equation}




We consider the following set of incentive compatible contracts for Market Maker $p$ when $\alpha^q$ is fixed by the other Market Maker. 




\[\Xi^p:=\{Y_T^{p,Y^p_0,Z^p,d}, Z^p\in \mathcal Z^p, Y_0^p\geq R_0^p\},\]
where the process $Y_t^{p,Y^p_0, Z^p,d}$ is defined by:
