    Notice that $V_T(\xi^p,\alpha^{q}, \alpha^p,d) =\xi^p$ and $V_0(\xi^p,\alpha^{q}, \alpha^p,d)= \mathbb{E}^{\alpha} \Bigr[g^p(X_T) + \xi^p -\int_0^T \frac{(\lambda^p_s - \lambda_0 )^2}{2} ds \Bigr]$ since $g(X_0)=0$.

    We want to show that $V_t$ is well-defined. As $\xi^p \in L^1(\mathbb{P}^{\alpha})$ and $\lambda^p$ is bounded by $\lambda_{\infty}$, the proof is reduced to show that $g^p(X_t) \in  L^1(\mathbb{P}^{\alpha})$, for every $t \in [0,T]$.


    %Define $W_{max}: = \max_{t \in [0,T]} W_t$ and $W_{min}: = \min_{t \in [0,T]} W_t$. Since $W_0 = 0$, $W_{max} \geq 0$ and $W_{min} \leq 0$. By section 10.2 in \cite{privault}, $W_{max}$ has the same distribution as $|W_T| $ and  $W_{min}$ has the same distribution as  $-W_{max}$. 

    Let $\tilde W^{p}_t = -\int_0^t \frac{\mu^p_s}{\sigma} ds + W^{p}_t$.  Define $W^{p}_{max}: = \max_{t \in [0,T]} \tilde W^{p}_t$ and $W^{p}_{min}: = \min_{t \in [0,T]} \tilde W^{p}_t$. Note that $\tilde W^{p}_t$ is a Brownian motion under $\mathbb{P}^{\alpha}$. Since $W_0 = 0$, $W^{p}_{max} \geq 0$ and $W^{p}_{min} \leq 0$. Using the reflexivity of the Brownian motion, $W^{p}_{max}$ has the same distribution as $|\tilde W^{p}_T| $ and  $W^{p}_{min}$ has the same distribution as  $-W^{p}_{max}$. In addition, $|\int_0^t K^p P^p_s dN^p_s| \leq K^pN^p_t(|P_0^*| + t \mu_{\infty}+\sigma W^{p}_{max} - \sigma W^{p}_{min})$.  

