\begin{remark}[Similarities and differences with variance reduction techniques] Another potential application of solving a $\psi$-FBSDE rather than the original FBSDE is to reduce variance, for instance in derivative pricing. If $Y$ is a deeply out-of-the-money financial derivative written on the asset $X$, standard Monte Carlo methods often yield high variance because only a small fraction of samples have a positive payoff. By adjusting the dynamics of $X$ via a suitable $\psi$, one can ensure that more paths end up in the money, thereby reducing variance in the estimated price (see \cite{andersson2025multi} for an example).

