\end{equation}
with $b(t,x,q,p)=x+p$ and $f(t,x,q,p)=x^2-\frac{1}{2}p^2$. The associated FBSDE is given by
\begin{equation}\label{FBSDE_example}\begin{dcases}
  X_t
  =
  x_0-\int_0^t (X_s + Z_s)\emph{d} s + \int_0^t\emph{d} W_s,\\
  Y_t
  =
  X_T^2 
  + 
  \int_t^T \Big(X_s^2 -\frac{1}{2}Z_s^2\Big) 
  \emph{d} s -\int_t^T Z_s\emph{d} W_s,\quad t\in[0,T].
    \end{dcases}
\end{equation}
By choosing $\psi(t,x,y,z)=-z$ we obtain the decoupled FBSDE
\begin{equation}\label{FBSDE_example_2}\begin{dcases}
  X_t^\psi
  =
  x_0-\int_0^t X_s^\psi\emph{d} s + \int_0^t\emph{d} W_s,\\
  Y_t^\psi
  =
  (X_T^\psi)^2 
  + 
  \int_t^T \Big((X_s^\psi)^2 +\frac{1}{2}(Z_s^\psi)^2\Big) 
  \emph{d} s -\int_t^T Z_s^\psi\emph{d} W_s,\quad t\in[0,T].
    \end{dcases}
\end{equation}
Note that this example gives merely one of infintely many ways to rewrite a coupled FBSDE into a decoupled BSDE. For instance, eliminating the drift entirely would simplify the forward SDE to a Brownian motion centered at $x_0$. In such a scenario, the term $Z^\psi X^\psi$ would be included in the BSDE's driver, along with $\frac{1}{2}(Z^\psi)^2$, as previously described.
In subsequent sections, we discuss different options for $\psi$.
\end{example}


\begin{example}[A case where the deep BSDE method fails to converge]
    Herein, we present a typical example illustrating the failure of the deep FBSDE method to converge. We consider a coupled FBSDE with a driver that is quadratic in $z$ and a terminal condition that is quadratic in $x$, see \eqref{FBSDE_LQG}. In Figure~\ref{YEY}, we compare the approximation of $Y$ with its almost analytical counterpart (see \eqref{eq:YZ}), both for one representative realization and for the sample mean. The exact initial condition for the BSDE is $Y_0=8.99$, whereas the deep BSDE method yields the approximate initial value$y_0^*=28.44$. We note that although the initial condition is significantly incorrect, the terminal condition is closely approached (i.e., $g(X_N^{h,y_0,\zeta})-Y_N^{h,y_0,\zeta}$ is small both on a pathwise level and in the mean-squared sense). It is worth noting that the numerical algorithm has converged, as indicated by the loss function no longer decreasing and $y_0$ remaining constant. Moreover, the algorithm is stable, with repeated runs yielding similar values for the loss function and $y_0$. 

\end{example}
