\subsection{Experimental Setup}

For each patient, both DQN and PPO agents were trained using the patient’s original anatomy from the pCT and two augmented anatomical variations. The state representation included the normalized DVH curves for all structures \big(dim=$(15, 100)$\big), while the discrete action space consisted of $22$ priority adjustments for CTVs and OARs. Each agent was trained for $100$ episodes, with each episode consisting of up to $15$ priority adjustment steps or terminating early if the maximum plan quality score of $150$ was achieved. Algorithm~\ref{alg:drl_impt_replan} provides a detailed overview of this DRL-based replanning process, illustrating the integration of priority tuning and plan optimization within our experimental framework.

The trained agents were then tested on the patient’s new anatomy captured in the rpCT. Specifically, starting from an initial default priority set $\Vec{\omega}_0$, the agents performed priority tuning within $15$ tuning steps, where each step involved selecting a tuning action based on the plan quality of the intermediate plan and was followed by inverse plan optimization. For comparison, a new treatment plan was also manually generated for each case on the new anatomy by a human planner through manual priority tuning. The performance of the agents was assessed by comparing the plan quality scores and dosimetric metrics of the agent-generated plans with those of the manually created plans. 

To facilitate the interaction between the RL agents and the treatment planning process, we developed a custom OpenAI Gymnasium-compatible environment that simulates the treatment planning workflow. At each timestep, the environment processes the current beamlet weights $\Vec{x}$ along with the dose influence matrices $\Vec{D}_{\text{CTV}m}$ and $\Vec{D}_{\text{OAR}_k}$ for each CTV $m$ and OAR $k$. It then solves the DVH-based optimization problem using the priority set $\Vec{\omega}$, modified by the agent-selected action $a_t$, updates $\Vec{x}$, and generates a new dose distribution $\Vec{d}$, which is subsequently used to compute the plan score and reward via Eqn.~\ref{eq:dij_mat}. Each action $a \in \{0,1,\dots,21\}$ updates priority weights $\Vec{\omega}$ according to:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:priority_update_rule}
    \Vec{\omega}_{(t+1)} = \big[ \Vec{\omega}_{(t)} + \Delta_a \big]_{0}^{1}, \quad t\in[0,14],
\end{align}
where $[x]_0^1 \triangleq \min(\max(x, 0), 1)$ ensures weights remain within valid bounds. All experiments were conducted on a workstation equipped with an NVIDIA RTX $5000$ Ada Generation GPU with $32$~\si{GB} of memory.

\subsection{Plan Quality Comparisons}

The same $150$-point scoring system used to calculate rewards during DRL training was employed to quantify the plan quality of treatment plans generated for each patient’s new anatomy captured in the rpCT. The resulting plan quality scores are presented in Table~\ref{tab:scores}. The score for each dosimetric metric of the generated plans are summarized in Appendix~\ref{appndx:complete_results}. As shown, both DQN and PPO agents effectively adjusted the priorities of planning objectives, significantly improving the plan quality compared to the initial plans generated using the default priority set. Notably, for every case, both agents achieved plans that were comparable to or better than those manually created by a human planner. PPO-generated plans consistently achieved the highest quality scores across all cases (mean: $142.74 \pm 5.16$), outperforming both manual plans ($137.20 \pm 5.58$, $p < 0.01$ via paired \textit{t}-test) and DQN-generated plans ($139.78 \pm 6.84$). Furthermore, the PPO-generated plans exhibited lower score variability ($\sigma = 5.16$, vs. $6.84$ for manual plans and $6.84$ for DQN-generated plans), indicating enhanced consistency and reliability in adapting to anatomical changes.

