\begin{abstract}

This paper contains the strongest and at the same time most calculable knot invariant ever.


Let $\Theta$ be the topological moduli space of all ordered oriented tangles in 3-space. We construct a non-trivial combinatorial 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$ for $\Theta$ that takes its values in $H_0(\Theta;\mathbb{Z})$. The 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$ has a very nice property, called the {\em scan-property}: if we slide a tangle $T$ over or under a given crossing $c$ of a fixed tangle $T'$, then the value of $\mathbb{L}$ on this arc $scan(T)$ in $\Theta$ is already an isotopy invariant of $T$.

In particular, let $D$ be a framed long knot diagram. We take the product with a fixed long knot diagram $K$ and we consider the 2-cable, with a fixed crossing $c$ in $2K$.  $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))$ gives an element in $H_0(\Theta)$. To this element we associate the {\em set of Alexander vectors}, consisting of the corresponding  integer multiples of the one-variable Alexander polynomials of (the standard closures) of all sub-tangles of each of the tangles. We can vary the knots $(K,c)$ and moreover we can iterate our construction by starting now again the $scan$ with the tangles in $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))$ and so on. The result is the infinite {\em Alexander tree}, which is an isotopy invariant of the knot represented by $D$. 

{\em As an example we show with just one edge of the Alexander tree that the knot $8_{17}$ is not invertible!} This makes the Alexander tree a very promising candidate for a complete and "locally" calculable knot invariant, because the tangles in $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))$ can be drawn with linear complexity and their Alexander polynomials can be calculated with quartic complexity with respect to the number of crossings of $D$.




\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents


\section{Introduction and method}

The reader who is only interested in the formulation of the main result of this paper, could consult directly  Section 4. The Sections 1, 2 and 3 contain the proof that $\mathbb{L}$ is a 1-cocycle. Section 5 illustrates the 1-cocycle (which is very useful to understand how it works in practice) and Sections 6 and 7 contain the first applications.
\vspace{0,6 cm}

Linking numbers where invented by Gauss in 1833. They are contained in the very first knot polynomial, discovered by Alexander in 1923. Since 1984 many new knot invariants were discovered, in particular the well known quantum knot invariants, see e.g. \cite{K}. These invariants are  more  sophisticated and stronger than the Alexander polynomial. But they are much more difficult to calculate, see \cite{BV} for the most calculable known quantum knot invariants.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

{\em In this paper we propose a completely different approach. We stay just with the Alexander polynomial (often written in the form of the Conway polynomial) and sometimes even only with one of the coefficients of the Conway polynomial, namely the linking number or the Vassiliev invariant $v_2$ of order 2. But starting from a knot diagram, we move in a controlled way in the set of all isotopy classes of ordered tangles. This gives an ordered set of Alexander polynomials as an isotopy invariant of our knot. In an example we show that the knot $8_{17}$ is not invertible by using just linking numbers and the fact that $v_2 \not=0$ for the knot $6_2$ (without making any use of group theory or coverings).}
\vspace{0,4 cm}


It is very useful, but not really necessary, to replace knots by long knots. Here we consider the projection $pr: \mathbb{R}^3  \to \mathbb{R}^2$ given by $(x,y,z) \to (x,y)$, where the long knots coincides with the $x$-axes outside a compact set. Smoothing an ordinary crossing $q$ of a diagram $D$ of a long knot with respect to the orientation splits $D$ into two oriented connected components. One is a long knot and the other is a compact knot. We call $D^+_q$ the component which goes from the over-cross to the under-cross at $q$ and by $D^-_q$ the remaining component and we call $q$ a $1-crossing$ if $D^+_q$ is the long knot, otherwise $q$ is called a $0-crossing$.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

The main idea (which we have followed now for a very long time) is to consider 1-parameter families of long knot diagrams instead of just a single diagram, i.e. arcs and loops in the moduli space of long knots and more generally tangles with the fixed projection into the plane. Such moduli spaces were extensively studied in  \cite {Bu},  \cite {Bu2}, \cite {Gr}, \cite {H}. Vassiliev has introduced the finite type cohomology classes of the moduli space of long knots \cite {V1}, \cite {V2}. Combinatorial formulas for some classes in dimension 1 were obtained in  \cite {T}, \cite {S}, \cite {KK}, \cite {Mor1}, \cite {Mor2}, \cite {Mor3}. A thorough study from quantum topology of the tetrahedron equation  is contained in \cite {KaKo}.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

In \cite {F2} and \cite {F3} we have developed a machinery in order to construct
combinatorial 1-cocycles for $\Theta$. We do not assume that the reader is familiar with this, but in order to keep this paper relatively short and comprehensible we concentrate here on the main aspects. That means that we will skip many details and we do not always formalize our definitions and proofs. For the same reason we will also mention only very briefly a refinement of our main construction.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

In a generic arc in $\Theta$ we see a finite number of ordinary signed Reidemeister moves. A combinatorial 1-cochain is a discrete integration over these moves. The combinatorial 1-cocycle  $\mathbb{L}$ associates exactly to each R III-move and to those R II-move, whose branches have an identical tangent direction, a couple of signed tangles, obtained from {\em partial smoothings} of the triple crossing and correspondingly of the auto-tangency. A {\em partial smoothing } of a triple crossing or an auto-tangency is its local replacement by a given combination of tangles with lower numbers of crossings, which is glued to the rest of the tangle.
We take then the formal sum with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}$ of the  global tangles. {\em The corresponding combinatorial 1-cochain is a 1-cocycle if and only if it satisfies the positive tetrahedron equation, the cube equations and the wandering cusps equations.} All other conditions for a combinatorial 1-cochain to be a 1-cocycle are automatically satisfied. The interested reader should consult  the Chapters 2 and 4 in \cite{F2} and Chapter 6 in \cite{F3} for a proof.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

To each Reidemeister move of type III corresponds a diagram with a {\em triple 
crossing} $p$: three branches of the knot (the highest, middle and lowest with respect to the projection $pr$. A small perturbation of the triple crossing leads to an ordinary diagram with three crossings near $pr(p)$.
 We name the corresponding branches of the knot the lowest, middle and highest branch. We call the crossing between the highest and the lowest branch the {\em distinguished crossing} $d$. The two new crossings arising from a Reidemeister II move are also called distinguished and denoted by $d$ too.

We had introduced the {\em trace graph} $TG(D_t)$ of an isotopy $D_t$, $t \in [0,1]$, as an immersed graph in the plan $(x,t)$,  see  \cite {F2}. We follow a crossing $c_t$ in the isotopy and we orient the corresponding arc in $(x,t)$ in such a way that the local mapping degree for the projection into the $t$-axes is +1 if the crossing $c_t$ is positive and is -1 if it is negative. We glue the arcs together in Reidemeister II moves but we do not care about the Reidemeister III moves, i.e. either we consider the three crossings in the move as virtual or we consider a resolution by separating the branches of the triple point. The connected components of the resulting arcs are called {\em cobordism components of crossings} and are denoted by $c_t$. We will use cobordism components of crossings only locally, because globally Morse modifications of index 1 of the cobordism components are very difficult to control, compare Chapter 2 in \cite {F2}.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

The meridian of the stratum of codimension 2 of a diagram with a quadruple crossing in the discriminant (of non-generic projections) in the moduli space  consists of a sequence of eight R III moves. They come pairwise, as the remaining branch may be positioned on the left or the right of the triple crossing. The tetrahedron equation requires that the contributions of these eight moves cancel out together. The tetrahedron equation is called {\em positive} if all involved six ordinary crossings are positive. Hence, each of the  R III-moves corresponds locally to a braid relation $\sigma_1 \sigma_2 \sigma_1=\sigma_2 \sigma_1 \sigma_2$ in the braid group $B_3$. Moreover, we can attach a sign to the R III-move by declaring that the move is positive if it goes from the expression on the left side to the one on the right side. 



We name the four local branches in the quadruple crossing from the lowest to the highest (with respect to the $z$-coordinate for the projection into the plane) by 1 up to 4, and we name the unique crossing between two branches by the corresponding couple of numbers as well as the triple crossings by the corresponding triple of numbers.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

{\em The heart of the matter is the simple fact, that the partial smoothing locally given by $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ for each of the eight triple crossings in the meridian gives a solution in the braid group $B_4$ of the positive tetrahedron equation. Moreover, only the contributions of the four triple crossings with the same distinguished crossing $d=14$ do not cancel out in their respective pairs.}

In other words, instead of looking for complicated solutions of the Yang-Baxter equation, we use a simple but very strong solution of the tetrahedron equation, in fact a tangle-valued solution. It would be interesting to find out, if there is also some mathematical physics behind this solution.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

Different local types of triple crossings come together in auto-tangencies with in addition a transverse branch, see Section 3. These strata of the discriminant are called $\Sigma^{(2)}_{trans-self}$, see \cite{F2}. We make a graph $\Gamma$ in the following way: the vertices correspond to the different local types of triple crossings. We connect two vertices by an edge if and only if the corresponding strata of triple crossings come together in auto-tangencies with in addition a transverse branch. It turns out that the resulting graph is the 1-skeleton of the 3-dimensional cube $I^3$.
In particular, it is connected. The solution of the positive tetrahedron equation tells us what is the contribution to the 1-cocycle of a positive triple crossing (i.e. all three involved crossings are positive). The meridians of the strata of auto-tangencies with in addition a transverse branch give equations which allow us to determine the contributions of all other types of triple crossings. However, a global phenomenon occurs: each loop in $\Gamma$ could give an additional equation. Evidently, it suffices to consider the loops which are the boundaries of the 2-faces from $skl_2(I^3)$. We call all the equations which come from the meridians of $\Sigma^{(2)}_{trans-self}$ and from the loops in $\Gamma = skl_1(I^3)$ the {\em cube equations}. (Notice that a loop in $\Gamma$ is more general than a loop in the moduli space. For a loop in $\Gamma$ we come back to the same local type of a triple crossing but not necessarily to the same diagram of the tangle.)

It turns out that the cube equations have a unique solution, but auto-tangencies with identical tangent direction have to contribute too. It is then very easily checked that the wandering cusps equations are satisfied, i.e. the 1-cocycle vanishes on the meridian of a cusp with in addition a transverse branch. The result is finally our 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

The scan property of $\mathbb{L}$ comes from the fact that if we push a tangle diagram $T$ over or under a fixed crossing $c$ in another fixed tangle diagram $T'$ then all the distinguished crossings $d$ of contributing moves are on the same cobordism component of crossings and this is just the cobordism component of the crossing $c$. The contributions of moves in an isotopy of $T$ before the pushing cancel out with the inverse moves after the pushing, because there is only one branch of $T'$ all over or all under the tangle $T$.

Moreover, the 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$ splits into two 1-cocycles: one where all distinguished crossings are $1-crossings$ and one where all distinguished crossings are $0-crossings$.


In all our examples for 2-cables, i.e. we add the longitude to the framed long knot, $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D)) \not=0$ already with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}/ 2\mathbb{Z}$.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

We show that $\mathbb{L}$ represents a non-trivial cohomology class in the topological moduli space of 2-cables $\Theta_2$ of long framed knots, by showing that it is non-trivial on the following loop: we consider the 2-cable of the  product $3_1^+* 4_1$ and we push the 2-cable of the knot $3_1^+$ through the 2-cable of the knot $4_1$, followed by pushing the 2-cable of $4_1$ through the 2-cable of $3_1^+$. We call this loop a {\em bracket cycle} and denote it by $[3_1^+,4_1]_2$. In the space of long knots $\Theta_1$, the corresponding loop $[3_1^+,4_1]$ is the Lie bracket in dimension 0 in the Gerstenhaber algebra $H_*(\Theta_1,\mathbb{Q})$, see \cite{B} and also \cite{T}. $\mathbb{L}$ is the first combinatorial 1-cocycle which is non-trivial on a loop like $[3_1^+,4_1]_2$ for knots with framing 0.


The proof that  $\mathbb{L}$ is non-trivial on this loop is rather simple and can be done by hand, because we can do the calculations first without taking the 2-cable and we can restrict ourself only to $1-crossings$ or only to $0-crossings$. For example, there are diagrams of $3_1^+$ and $4_1$ with just a single $1-crossing$ for each of them. The tangles in the value of $\mathbb{L}$ without cabling come in pairs which cancel out together. {\em However, if we add the longitude of the knot then some of these pairs do no longer cancel out and we can detect this sometimes already with linking numbers!} 
\vspace{0,4 cm}

The following remark is of philosophical nature and has no mathematical contents.

\begin{remark}

We know from Waldhausen that the peripheral system is a complete knot invariant. It is amazing to compare this with our approach. 

We have a base point (the point at infinity on the knot) because we consider long knots. We use it by taking a meridian of the long knot close to its base point. Then the $1-crossings$ and $0-crossings$ $q$ are just determined by the linking number of $D^+_q$ with the meridian. Consequently, the splitting of $\mathbb{L}$ for the distinguished $1-crossings$ and the distinguished $0-crossings$ uses the meridian. We make heavy use of the longitude, because we have always to replace the knot by its 2-cable to get something interesting. We have no longer the knot group, but we have now a bunch of ordered oriented tangles. In the knot group it can be difficult to show that two words do not represent the same element in the group. But tangles can often be simply distinguished by Alexander polynomials. Instead of a Cayley graph we have now the Alexander tree. It could well be that starting from a diagram $D$ we find the same two tangles on different branches of the tree. This gives us  identical sub-trees with the roots in these two tangles. It is natural to identify these sub-trees and we would get the {\em Alexander graph}. This seems to be an interesting object, but it has to be studied with a computer program.

\end{remark}


The 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$ can be evidently generalized for links.


Finally, let us mention that our approach does not work at all in {\em virtual} knot theory, because there are forbidden moves in all interesting 1-parameter families of virtual knot diagrams. 

However, it could perhaps work for {\em welded knots}, because we can still define an arc $scan$.
\vspace{0,4 cm}



\section{The solution in $B_4$ of the positive tetrahedron equation}

We show the adjacent strata of positive triple crossings together with their co-orientations (to the positive side) for a positive quadruple crossing in Fig.~\ref{quadcros}. Its unfolding is shown in Fig.~\ref{unfoldquad}, compare Chapter 2 in \cite{F2} or Chapter 3 in \cite{F3}.


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{quadcros.pdf}
\caption{\label{quadcros} The names of the strata of triple crossings in the intersection of a normal 2-disc of a positive quadruple crossing}  
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{unfoldquad.pdf}
\caption{\label{unfoldquad} Meridian in the unfolding of a positive quadruple crossing}  
\end{figure}

\begin{lemma}
Replacing each of the eight triple crossings by the partial smoothing locally given by $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ gives a solution with integer coefficients in the braid group $B_4$ of the positive tetrahedron equation. 

\end{lemma}

{\em Proof.}


First of all we observe that no matter the partial smoothing the stratum $P_2$ cancels out with $\bar P_2$ and $P_3$ cancels out with $\bar P_3$ , because just the branch $1$ goes all under it or the branch $4$ goes all over it with an isotopy and the moves $P_i$ and $\bar P_i$ have always different signs.

We write now the contributions of the partial smoothings locally given by  $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ of the remaining strata as signed  words in $B_4$ and we apply the braid relations.

$-P_1$: $-\sigma_1\sigma_2^2\sigma_1\sigma_2+(\sigma_1\sigma_3^2\sigma_1\sigma_2=\sigma_1^2\sigma_3^2\sigma_2)$

$+\bar P_1$:$(\sigma_2\sigma_3\sigma_1^2\sigma_3=\sigma_2\sigma_1^2\sigma_3^2)-(\sigma_2\sigma_3\sigma_2^2\sigma_3=\sigma_3\sigma_2\sigma_3\sigma_2\sigma_3=\sigma_3\sigma_2^2\sigma_3\sigma_2)$

$+P_4$:$(\sigma_2\sigma_1\sigma_2^2\sigma_1=\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1=\sigma_1\sigma_2^2\sigma_1\sigma_2)-(\sigma_2\sigma_1\sigma_3^2\sigma_1=\sigma_2\sigma_1^2\sigma_3^2)$

$-\bar P_4$: $-(\sigma_3\sigma_1^2\sigma_3\sigma_2=\sigma_1^2\sigma_3^2\sigma_2)+\sigma_3\sigma_2^2\sigma_3\sigma_2$

\vspace{0,4 cm}

As we see, the contributions cancel out two by two but they mix all four strata. The crossing $14$ is the distinguished crossing $d$ for the four strata.

$\Box$

The following remark is of historical nature and explains how we have found our 1-cocycle.

\begin{remark}

Chapter 6 of \cite{F3} has existed as a preprint already in 2013. In particular it contains the proof that the 2-variable Kauffman polynomial evaluated on the partial smoothing 
$\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ is a solution of the positive tetrahedron equation. In much earlier work we had proven that the HOMFLYPT polynomial evaluated on the partial smoothing $\sigma_1-\sigma_2$ is a solution of the positive tetrahedron equation and that it has the $scan-property$. It leads to a polynomial valued 1-cocycle and Dror Bar-Natan was so kind to program it even for the 2-cable of long knots, see \cite{BN}. Unfortunately, we have applied it just in the case where $T'$ is the 2-cable of a trivial curl. So, it is not surprising that the value of the 1-cocycle was just the HOMFLYPT polynomial of the 2-cable of the knot. Probably, it would have given already something interesting if we would have replaced $T'$ by the 2-cable of a non-trivial knot with a fixed crossing in it.

It took me then a very long time to make the following trivial observation: if the partial smoothing $\sigma_1-\sigma_2$ gives a solution  of the positive tetrahedron equation for the HOMFLYPT polynomial then the partial smoothing $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ does as well. Indeed, one of the skein relations for the HOMFLYPT polynomial as an invariant under regular isotopy (i.e. the projection into the plan is always an immersion) implies that for each $i$ holds

$P_{\sigma_i^2}=P_{id}+zP_{\sigma_i}$.

Consequently, $P_{\sigma_1^2}-P_{\sigma_2^2}=(P_{id}+zP_{\sigma_1})-(P_{id}+zP_{\sigma_2})=z(P_{\sigma_1}-P_{\sigma_2})$.
\vspace{0,4 cm}

This means that the {\em same} partial smoothing $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ leads to a solution of the positive tetrahedron equation for the HOMFLYPT polynomial (related to $SU(n)$ representations) and as well for the Kauffman polynomial (related to $SO(n)$ representations). How is this possible? The answer is evident: it should be true already on the level of tangles! The rest was routine.


\end{remark}

\section{The solution of the cube equations and of the wandering cusps equations}


We show the eight local types of triple crossings in Fig.~\ref{loctricross}. The {\em signs} correspond to the side with respect to the corresponding stratum of the discriminant and they are uniquely determined from the edges in the cube by the sign for the local type 1 (which we have used in the positive tetrahedron equation). The sign of the move is $+1$ if it goes from the negative side to the positive side and $-1$ otherwise.  The sign of a R II-move is defined to be $+1$ if the move increases the number of crossings and $-1$ otherwise. 


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{loctricross.pdf}
\caption{\label{loctricross} Local types of a triple crossing}  
\end{figure}

The cube is shown in Fig.~\ref{gamma}.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{gamma.pdf}
\caption{\label{gamma} The cube $\Gamma$}  
\end{figure}

There are two basic local types of triple crossings for oriented tangles: we call them {\em braid-like} (the only sort which occurs for braids) and {\em star-like} (which never occure for braids),  compare  Fig.~\ref{loctricross}. It is well known, that star-like triple crossings can be avoided in each  1-parameter family of diagrams (i.e. arcs and loops in $\Theta$) by a homotopy relative to the endpoints. This follows essentially from the cube equations in \cite {F2}, but see also \cite {P}.

But we have to be careful. We have shown in particular in \cite{F4} that this is no longer the case for 2-parameter families:  there is a contractible loop already in the space of long knots $\Theta_1$, which does not contain star-like triple crossings, but each generic contracting 2-disc for this loop in $\Theta_1$ contains necessarily star-like triple crossings.
Hence,  in practice in calculations we can avoid star-like triple crossings. But in the proof we have to consider them too in order to show that the 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$ is well defined.
\vspace{0,4cm}

The 2-sphere, which is the boundary of the cube, has an additional structure. The six edges which connect the braid-like triple crossings, i.e. the local types $1-7-4-8-3-5$, form an {\em equator} of the 2-sphere. The star-like types 2 and 6 form the {\em poles} of the 2-sphere and there are six edges which connect the poles with the equator. 
\vspace{0,4cm}

We move now from the positive triple crossing (local type 1) along the edges of the equator. First of all we observe that R II-moves can only contribute non-trivially if the transverse branch moves between the two branches of the R II-move. This happens only for the edges $7-4$ and $5-3$, because R II-moves with different tangent directions do not contribute to $\mathbb{L}$. Notice that in this case the two new crossings from an auto-tangency  are exactly the distinguished crossings $d$ from the R III-moves. Hence all four distinguished crossings in the moves are on the same cobordism component of crossings.


We can write the diagram with two new crossings from a R II-move with identical tangent direction locally as a 2-braid $\sigma_1\sigma_1^{-1}$ or $\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_1$.

{\em The partial smoothing of a R II-move with identical tangent direction is then defined by $\sigma_1-\sigma_1^{-1}$.}
\vspace{0,4cm}



The meridian of the edge $1-7$ is shown in  Fig.~\ref{1-7}. The partial smoothing of the type 1 determines now the partial smoothing of the type 7. We can write them as 3-braids. The signs of the two R III-moves are always opposite by definition. The partial smoothings are in the brackets.

