The following remark contains an important refinement of our 1-cocycle.

\begin{remark}
Let $\Theta^{reg}$ be the topological moduli space of all regular tangles, i.e. the projection into the plane is always an immersion. As well known, $\pi_0(\Theta^{reg})$ is determined by $\pi_0(\Theta)$ together with the writhe and the Whitney index of each component of the tangle. We consider now all diagrams up to regular isotopy, i.e. R I-moves do not longer occur.

We add to the definition of $\mathbb{L}$ the signed contributions of R II-moves with opposite tangent direction. It is given by the difference of the tangles obtained from smoothing first the new negative crossing with respect to the orientation and then the same for the new positive crossing. Evidently, in $H_0(\Theta)$ these tangles cancel out together, but not in $H_0(\Theta^{reg})$, because the writhe change and moreover the small curl is attached to different components of the tangle.

The tetrahedron equation is exactly the same as before and it turns out that the refined 
$\mathbb{L}^{reg}$ satisfies the cube equations for regular isotopies. We left the verification to the reader. There is no wandering cusps equation. Hence it is a 1-cocycle too.

It  could be that $\mathbb{L}^{reg}$ is even stronger than $\mathbb{L}$, because there are less cancellations if we add add to the Alexander polynomials the information about the writhe and the Whitney index of the components of the tangles.

\end{remark}
