\section{The 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$, its scan-property and the Alexander tree}


The local types of triple crossings together with the coorientation of the stratum of the discriminant are given in Fig.~\ref{loctricross}. The sign of a R III-move is $+1$ if it goes from the negative to the positive side, otherwise it is $-1$. A R II-move has the sign $+1$ if it increases the number of crossings, otherwise it is $-1$.

In an generic 1-parameter family of diagrams we replace each triple crossing $p$ and each auto-tangency $p$ with identical tangent direction by its local partial smoothing, i.e. a linear combination of tangles with less crossings, which we glue to the rest of the tangle. We denote the resulting combination of tangles by $III(p)$ and correspondingly $II(p)$.

\begin{definition}

The {\em partial smoothings} are defined in the following way.

type 1 $=\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1$:  $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$

type 3 $=\sigma_2^{-1}\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_2$: $\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_2-\sigma_1\sigma_2^{-1}$

type 4 $=\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_2^{-1}\sigma_1$: $\sigma_2\sigma_1^{-1}-\sigma_2^{-1}\sigma_1$

type 5 $=\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_2\sigma_1$: $\sigma_1\sigma_2^{-1}-\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_2$

type 7 $=\sigma_2^{-1}\sigma_1\sigma_2$: $\sigma_2^{-1}\sigma_1-\sigma_2\sigma_1^{-1}$

type 8 $=\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_2^{-1}\sigma_1^{-1}$: $\sigma_1^{-2}-\sigma_2^{-2}$

star-like type 2 ($w(d)=+1$): the boxes in Fig.~\ref{eq7-2}

star-like type 6 ($w(d)=-1$): the boxes in Fig.~\ref{eq3-6}
\vspace{0,4cm}

Auto-tangency with identical tangent direction:

$\sigma_1\sigma_1^{-1}=\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_1$: $\sigma_1-\sigma_1^{-1}$.

\end{definition}


\begin{definition}
Let  $s \subset \Theta$ be a generic arc. The {\em combinatorial 1-cochain} $\mathbb{L}$ with values in the free $\mathbb{Z}$-module $\mathbb{Z} \{\pi_0(\Theta)\}$ is defined by 
\vspace{0,4cm}

$\mathbb{L}(s)= \sum_{p \in s} sign(p)\{III(p)\}+\sum_{p \in s} sign(p)\{II(p)\}$
\vspace{0,4cm}

where $p$ in $s$ are all triple crossings, respectively auto-tangecies with identical tangent direction,  and $\{III(p)\}$, respectively $\{II(p)\}$, denotes the combination of isotopy classes of the tangles in $III(p)$, respectively in $II(p)$, i.e. elements in $\pi_0(\Theta)$.

\end{definition}


The following is the first part of our main result.

\begin{theorem}
The combinatorial 1-cochain $\mathbb{L}$ is a 1-cocycle which represents a non-trivial class in $H^1(\Theta;H_0(\Theta))$.

\end{theorem}

The proof that $\mathbb{L}$ is a 1-cocycle is contained in the Sections 1,2 and 3 and the proof that it represents a non-trivial cohomology class is contained in Section 7.
\vspace{0,6cm}


$\mathbb{L}$ has a nice property, which is extremely useful. For easier understanding we will formulate it only in the very special case of our later examples.

Let $D$ be a long knot diagram, which we want to study, and let $K$ be a chosen  auxiliary long knot diagram. We consider their product, which we call the {\em green component} and we take its 2-cable with respect to the blackboard framing and call the new component the {\em red longitude}. We chose a {\em green crossing} $c$ (i.e. over- and under-strand are both green) in the 2-cable $2K$ and we consider the arc in $\Theta$ which consists of pushing monotonously the whole 2-cable $2D$ just over or under the green branch which contains the crossing $c$, see Fig.~\ref{scanc}. This arc is called the {\em scan of $2D$ with respect to $c$} and denoted by $scan(2D)$. If we forget the red longitude, then we have simply the scan of $D$ with respect to $c$. Notice, that instead of $2D$ we could have taken a more general tangle $T$, but the auxiliary tangle $T'$ has to be the 2-cable of a long knot, otherwise we could get blocked.


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{scanc.pdf}
\caption{\label{scanc} $scan(T)$ with respect to the crossing $c$}  
\end{figure}


We apply now $\mathbb{L}$ to $scan(2D)$, but only over those RIII-moves and R II-moves where the distinguished crossing is a green crossing. Each time when we apply a partial smoothing, the green component splits into a long knot, for which we keep the green colour, and a compact knot, which we call the {\em black component}.

\begin{definition}

$\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$ is the part of $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))$ where we sum up only those contributions of $p$, where the distinguished crossing $d$ is a green crossing.

\end{definition}

Notice, that we don't need to keep fix the whole auxiliary diagram $2K$. Indeed, we can allow all regular isotopies of $K$ (because the framing has to be preserved) which preserve the crossing $c$ and which do not move a branch of $K$ between the over-branch and the under-branch of $c$. The couple $(K,c)$ up to this equivalence relation is called our {\em scan parameter}.

The following is the second part of our main result. (See Section 1 for the definition of the cobordism components of crossings.)

\begin{theorem}

$ \mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green} \in H_0(\Theta)$ is an isotopy invariant of $2D$ and hence of the framed long knot represented by $D$. Moreover, each green distinguished crossing $d$ in $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$ belongs to the cobordism component of the crossing $c$.

\end{theorem}


\vspace{0,4cm}

{\em Proof.}


All the green crossings belong to the component of the green knot. Hence, we have only to show that for a $scan$ of a single knot all the distinguished crossings belong to the same cobordism component. We give an example in Fig.~\ref{cobor}. The general case is completely analogous and is left to the reader.
\vspace{0,2cm}


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{cobor.pdf}
\caption{\label{cobor} All distinguished crossings are on the cobordism component $c=c_1$}   
\end{figure}
To be more specific in the theorem, let us consider the case where the tangle $2D$ is pushed under the over-cross of $c$. The other case is completely analogous. Let $D_s, s \in{0,1}$, be an isotopy from $2D$ to $2D'$. After the $scan$ we bring $2D'$ to $2D$ by the inverse isotopy $D_{-s}$. But we can perform the isotopies $s$ and $scan$ at the same time. The arc $scan$ will pass through quadruple crossings, auto-tangencies with a transverse branch and cusps with a transverse branch. We have to check that $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D_s))_{d-green}$ does not depend on $s$. For auto-tangencies and cusps with a transverse branch this is just a consequence of the cube and wandering cusps equations. In a quadruple crossing the highest branch 4 is necessarily the over-cross of $c$. Consequently, the R III-move in $D_s$ corresponds to the stratum $P_3$ and cancels out with the stratum $\bar P_3$. If the lowest branch 1 is green, then there will be contributions to $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D_s))_{d-green}$ but all with the same green distinguished crossing $d=14=c$. It follows now from the tetrahedron equation that $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D_s))_{d-green}$ does not depend on $s$.

$\Box$
\vspace{0,4cm}

$\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$ has a very useful property in order to simplify calculations.


 {\em A contribution of a R III-move where all three branches are green could cancel out with a contribution of a R III-move where the middle branch is the red longitude, only if the R III-move is of local type 1 or 2 if $w(c)=+1$, and 6 or 8 if $w(c)=-1$.}

Indeed, the crucial point is that the red longitude stays always unchanged if all three branches are green. Only its linking with the red and the black component can change for different moves. But if the middle branch is the red longitude, then in the partial smoothing it can stay a red longitude up to isotopy only for the local types 1, 2, 6 and 8 (compare Definition 1).  In all other cases  the partial smoothings induce a non-trivial permutation of the endpoints and the standard closure of the tangle becomes a knot instead of a 3-component link. This knot is also an invariant but we will consider in the examples only the 3-component links.
\vspace{0,4cm}

The distinguished crossing could be a $0-crossing $ or a $1-crossing$, see Section 1. The type of the crossings does not change on the cobordism component of the crossing.  As already mentioned, we can in general not control the cobordism components of crossings in a global isotopy, as e.g. the Fox-Hatcher loop. However, there can be Morse modifications of index 1 only between components with the same type of crossings. 

{\em Consequently, we can restrict $\mathbb{L}$ to only those $p$ where the distinguished crossing $d$ is a $0-crossing$, respectively a $1-crossing$. The resulting 1-cocycle is called $\mathbb{L}_0$, respectively $\mathbb{L}_1$. Of course, 
$\mathbb{L}=\mathbb{L}_0+\mathbb{L}_1$.}

For the $scan$ this does not give anything new ($\mathbb{L}(scan(2D_s))_{d-green}$ belongs to $\mathbb{L}_0$ or $\mathbb{L}_1$ is just determined by the type of $c$), but it will be very useful to simplify calculations for complicated loops in the moduli space $\Theta$.
\vspace{0,4cm}

We use the invariants $ \mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$ to define the {\em Alexander tree} in the natural way. Its root is the diagram $D$ and we attach to it the Alexander (or like-wise Conway) polynomial of $D$. The edges from $D$ correspond to the scan parameters $(K,c)$ and in the other vertex of the edge we have the unordered set of the integer multiples of the isotopy classes of tangles in $ \mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$. For simplicity we forget about all the R III-moves where the middle branch is the red longitude and which are of type 3, 4, 5 or 7 (i.e. the standard closure of the whole tangle is a knot). We consider only the remaining cases where each of the tangles has exactly three components: the green long knot, the red longitude and a compact knot, the black component. To each (standardly closed) tangle in $ \mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$ we associate its {\em Alexander vector}. It consists of the Alexander (or Conway) polynomials of {\em (the whole link, green-black link, green-red link, red-black link, green knot, black knot, red knot)}. To the vertex in the tree we associate now the unordered set of the Alexander vectors with their corresponding multiplicities.

We can vary the edges $(K,c)$, but for each tangle $T$ in $ \mathbb{L}(scan(2D))_{d-green}$ we can also consider now $ \mathbb{L}(scan(T))_{d-green}$ again with respect to $(K,c)$ and so on. The result will be multiples of isotopy classes of tangles, each with exactly two long knots but with increasing number of compact components (but which are ordered by the level of the tree where they first appeared). Each time we associate to a vertex now the unordered set of the multiples of the Alexander vectors (of increasing length). It follows from the Theorems 1 and 2 that
{\em the infinite Alexander tree is an isotopy invariant of $D$.}
\vspace{0,4cm}

To make further progress we need now of course a computer program, in order to see how far we have to move from $D$ in the trees for perhaps distinguishing all knots. Unfortunately,  I'm not able to write such a program. So, everybody interested in this would be very welcome to participate!


