First of all we order the contributions by the linking numbers of the long knot with the compact knot. If necessary, we consider now the two knot types, or just their $v_2$ (which can be calculated with quadratic complexity). In this example they are all trivial knots, besides for the two cases of linking number 1. In one of them the long knot is the trefoil and in the other case the compact knot is the trefoil. This shows that $\mathbb{L}(scan(3_1^+)) \not =0$, but it is not really interesting. The contributions with linking numbers 2 and 3 cancel out pairwise.

The case of linking number 3 is particularly interesting. The tangles close to an alternating link of 8 crossings. In fact, it is the link $L8a10\{1\}$ in https://knotinfo.math.indiana.edu/. Its Conway polynomial is $\nabla_L(z)=3z-z^5$. They cancel out together, but one of them comes from a R III-move and the other from a R II-move. Let us add now the red longitude. We are only interested in the contributions where the red longitude stays a red longitude. As mentioned above, we have only to consider here the R III-moves with all three branches are green and of course the R II-move with both branches are green. In Fig.~\ref{scanred} we show the R III-move together with the red longitude of the middle branch as well as the R II-move. In the figure we can forget about the red longitude of the highest branch and of the lowest branch, because they are isotopic for both moves, no matter the partial smoothings.



\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{scanred.pdf}
\caption{\label{scanred} The two moves with the partial smoothings $L8a10\{1\}$ as the green-black link and the red longitude}   
\end{figure}

In Fig.~\ref{scanred} we show also the partial smoothings which give $L8a10\{1\}$ as the green-black link for the two moves and we make a very important observation, even without any calculation:

{\em The corresponding linking numbers of the green-red link and as well of the black-red link are different for the two moves!}

Hence, we know that the Alexander vectors of the two 3-component links are different, even without actually calculating them.
\vspace{0,4cm}

The big advantage is, that we can make now conclusions about $scan(3_1^+)$ even without cabling. {\em The link $L8a10\{1\}$, and hence its Conway polynomial $\nabla_L(z)=3z-z^5$, is an invariant of the knot $3_1^+$, corresponding to the edge $(4_1,c)$.} 

For any knot diagram $D$ we consider $scan(D)$ with respect to $(4_1,c)$ and we consider the Conway polynomials  of all the links in $\mathbb{L}(scan(D))$ without applying cancellations. If $\nabla_L(z)=3z-z^5$ does not occur, then $D$ is not the positive trefoil!

Finally, let us mention the promise which makes the link $L8a10\{1\}$ to us. We start with a 3-crossing and a 4-crossing knot, but we end up with a 8-crossing link as an invariant of the 3-crossing knot. This suggests that our construction is probably indeed expanding in $\pi_0(\Theta)$ with respect to the number of crossings.



\section{A new proof that the knot $8_{17}$ is not invertible}

Trotter was the first, who has shown that non-invertible knots really exist (i.e. knots not isotopic to itself with reversed orientation), see \cite{Tr}. Hartley has shown that the knot $8_{17}$ is the smallest non-invertible knot, see \cite{Har}, by using covering invariants  of certain irregular metacyclic and metabelian covers.

It is well known that all quantum knot invariants (including Khovanov and Heegaard-Floer homologies) can not detect that a knot is not invertible.

It is not known whether or not finite type (or Vassiliev-Goussarov) invariants can detect non-invertibility for knots. However, Kuperberg has shown that if they fail to detect non-invertibility of knots then they fail to distinguish all non-oriented knots as well, see \cite{Ku}.

This indicates, that distinguishing the knot orientation with invariants is the most difficult and most important part of the knot isotopy problem for classical knots.

{\em We will reprove here in an extremal simple way that the knot $8_{17}$ is not invertible, which indicates the power of our invariants.}
\vspace{0,4cm}

To get the inverse of a long knot we have to make a rotation of the 3-space around the y-axes by $\pi$ and then change the orientation of the resulting knot. A knot is invertible, if and only if the two knots are now isotopic as long knots in the usual sense.

We consider the same $scan$ as in Section 5, but with $8_{17}$ and $-8_{17}$ instead of the trefoil. They are shown in Fig.~\ref{8174}.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{8174.pdf}
\caption{\label{8174} The $scan$ of $8_{17}$ and of $-8_{17}$ with respect to $c$ in $4_1$}   
\end{figure}

There are exactly 10 moves to consider in each of the $scans$ (we let the reader draw the pictures by himself). We restrict ourself on only those contributions of the smoothings with $lk(green, black)=2$. 

In the case of $8_{17}$ there are exactly six such contributions and one easily sees that in each of them the green component is the trivial knot. (Again, we do not have to recognize the trivial knots, it is enough to calculate that $v_2=0$ for each of them.)

In the case of $-8_{17}$ there are also exactly six contributions with $lk(green, black)=2$. The green component is the trivial knot for four of them, but for two of them it is the knot $6_2$ (again, we need only $v_2=-1\not = 0$). This happens exactly for the crossings $a$ and $b$ in Fig.~\ref{8174}. The corresponding R III-moves are both of type 4 and of negative sign. We show these contributions in Fig.~\ref{81762}.


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{81762.pdf}
\caption{\label{81762} The two contributions where the green component is the knot $6_2$}   
\end{figure}


Evidently, the links are isotopic. However, after adding the red longitude, for the triple crossing with $a$ we have $lk(red,black)=-1$ and $lk(red ,green)=1$. But  for the triple crossing with $b$ we have $lk(red, black)=lk(red,green)=0$. We show the two links in Fig.~\ref{linking}, because this is so important. Consequently, the two corresponding Alexander vectors do not cancel out together.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{linking}
\caption{\label{linking} The two links with the red longitude}   
\end{figure}

The crossing $c$ is negative. Hence it remains to check for $8_{17}$ and $-8_{17}$, that if we smooth the crossing $c$ for each negative triple crossing (i.e. of type 8) in the $scan$, where the red longitude is the middle branch, then the green component is never the knot $6_2$. In fact, we easily see that it is always the trivial knot, besides in one case for $-8_{17}$, where it is a negative trefoil. Hence, our two Alexander vectors occur just once and only for $-8_{17}$ and not for $8_{17}$. It follows from Theorem 2 that the knots are not isotopic.
\vspace{0,2cm}

Let us summarize: we have used the red longitude in order to show with $\mathbb{L}(scan(2D))$ for $D=-8_{17}$, that without applying cancellation there should be {\em always} a green long knot $6_2$ in $\mathbb{L}(scan(D))$ and hence a green long knot with the Conway polynomial $1-z^2-z^4$. There could be complicated diagrams $D'$ for $8_{17}$, which contain also pairs of green long knots with the Conway polynomial $1-z^2-z^4$ in $\mathbb{L}(scan(D'))$. However, this is not the case for the standard diagram of $8_{17}$ and hence $8_{17}$ and $-8_{17}$ are not isotopic. Notice, that we have needed in the proof from the 2-cable just two linking numbers!
\vspace{0,2cm}

Let us mention, that $lk(green, black)=3$ never occurs for $8_{17}$, but exactly twice for $-8_{17}$, namely for the other partial smoothing of the crossing $b$ and for a partial smoothing of the crossing $e$ (and the green component is for both a negative trefoil). It could well be, that after adding the red longitude, the Alexander polynomials of the two 3-component links show again that the knots are not isotopic. But we haven't checked this (because writing the DT notations of the diagrams is rather laborious).




\section{The 1-cocycle $\mathbb{L}$ is sometimes non-trivial on a bracket cycle}

In order to calculate $\mathbb{L}_1$ on the bracket cycle $[3_1^+,4_1]$, compare Section 1, we have to calculate it on four $scans$: pushing $3_1^+$ under the over-cross of $c$, pushing it over the under-cross of $c$, pushing $4_1$ under the over-cross of $\bar c$ and finally pushing $4_1$ over the under-cross of $\bar c$, compare 
Fig.~\ref{314}.

The first $scan$ is contained in Section 5, where we have seen that the link $L8a10\{1\}$ is an invariant, compare Fig.~\ref{scan31}. The second $scan$ is quit similar but with opposite signs. There are also exactly two contributions with linking number 3 and which cancel out together, one which comes from a R III-move and the other from a R II-move. We show the link in Fig.~\ref{secscan}. 


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics{secscan.pdf}
\caption{\label{secscan} The link $L8a10\{1\}$ in the $scan$ over the under-cross of $c$}   
\end{figure}

It is again $L8a10\{1\}$, but with a different diagram. We add now for the $L8a10\{1\}$ in the first $scan$  the red longitude which has the linking number 1 with the black component and we get a 3-component link $L_1$. We do the same for the second $scan$ and we get a link $L_2$. All the corresponding 2-component sub-links in $L_1$ and $L_2$ have the same linking numbers and the green-black sub-link is $L8a10\{1\}$ in both cases. I'm grateful to Stepan Orevkov for calculating the corresponding  Alexander polynomials.

It turns out that 

$\Delta_{L_1}(t)= (t-1)^2(2-t-6t^2+2t^3+2t^4-7t^5+2t^6+2t^7-6t^8-t^9+2t^{10})$

and 

$\Delta_{L_2}(t)= (t-1)^2(2-3t-2t^2-3t^5-2t^8-3t^9+2t^{10})$.

Consequently, their contributions do not cancel out in $[3_1^+,4_1]_2$ for the 2-cables.
\vspace{0,4cm}

In the remaining two $scans$ we have to push $4_1$ under and over the crossing $\bar c$. It turns out that there aren't any contributions with the linking number 3 at all. However, we have to be careful, because $w(\bar c)=+1$ and $4_1$ has positive crossings too. But the red longitude is always linked with one of the two other branches in the smoothings of the triple crossing. Hence, we have just to consider the linking numbers of the two components when we smooth the ordinary crossing $\bar c$ with respect to the orientation in the positive triple crossings which we have already considered. But this linking number is at most 2 in all cases and we are done. The above polynomials give two different Alexander vectors for $[3_1^+,4_1]_2$ (already with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}/ 2\mathbb{Z}$).

In order to make the framing trivial for the positive trefoil we add three small curls each attached with a negative 0-crossing. Evidently, this doesn't change anything for the links in $\mathbb{L}_1([3_1^+,4_1])$ and the above links $L_1$ and $L_2$ still don't cancel out.







